// Question bank for the Ground Up Hardware Trainer.
// Entry: [id, difficulty, question, answer, explanation, tags?]
//   difficulty: E (easy), M (medium), H (hard)
//   answer: [correct, distractor, distractor, distractor] for multiple choice, or true/false
//   tags: o = from a real OA, v = variant of an OA question, r = reported online,
//         b = beyond the book (the explanation teaches it), a = computer arithmetic
// The id prefix is the chapter of "Hardware from the Ground Up" that teaches the topic, except for the few
// questions listed in MOVED (after RAW), which are taught in a later chapter.
const RAW = [
// 1 Electricity
["01-01","E","A 1 kΩ resistor is connected across a 5 V supply. What current flows?",["5 mA","0.2 mA","5 A","50 mA"],"Ohm's law: I = V/R = 5 V / 1000 Ω = 5 mA."],
["01-02","M","Three 300 Ω resistors are connected in parallel. What is the total resistance?",["100 Ω","900 Ω","300 Ω","150 Ω"],"Equal resistors in parallel give R/n = 300/3 = 100 Ω. Parallel resistance is always below the smallest resistor."],
["01-03","E","Two resistors are connected in series. What do they always share?",["The same current","The same voltage","The same power","Nothing; it depends on their values"],"Series parts sit on one path, so the same current flows through each, and their voltages add."],
["01-04","M","A voltage divider has Vin = 3.3 V, R1 = 10 kΩ on top and R2 = 20 kΩ on the bottom. What is Vout across R2?",["2.2 V","1.1 V","1.65 V","3.3 V"],"Vout = Vin × R2/(R1 + R2) = 3.3 × 20/30 = 2.2 V."],
["01-05","E","A 10 Ω resistor carries 2 A. How much power does it dissipate?",["40 W","20 W","5 W","200 W"],"P = I²R = 4 × 10 = 40 W."],
["01-06","E","Voltage is always measured between two points.",true,"Voltage is a difference in energy per charge between two points. A node voltage is measured relative to ground."],
["01-07","M","Board A sends a digital signal to board B over a single wire and nothing else. Board B reads random values. What is the most likely cause?",["The boards share no ground, so B has no reference and the current has no return path","The wire is too short for the signal to settle, so B samples it before it arrives","Board B's clock is too fast, so it samples each bit several times","The signal voltage is too high, so B's input clamps and reads noise"],"The sender's 1 is defined relative to the sender's ground. Without a shared ground there is no common reference and no return path.","r"],
["01-08","E","A CMOS logic input left unconnected (floating) reliably reads as 0.",false,"A floating input has an undefined voltage that drifts with noise, so it can read as 0 or 1. Tie it to a level, or use a pull-up or pull-down resistor."],
["01-09","M","What does a pull-up resistor do on an input that a push button connects to ground?",["Holds the input at 1 when open, and limits current when pressed","Speeds up the input's switching by adding a current path to VDD","Amplifies the button signal so the input sees a full logic level","Protects the button contacts from static discharge when pressed"],"Open: no current flows, so the input sits at VDD. Pressed: the input connects to ground, and the resistor limits the current."],
["01-10","M","A wire is made twice as long and given twice the cross-sectional area. Its resistance:",["Stays the same","Doubles","Halves","Quadruples"],"R = ρL/A. Doubling L doubles R, doubling A halves it, and the effects cancel."],
["01-11","M","A 1.00 V regulator feeds a chip drawing 200 A through 0.25 mΩ of path resistance. What voltage reaches the chip?",["0.95 V","0.75 V","1.00 V","0.995 V"],"IR drop = 200 × 0.00025 = 0.05 V, so the chip sees 1.00 − 0.05 = 0.95 V."],
["01-12","E","An ammeter is connected in parallel with the component whose current it measures.",false,"An ammeter goes in series, because the current must flow through it. A low-resistance ammeter placed in parallel creates a short."],
["01-13","E","What is the period of a 2 GHz clock?",["0.5 ns","2 ns","5 ns","0.2 ns"],"T = 1/f = 1/(2 × 10⁹) = 0.5 ns."],
["01-14","E","A GPU runs at 0.9 V and draws 400 A. What is its power?",["360 W","444 W","400 W","36 W"],"P = VI = 0.9 × 400 = 360 W, and all of it becomes heat."],
["01-15","E","Kirchhoff's current law says the current flowing into a node equals the current flowing out.",true,"Charge does not pile up at a junction."],
["01-16","M","Two 1 kΩ resistors in series are placed in parallel with a 2 kΩ resistor. The total resistance is:",["1 kΩ","4 kΩ","667 Ω","2 kΩ"],"The series pair is 2 kΩ, and 2 kΩ in parallel with 2 kΩ is 1 kΩ."],
["01-17","M","A 5 V source with 1 Ω of internal resistance drives a 4 Ω load. The voltage across the load is:",["4 V","5 V","1 V","2.5 V"],"The current is 5/(1 + 4) = 1 A, so the load sees 4 V and 1 V is lost inside the source."],
["01-18","E","Kirchhoff's voltage law says that around any closed loop:",["The voltage rises and drops sum to zero","The currents entering and leaving sum to zero","The resistances along the loop sum to zero","The power delivered around the loop stays constant"],"Energy per charge is conserved around a loop."],
["01-19","H","64 output pins switch at once, each pushing a 20 mA step in 1 ns through a shared ground connection with 0.5 nH of inductance. About how much does the chip's internal ground bounce?",["0.64 V","0.01 V","1.28 V","0.32 V"],"The total di/dt is 64 × 20 mA / 1 ns = 1.28 A/ns, and V = L·di/dt = 0.5 nH × 1.28 A/ns = 0.64 V. This is simultaneous switching noise; more ground pins and decoupling reduce it.","r"],
["01-20","M","At high frequency, the return current of a signal on a circuit board flows mainly:",["In the ground plane directly underneath the signal trace, where the loop inductance is smallest","Along the shortest straight line from the load back to the source","Through whichever path has the lowest DC resistance, wherever it lies","Spread evenly across the whole ground plane, regardless of the trace"],"Current returns where the loop area is smallest. A gap in the plane under a trace forces a large loop and causes noise.","r"],
["01-21","E","A 3.3 V output drives an LED through a 220 Ω resistor, and the LED drops 2.0 V. The current is about:",["5.9 mA","15 mA","9.1 mA","2.0 mA"],"(3.3 − 2.0)/220 ≈ 5.9 mA."],
["01-22","M","An EV inverter carries 300 A DC on a 400 V bus. To measure that current while keeping the measuring circuit isolated from the high-voltage bus, engineers usually use:",["A Hall-effect or fluxgate sensor around the conductor","A current transformer, since it works at DC","A shunt resistor read by an ADC on the low-voltage ground","An ammeter placed in parallel with the bus"],"A Hall or fluxgate sensor measures the magnetic field the current makes, so it reads DC and stays galvanically isolated. A current transformer needs a changing current, so it cannot see DC. A shunt is accurate, but a 100 µΩ shunt at 300 A drops 30 mV and burns 9 W, and its signal sits at the bus voltage, so it needs an isolated amplifier.","b"],
// 2 Capacitors, inductors and signals
["02-01","M","A transistor that acts like a 2 kΩ resistor drives a 50 fF load. About how long until the load reaches 50% of its final voltage?",["69 ps","100 ps","35 ps","220 ps"],"τ = RC = 100 ps, and 50% is reached at 0.69τ ≈ 69 ps."],
["02-02","E","The voltage across a capacitor can change instantly.",false,"I = C·dV/dt, so an instant change would need infinite current. Capacitor voltage always changes smoothly."],
["02-03","E","If the voltage on a capacitor doubles, the energy it stores:",["Quadruples","Doubles","Stays the same","Halves"],"E = ½CV², so energy scales with V²."],
["02-04","E","Two 1 µF capacitors connected in series give:",["0.5 µF","2 µF","1 µF","0.25 µF"],"Series capacitors combine like parallel resistors: (1 × 1)/(1 + 1) = 0.5 µF. Parallel capacitors add."],
["02-05","M","The current through a 1 nH inductance changes by 2 A in 1 ns. What voltage appears across it?",["2 V","0.5 V","2 mV","20 V"],"V = L·dI/dt = 10⁻⁹ × 2/10⁻⁹ = 2 V."],
["02-06","H","Which best supplies a chip's power pins during a current step that lasts a few nanoseconds?",["A small, low-inductance capacitor placed right at the pins","A 100 µF capacitor 10 cm away","No capacitor; the regulator responds instantly","An inductor in series with the pins"],"Fast current steps are limited by path inductance. A small capacitor at the pins has the least; a large capacitor far away cannot deliver its charge quickly.","r"],
["02-07","M","Why do boards still use large bulk capacitors alongside small ones?",["They store more charge for slower, longer current changes","They respond faster than small capacitors","They lower the clock frequency","They block DC from the chip"],"Decoupling is a ladder: bulk capacitors for slow changes, small ceramics for faster ones, on-package and on-die capacitance for the fastest."],
["02-08","H","A 50 Ω transmission line ends in a 25 Ω load. The reflection coefficient is:",["−1/3","+1/3","−1/2","0"],"Γ = (ZL − Z0)/(ZL + Z0) = (25 − 50)/75 = −1/3: a third of the step reflects, inverted."],
["02-09","M","A transmission line is left open at its far end. The reflection is:",["Complete, with the same polarity (Γ = +1)","Complete and inverted (Γ = −1)","Zero","Half, and inverted"],"ZL = ∞ gives Γ = +1. A short gives −1 and a matched load gives 0."],
["02-10","E","Terminating a line with a load equal to its characteristic impedance eliminates the reflection.",true,"ZL = Z0 makes Γ = 0."],
["02-11","H","A 100 MHz square wave looks like a sine wave on a 150 MHz oscilloscope. Why?",["The scope filters out the 3rd and higher harmonics, leaving only the fundamental","The probe adds a DC offset that rounds off the corners of the wave","The scope's sample rate is too high, so it smooths the edges","The signal generator distorts square waves above 100 MHz"],"A square wave is a fundamental plus odd harmonics (300 MHz, 500 MHz, ...). Above the scope's bandwidth they are filtered out."],
["02-12","M","About what rise time can a 350 MHz oscilloscope show?",["1 ns","0.35 ns","3.5 ns","10 ns"],"t_rise ≈ 0.35/bandwidth = 0.35/350 MHz = 1 ns."],
["02-13","E","With a steady DC voltage across it, a capacitor behaves like:",["An open circuit","A short circuit","A resistor","An inductor"],"Once charged, no current flows into it. Its reactance 1/(2πfC) is infinite at f = 0."],
["02-14","M","For an RC circuit, the 10% to 90% rise time is about:",["2.2 RC","0.69 RC","1 RC","5 RC"],"0.69RC is the 50% point, RC is 63%, and about 5RC is fully settled."],
["02-15","E","Every on-chip wire and every transistor gate acts as a capacitor.",true,"Conductors separated by insulation form capacitors, which is why switching costs time and energy."],
["02-16","H","A decoupling capacitor has C = 100 nF and a series inductance (ESL) of 1 nH. Above roughly what frequency does it stop behaving like a capacitor?",["About 16 MHz, its self-resonant frequency","About 1.6 GHz","About 160 kHz","It always behaves like a capacitor"],"f = 1/(2π√(LC)) = 1/(2π√(10⁻⁹ × 10⁻⁷)) ≈ 16 MHz. Above it the inductance dominates, which is why smaller, lower-ESL capacitors are added for higher frequencies.","rb"],
["02-17","M","Placing a decoupling capacitor farther from the power pins mainly hurts it by:",["Adding loop inductance between the capacitor and the pins","Reducing its capacitance, since long traces discharge it","Raising its ESR so it can no longer store charge","Raising the chip's clock frequency through extra coupling"],"The extra trace and via length adds inductance, which limits how fast the capacitor can deliver current.","r"],
["02-18","H","A 10 fF capacitor charged to 1 V is connected to an uncharged 30 fF capacitor. The final shared voltage is:",["0.25 V","0.5 V","0.75 V","0.33 V"],"Charge is conserved: 10 fC spread over 40 fF gives 0.25 V. Charge sharing like this limits DRAM reads and troubles dynamic logic."],
["02-19","M","A signal travels about 15 cm per ns on a circuit board. A trace needs transmission-line treatment when its delay exceeds about a sixth of the rise time. For a 0.6 ns rise time, the critical length is about:",["1.5 cm","15 cm","9 cm","0.15 cm"],"A sixth of 0.6 ns is 0.1 ns, and 0.1 ns × 15 cm/ns = 1.5 cm. Longer traces need controlled impedance and termination."],
["02-20","M","A driver with 10 Ω of output resistance drives a 50 Ω line. A series termination resistor at the driver should be about:",["40 Ω","50 Ω","10 Ω","60 Ω"],"The driver plus the resistor should equal Z0: 10 + 40 = 50 Ω. The reflection from the open far end is then absorbed at the source."],
["02-21","E","An inductor opposes:",["Changes in current","Changes in voltage","Steady DC current","Any current at all"],"V = L·di/dt. A constant current produces no voltage, and a fast change produces a large one."],
["02-22","M","The equivalent series resistance (ESR) of a decoupling capacitor:",["Causes an I·ESR voltage drop during current steps, but also damps resonance","Increases its effective capacitance at high frequency","Has no effect, because it is in series with a capacitor","Lets it store more energy, at the cost of slower charging"],"A little ESR helps damp ringing between capacitors; too much weakens the decoupling."],
["02-23","E","Capacitors connected in parallel:",["Add their capacitances","Combine like parallel resistors","Keep only the largest value","Cancel out"],"C_total = C1 + C2 + … Series capacitors combine like parallel resistors."],
["02-24","E","A 5 V step is applied to a 1 kΩ resistor in series with an uncharged 1 µF capacitor. The current just after the step, and long after it, is:",["5 mA, then 0","0, then 5 mA","5 mA the whole time","2.5 mA, then 5 mA"],"An uncharged capacitor cannot change its voltage instantly, so at first it acts like a short and the full 5 V sits across the resistor: 5 V / 1 kΩ = 5 mA. The current then decays as e^(−t/RC), with RC = 1 ms. Once the capacitor has charged to 5 V it acts like an open circuit, and no current flows."],
// 3 Heat
["03-01","M","A 250 W chip has 0.2 °C/W of total thermal resistance to 25 °C air. What is its junction temperature?",["75 °C","50 °C","125 °C","62.5 °C"],"Tj = 25 + 250 × 0.2 = 75 °C."],
["03-02","H","A 400 W chip must stay below 95 °C in 40 °C air. What is the maximum total thermal resistance?",["0.1375 °C/W","0.2375 °C/W","0.1 °C/W","7.3 °C/W"],"(95 − 40)/400 = 0.1375 °C/W."],
["03-03","M","Why do many heat sinks combine a copper base with aluminum fins?",["Copper conducts better where heat is concentrated; aluminum is lighter and cheaper for the large fin area","Aluminum conducts heat better than copper, so it goes where heat spreads out","Copper cannot be extruded into thin fins, so aluminum is the only option","The two metals form a thermocouple that pumps heat away from the chip"],"Copper conducts about twice as well as aluminum, but it is about 3.3× denser and costs more.","r"],
["03-04","E","The main purpose of a heat sink's fins is to:",["Increase the surface area for convection","Store heat during bursts and release it later","Insulate the chip from the hot air around it","Raise the thermal resistance so the chip heats evenly"],"Convection grows with area, and a fan forces air across the fins.","r"],
["03-05","E","Adding fins to a heat sink increases its sink-to-air thermal resistance.",false,"More fin area lowers the thermal resistance, until the fins are packed too tightly for air to flow.","r"],
["03-06","M","A thicker layer of thermal paste always improves cooling.",false,"Paste conducts far worse than metal. It should only fill the microscopic air gaps; extra thickness adds resistance."],
["03-07","E","A heat pipe moves heat mainly by:",["Evaporation at the hot end and condensation at the cold end","A small internal pump pushing water from end to end","Radiation from the hot end to the cold end inside the pipe","Electrical current that carries heat along the copper"],"Phase change gives an effective conductivity far above solid copper.","r"],
["03-08","M","Per unit volume and per degree, how much heat does water carry compared with air?",["Thousands of times more","About the same","About 4 times more","Less"],"Water has about 4× the specific heat of air and is about 800× denser."],
["03-09","E","Thermal resistances along the path junction → case → sink → air combine by:",["Adding in series","Combining like parallel resistors","Multiplying","Taking the largest one"],"Heat flows through each layer in turn, so the resistances add."],
["03-10","M","Thermal runaway happens because:",["Higher temperature raises leakage power, which raises the temperature further","Fans spin faster when hot, which draws more power and heat","Copper softens as it heats, raising its thermal resistance","Clock frequency rises automatically with temperature"],"Leakage grows exponentially with temperature. If cooling cannot keep up, the loop escalates."],
["03-11","H","To throttle, firmware lowers voltage by 10% and frequency by 10%. Dynamic power falls by about:",["27%","10%","20%","50%"],"P ∝ V²f: 0.9² × 0.9 = 0.729, about a 27% reduction.","v"],
["03-12","E","How does a heat sink hand its heat to the surrounding air?",["Convection","Conduction only","Radiation only","Evaporation"],"Conduction carries heat through the metal; convection hands it to moving air. Radiation is minor at chip temperatures."],
["03-13","E","In steady operation, essentially all the electrical power a chip consumes becomes heat.",true,"The chip stores almost no energy, so power in equals heat out."],
["03-14","M","A chip's heat path is 0.1 °C/W junction-to-case, 0.05 °C/W through the paste and 0.15 °C/W sink-to-air. At 200 W in 30 °C air, the junction is at:",["90 °C","60 °C","30 °C","120 °C"],"The total is 0.3 °C/W, giving a 60 °C rise above the 30 °C air."],
["03-15","M","Why does a chip's hotspot run hotter than its average temperature?",["Power density is uneven, and heat spreads sideways only so fast","The package reflects heat back onto the center of the die","Fans cool only the edges of the die, never the middle","The sensors sit in the hottest spot, so the average is guessed"],"A dense block, such as a tensor-core array, can run far hotter than the die average. Heat spreaders and careful floorplanning help."],
["03-16","E","A fan mainly lowers which thermal resistance?",["Sink-to-air","Junction-to-case","Die-to-package","Thermal paste"],"Moving air across the fins speeds up convection.","r"],
["03-17","H","Assume leakage power doubles for every 10 °C rise. A chip leaks 20 W at 60 °C. About how much does it leak at 90 °C?",["160 W","60 W","80 W","40 W"],"Three 10 °C steps: 20 × 2³ = 160 W. This feedback is what drives thermal runaway."],
["03-18","M","High-end GPUs often use liquid cold plates because:",["Water carries heat away far more effectively than air","Water is a perfect electrical insulator","Air cannot touch metal","They make the card lighter"],"Water's higher heat capacity and density let a cold plate remove kilowatts in a small space.","r"],
// 4 Semiconductors
["04-01","E","Which dopant makes n-type silicon?",["Phosphorus (5 valence electrons)","Boron (3 valence electrons)","Germanium","Oxygen"],"The fifth electron has no bond partner and is easily freed."],
["04-02","E","The majority carriers in p-type silicon are:",["Holes","Electrons","Protons","Ions"],"Boron leaves an incomplete bond, a hole, which acts as a positive carrier."],
["04-03","M","How does electron mobility in silicon compare with hole mobility?",["Electrons are about 2–3× more mobile","Holes are about 2–3× more mobile","They are equal","Holes cannot move"],"About 1400 versus 450 cm²/V·s, which is why PMOS is weaker than NMOS of the same size."],
["04-04","M","As temperature rises, carrier mobility in silicon:",["Decreases","Increases","Stays constant","Drops to zero"],"A hotter lattice vibrates more (phonons), so carriers scatter more often and drift more slowly.","o"],
["04-05","M","As temperature rises, the resistance of a copper wire:",["Increases","Decreases","Stays constant","Drops to zero"],"Metal electrons are scattered by lattice vibrations too. Copper rises about 0.4% per °C.","o"],
["04-06","M","Why does mobility fall when silicon gets hotter?",["More phonon (lattice vibration) scattering","Fewer free carriers exist at high temperature","The band gap widens, slowing carriers down","Dopant atoms evaporate out of the lattice"],"Carriers collide with vibrating atoms more often and cannot build up speed.","v"],
["04-07","E","A hole is a positively charged particle, like a proton, moving through the crystal.",false,"A hole is a missing electron in a bond. Neighbouring electrons hop into it, so the vacancy moves like a positive charge."],
["04-08","E","A silicon diode starts conducting strongly in forward bias at about:",["0.6–0.7 V","0.026 V","3.3 V","0.1 V"],"Current rises exponentially once the built-in barrier is overcome."],
["04-09","M","A reverse-biased PN junction conducts:",["Only a tiny leakage current, until breakdown","Heavily, as soon as any reverse voltage is applied","Exactly like a resistor, in proportion to the voltage","As much as in forward bias, but in the other direction"],"The depletion region widens and blocks current."],
["04-10","H","Why is the p-type body under NMOS transistors tied to ground?",["To keep the source/drain-to-body junctions reverse biased","To make the transistor faster by lowering its threshold","To store charge that keeps the transistor on","To reduce gate capacitance by removing the channel"],"Holding the body at the lowest voltage keeps its PN junctions from forward biasing."],
["04-11","M","How does transistor leakage change in hot silicon?",["It rises steeply","It falls","It stays the same","It reverses direction"],"More carriers are generated by heat and thresholds drop, so off-current grows rapidly."],
["04-12","M","The thermal voltage kT/q at room temperature is about:",["26 mV","0.7 V","1.12 V","260 mV"],"It appears in the diode equation and in subthreshold leakage."],
["04-13","E","Pure (undoped) silicon is a good conductor at room temperature.",false,"Fewer than one atom in a trillion has a freed electron, so it conducts poorly. Doping makes it useful."],
["04-14","M","As temperature rises, the number of thermally generated (intrinsic) carriers in silicon:",["Increases exponentially","Decreases, as the lattice traps them","Stays constant, set by the doping","Drops toward zero as atoms vibrate"],"More thermal energy breaks more bonds. This is one reason leakage and junction currents rise quickly when hot.","v"],
["04-15","H","Copper's resistance rises with temperature. The resistance of pure (intrinsic) silicon:",["Falls, because far more carriers are generated","Also rises, for the same scattering reason","Stays the same, since it has no dopants","Becomes infinite, since no carriers remain"],"In a metal the number of carriers is fixed and extra scattering wins. In intrinsic silicon the carrier count grows exponentially and outweighs the mobility loss.","v"],
["04-16","M","At a constant current, a silicon diode's forward voltage changes with temperature by about:",["−2 mV per °C (it falls)","+2 mV per °C","+0.4% per °C","It does not change"],"It falls by about 2 mV for every °C. A hotter junction makes more carriers thermally, so the same current needs less forward voltage. Chips use this steady, predictable drift in their on-die temperature sensors.","b"],
["04-17","M","Heavier doping makes carrier mobility:",["Lower, because carriers scatter off more ionized dopant atoms","Higher, because more carriers share the current","Unchanged, since mobility depends only on temperature","Zero, because the dopants trap every carrier"],"Each dopant atom gives up its carrier and is left as a charged ion fixed in the crystal. Moving carriers are deflected by these charges (impurity scattering), on top of the lattice-vibration scattering in Chapter 4, so more doping means lower mobility.","b"],
["04-18","H","In a very strong electric field, carrier drift velocity:",["Saturates at a maximum value","Keeps rising in proportion to the field","Falls to zero","Reverses direction"],"This is velocity saturation. It is why short-channel transistors deliver less current than the square law predicts."],
["04-19","M","The band gap of silicon is about:",["1.12 eV","0.026 eV","3.4 eV","0.7 eV"],"It is the energy needed to free an electron from a bond, and it shrinks slightly as temperature rises."],
["04-20","E","When silicon gets hotter, the loss of mobility affects:",["Both electrons and holes, so both NMOS and PMOS get weaker","Only electrons, so only NMOS gets weaker","Only holes, so only PMOS gets weaker","Neither carrier; only the wires get slower"],"Phonon scattering affects every carrier.","v"],
["04-21","M","At a nominal (high) supply voltage, how does a CMOS chip respond to rising temperature?",["Transistors drive less current and wires gain resistance, so it gets slower","Transistors speed up while wires slow down, so it barely changes","Both transistors and wires speed up, so it gets faster","Nothing changes until the chip reaches its thermal limit"],"Mobility loss dominates when the overdrive is large, and wire resistance always rises.","v"],
["04-22","M","When an IGBT turns off, its current falls quickly at first and then slowly, in a tail. The tail comes from:",["Minority carriers stored in its drift region, which must recombine","The gate capacitance discharging through the driver","The freewheeling diode's forward voltage","Thermal runaway in the package"],"An IGBT conducts by injecting holes into a lightly doped drift region, which gives it a low on-voltage at high current. At turn-off those stored carriers can only leave by recombining, so the current trails off while the voltage is already high. The tail adds turn-off loss and limits the switching frequency. A MOSFET carries only majority carriers, so it has no tail.","b"],
// 5 MOSFETs
["05-01","E","An NMOS transistor turns on when:",["VGS > Vtn (gate high relative to the source)","VGS < 0","The drain is grounded","Gate current exceeds a threshold"],"A positive gate attracts electrons and forms an inversion channel."],
["05-02","M","A PMOS has its source at 1.0 V, its gate at 0 V, and |Vtp| = 0.3 V. It is:",["On","Off","Reverse biased","Broken down"],"VSG = 1.0 V > 0.3 V. A PMOS turns on when its gate is pulled below its source."],
["05-03","M","An NMOS pass transistor with its gate at VDD = 1.2 V (Vtn = 0.4 V) passes a logic 1. The output reaches:",["0.8 V","1.2 V","0.4 V","1.6 V"],"NMOS passes a weak 1: VDD − Vtn = 0.8 V.","v"],
["05-04","M","A PMOS pass transistor with its gate at 0 V (|Vtp| = 0.35 V) passes a logic 0. The output falls to:",["0.35 V","0 V","−0.35 V","0.65 V"],"PMOS passes a weak 0, stopping at |Vtp|.","v"],
["05-05","E","An NMOS transistor passes a ___ 0 and a ___ 1.",["strong; weak","weak; strong","strong; strong","weak; weak"],"NMOS is good at pulling down and PMOS is good at pulling up.","v"],
["05-06","H","Why does an NMOS pass only a weak 1?",["The output is its source, so VGS shrinks as it rises until it turns off","Electrons cannot carry a logic 1; only holes can","The gate oxide leaks charge away from the output node","The body effect pushes Vt to zero as the output rises"],"When passing a 1, the lower-voltage end (the output) is the source, so VGS = VDD − Vout.","v"],
["05-07","H","Two NMOS pass transistors are in series with both gates at VDD = 1 V, Vt = 0.3 V, and a 1 V input. The output is:",["0.7 V","0.4 V","1.0 V","0.1 V"],"With both gates at the full supply, the threshold drop happens only once.","r"],
["05-08","H","An NMOS whose gate is driven by a 0.7 V node passes a 1.0 V input (Vt = 0.3 V). The output is:",["0.4 V","0.7 V","1.0 V","0.3 V"],"Vout = min(Vin, VG − Vt) = min(1.0, 0.4) = 0.4 V. Drops pile up when degraded nodes drive gates.","r"],
["05-09","H","VDD = 5 V and Vt = 2 V. A 4 V input passes through an NMOS whose gate is at 5 V. The output node reaches:",["3 V","4 V","2 V","5 V"],"min(4, 5 − 2) = 3 V: the gate limits it, not the input.","r"],
["05-10","M","In saturation, which change doubles an NMOS's drain current?",["Doubling the width W","Doubling the length L","Doubling the overdrive VGS − Vt","Doubling Vt"],"I ∝ (W/L)(VGS − Vt)². Doubling W doubles I, doubling L halves it, and doubling the overdrive quadruples it."],
["05-11","M","Under the square law, doubling the overdrive (VGS − Vt) multiplies the saturation current by:",["4","2","√2","8"],"I ∝ (VGS − Vt)²."],
["05-12","M","If μn = 2.5 μp and an NMOS is 1 μm wide, how wide should a same-length PMOS be for equal current?",["2.5 μm","1 μm","0.4 μm","5 μm"],"(W/L)p/(W/L)n = μn/μp."],
["05-13","E","A MOSFET's gate draws a steady DC current while the transistor is on.",false,"The gate sits on an insulating oxide, so current flows only while its capacitance charges or discharges."],
["05-14","M","Lowering a transistor's threshold voltage makes it:",["Faster but exponentially leakier","Slower but much less leaky","Faster and also less leaky","Slower and also leakier"],"More overdrive gives more current, but subthreshold leakage grows exponentially as Vt falls."],
["05-15","M","The body effect means that when the source rises above the body, the threshold voltage:",["Increases","Decreases","Becomes zero","Changes sign"],"It makes a passed 1 even weaker and slows the upper transistors in a stack."],
["05-16","M","Why did the industry move to FinFET and gate-all-around transistors?",["Wrapping the gate around the channel gives better control and less leakage","They need no gate oxide, which removes gate leakage","They conduct with holes only, which balances NMOS and PMOS","They are cheaper to make per wafer than planar transistors"],"At very short channel lengths, a gate sitting only on top could no longer turn the channel fully off."],
["05-17","H","μCox = 200 μA/V², W/L = 4, Vt = 0.4 V and VGS = 1.0 V, in saturation. What is ID?",["144 μA","288 μA","72 μA","480 μA"],"½ × 200 μ × 4 × 0.6² = 144 μA."],
["05-18","H","VDD = 5 V and Vt = 2 V for every NMOS. M1 has its gate at 5 V and passes a 5 V input to node X. Node X drives the gate of M2, which passes a 5 V input to node Y. What are X and Y?",["X = 3 V, Y = 1 V","X = 3 V, Y = 3 V","X = 5 V, Y = 5 V","X = 3 V, Y = 0 V"],"X = min(5, 5 − 2) = 3 V. M2's gate is only 3 V, so Y = min(5, 3 − 2) = 1 V. Each degraded node driving a gate costs another threshold.","r"],
["05-19","M","VDD = 5 V and Vt = 2 V. An NMOS with its gate at 5 V passes a 1 V input. The output is:",["1 V","3 V","0 V","2 V"],"min(1, 5 − 2) = 1 V. Low inputs pass intact; only high inputs are clipped at VG − Vt.","r"],
["05-20","M","VDD = 5 V and Vt = 2 V. An NMOS has its gate driven by a node at 1.5 V. It:",["Stays off; its gate is below Vt","Passes 1.5 V to its output","Passes 3 V to its output","Passes 0.5 V to its output"],"VGS can be at most 1.5 V, under the 2 V threshold, so no channel forms and the output floats.","r"],
["05-21","M","An NMOS has VGS = 1.0 V, Vt = 0.4 V and VDS = 0.3 V. It is in the:",["Linear (triode) region","Saturation region","Cutoff region","Breakdown region"],"Saturation needs VDS ≥ VGS − Vt = 0.6 V. With 0.3 V it is in the linear region."],
["05-22","M","An NMOS has VGS = 0.8 V, Vt = 0.4 V and VDS = 1.0 V. It is in:",["Saturation","The linear (triode) region","Cutoff","Breakdown"],"VDS = 1.0 V ≥ VGS − Vt = 0.4 V, so the channel is pinched off at the drain."],
["05-23","H","At room temperature, subthreshold leakage changes by about 10× for every ___ change in threshold voltage.",["60–100 mV","1–5 mV","300–500 mV","1–2 V"],"The ideal subthreshold swing is about 60 mV per decade at 300 K; real devices are 70–100 mV. Lowering Vt by 100 mV can raise leakage about 10×.","b"],
["05-24","H","DIBL (drain-induced barrier lowering) means that a higher drain voltage:",["Lowers the threshold of a short-channel transistor, raising its leakage","Raises the threshold voltage, cutting leakage","Has no effect once the channel is short","Reduces gate capacitance, speeding the transistor up"],"In a short channel the drain's field helps pull down the source barrier, so off-current rises with VDS.","b"],
["05-25","M","Channel-length modulation means that in saturation:",["The drain current still rises slightly as VDS increases","The current stays exactly constant as VDS rises","The current falls slightly as VDS increases","The gate starts to conduct a small current"],"The pinch-off point moves toward the source, shortening the effective channel and giving a finite output resistance."],
["05-26","M","Two off NMOS transistors stacked in series leak:",["Much less than one off transistor","Twice as much as one off transistor","Exactly as much as one off transistor","Nothing, because two off devices block fully"],"The node between them rises slightly, giving the top transistor a negative VGS and the bottom one a smaller VDS. This is the stack effect."],
["05-27","M","Libraries offer the same cell with high-Vt and low-Vt transistors so that designers can:",["Use low-Vt cells only on critical paths, high-Vt cells elsewhere","Use low-Vt cells everywhere, since they also leak the least","Use high-Vt cells on critical paths, since they switch fastest","Pick a single Vt for the whole chip and skip leakage analysis"],"Multi-Vt optimization trades speed against leakage path by path."],
["05-28","M","High-k gate dielectrics, such as hafnium oxide, were introduced to:",["Cut gate tunneling leakage while keeping strong gate control","Make the gate oxide physically thinner to raise capacitance","Remove the need for a gate electrode altogether","Raise every transistor's threshold to about 1 V"],"A physically thicker high-k layer gives the same capacitance as ultra-thin SiO₂ but blocks tunneling current.","b"],
["05-29","H","There is a gate voltage at which a MOSFET's current barely changes with temperature, because falling Vt and falling mobility cancel. It is called the:",["ZTC (zero-temperature-coefficient) point","Threshold (turn-on) voltage","Pinch-off (saturation) voltage","Flat-band (zero-field) voltage"],"Below the ZTC point current rises with temperature (the Vt effect wins); above it current falls (mobility wins). Low supply voltages near or below it show temperature inversion.","vb"],
["05-30","E","A PMOS transistor turns on when its gate is:",["At least |Vtp| below its source","Above its source","At the same voltage as its drain","Floating"],"VSG > |Vtp|. With the source at VDD, a gate at 0 V turns it on."],
["05-31","M","A PMOS pass transistor with its gate at 0 V passes a strong:",["1","0","Z","Neither value"],"PMOS passes a full VDD but can only pull down to |Vtp|.","v"],
// 6 Analog
["06-01","M","An inverting amplifier has Rin = 2 kΩ, Rf = 10 kΩ and Vin = 0.3 V. What is Vout?",["−1.5 V","+1.5 V","−0.06 V","+1.8 V"],"Gain = −Rf/Rin = −5."],
["06-02","E","A non-inverting amplifier has Rf = 9 kΩ and Rg = 1 kΩ. Its gain is:",["10","9","−9","0.1"],"Gain = 1 + Rf/Rg."],
["06-03","M","With negative feedback, an ideal op-amp's two golden rules are:",["No current flows into the inputs, and the output drives V+ and V− to be equal","Infinite current flows into the inputs, and V+ is always 0","The output always equals V+, whatever the feedback","The gain always equals Rf, independent of Rin"],"These two rules solve nearly every op-amp circuit."],
["06-04","E","An op-amp used with no feedback to compare two inputs acts as a:",["Comparator (a 1-bit ADC)","Voltage follower (a buffer)","Integrator (a ramp generator)","Low-pass filter (a smoother)"],"The huge gain slams the output to its maximum or minimum."],
["06-05","E","A voltage follower (output tied to V−) has a gain of:",["1 (a unity-gain buffer)","0 (it blocks the signal)","Infinite (the open-loop gain)","−1 (an inverting buffer)"],"It lets a weak source drive a heavy load."],
["06-06","H","An NMOS biased at ID = 100 μA with 0.2 V overdrive drives RD = 10 kΩ in a common-source amplifier. The gain is about:",["−10","−1","+10","−100"],"gm = 2ID/Vov = 1 mA/V, and Av = −gm·RD = −10."],
["06-07","M","One step of a 12-bit ADC with a 3.3 V range is about:",["0.81 mV","3.3 mV","0.27 V","8.1 mV"],"3.3/4096 ≈ 0.81 mV."],
["06-08","M","A signal contains frequencies up to 20 kHz. The minimum sampling rate is:",["Just above 40 kHz","20 kHz","10 kHz","Above 200 kHz"],"Sample faster than twice the highest frequency (the Nyquist rate)."],
["06-09","M","Aliasing is caused by:",["Sampling slower than twice the highest signal frequency","Using more ADC bits than the signal needs","A DAC that is too slow for the sample rate","Negative feedback around the input amplifier"],"Fast components then masquerade as slow ones."],
["06-10","H","An LDO converts 3.3 V to 1.0 V for a 2 A load. How much power does the regulator waste?",["4.6 W","2 W","6.6 W","0.6 W"],"(3.3 − 1.0) × 2 = 4.6 W, for an efficiency of about 30%."],
["06-11","E","For a large voltage step-down, a switching (buck) regulator is generally more efficient than a linear regulator.",true,"Buck converters are typically 85–95% efficient; a linear regulator burns the whole voltage difference."],
["06-12","E","Which statement is correct?",["A BJT is current-controlled; a MOSFET is voltage-controlled","Both are current-controlled","A MOSFET's gate needs a steady base current","A BJT has an insulated gate"],"A BJT needs base current (IC = βIB); a MOSFET gate draws no DC current."],
["06-13","M","The cutoff frequency of an RC low-pass filter is:",["1/(2πRC)","RC","2πRC","0.69RC"],"Below it signals pass; above it they are attenuated."],
["06-14","M","In a basic current mirror, two matched transistors share the same VGS, so:",["The output copies the reference current, scaled by W/L","The output current is zero, since the gates draw none","The output voltage equals the input voltage","The two currents add at the output node"],"Equal VGS and equal size give equal saturation current. Doubling the output transistor's width doubles the copy.","b"],
["06-15","M","A differential amplifier rejects noise that appears equally on both of its inputs. This is measured by its:",["CMRR (common-mode rejection ratio)","Slew rate (maximum output slope)","Gain-bandwidth product (GBW)","PSRR (power-supply rejection ratio)"],"It amplifies the difference and ignores the common part.","b"],
["06-16","H","A bandgap reference produces a temperature-stable voltage by adding:",["A voltage that falls with temperature and one that rises with it","Two voltages that both rise with temperature, scaled by resistors","A resistor and a capacitor whose drifts cancel at room temperature","The supply voltage and ground, divided down by matched resistors"],"The opposite slopes cancel to first order, giving about 1.2 V.","b"],
["06-17","M","A PLL multiplies a 100 MHz reference with a divide-by-N counter in its feedback path. With N = 20, its output is:",["2 GHz","5 MHz","100 MHz","120 MHz"],"The loop adjusts the oscillator until f_out/N = f_ref."],
["06-18","M","A PLL's output jitter matters to a digital designer because:",["It reduces the timing margin left in every cycle","It raises the chip's supply voltage","It changes the logic function computed","It only matters for analog blocks"],"Timing analysis subtracts clock uncertainty (jitter plus margin) from the period."],
["06-19","M","An op-amp with a 2 mV input offset voltage is used as a non-inverting amplifier with a gain of 101. With its input grounded, the output sits at about:",["0.2 V","2 mV","0 V","20 mV"],"An ideal op-amp has zero offset. A real one acts like an ideal op-amp with a small voltage source in series with one input, and that source is amplified by the noise gain, 1 + Rf/Rg = 101 here: 2 mV × 101 ≈ 0.2 V. Precision circuits use low-offset or chopper op-amps, or trim the offset out.","b"],
["06-20","H","An op-amp's input stage is a single NMOS differential pair. To let its inputs work all the way from ground to the supply (rail to rail), designers:",["Add a PMOS differential pair in parallel, so one pair is always on","Raise the gain of the output stage","Remove the negative feedback","Make the tail current source larger"],"The input common-mode range is the span of input voltages over which the op-amp keeps working. An NMOS pair turns off when its inputs come within about a VGS of ground, and a PMOS pair turns off near the supply. Putting both in parallel covers the whole range, with care where one pair hands over to the other.","b"],
// 7 Binary, Boolean and number formats
["07-01","E","Decimal 45 in hexadecimal is:",["0x2D","0x45","0x2B","0x3D"],"45 = 101101₂ = 0010 1101 = 0x2D.","a"],
["07-02","E","0xB7 in decimal is:",["183","187","173","117"],"11 × 16 + 7 = 183.","a"],
["07-03","E","The range of an 8-bit two's complement number is:",["−128 to 127","−127 to 127","0 to 255","−256 to 255"],"−2ⁿ⁻¹ to 2ⁿ⁻¹ − 1.","a"],
["07-04","M","−20 in 8-bit two's complement is:",["1110 1100","1001 0100","1110 1011","0001 0100"],"+20 = 0001 0100. Invert: 1110 1011. Add 1: 1110 1100.","a"],
["07-05","M","How many bits do you need to give 1000 items unique IDs?",["10","9","11","1000"],"⌈log₂ 1000⌉ = 10, since 2¹⁰ = 1024."],
["07-06","H","For x = 1011 0110, what is x & (x − 1)?",["1011 0100","1011 0111","0000 0010","1011 0101"],"x − 1 = 1011 0101, and ANDing with x clears the lowest set bit."],
["07-07","M","Which test checks whether 32-bit addresses a and b are on the same 4 KB page?",["(a >> 12) == (b >> 12)","(a & 0xFFF) == (b & 0xFFF)","(a >> 4) == (b >> 4)","|a − b| < 4096"],"The low 12 bits are the offset and the rest is the page number. A small difference can still cross a page boundary.","r"],
["07-08","M","Addresses 0x12345FFF and 0x12346000 are on the same 4 KB page.",false,"Their page numbers, 0x12345 and 0x12346, differ, even though the addresses are only 1 apart.","r"],
["07-09","M","A mask with 1s in bits 8 through 11 (bit 0 is the LSB) is:",["0xF00","0xF0","0xF000","0xFF00"],"((1 << 4) − 1) << 8 = 0xF00.","r"],
["07-10","M","Simplify A + A′B (A′ means NOT A):",["A + B","AB","A","A′ + B"],"A + A′B = A + B."],
["07-11","M","By De Morgan's law, NOT(A·B) equals:",["A′ + B′","A′·B′","A + B","(A + B)′"],"Complement each variable and swap AND with OR."],
["07-12","H","How many different Boolean functions of 3 inputs exist?",["256","8","64","512"],"2^(2³) = 2⁸ = 256."],
["07-13","H","The 3-bit Gray code for 6 is:",["101","110","111","100"],"g = b ⊕ (b >> 1) = 110 ⊕ 011 = 101."],
["07-14","M","F(A,B,C) = Σm(1,3,5,7) simplifies to:",["C","A","AB","A + C"],"Those are exactly the rows where C = 1."],
["07-15","M","In 8-bit signed arithmetic, 127 + 1 gives:",["−128 (it overflows)","128 (it fits)","0 (it wraps to zero)","−1 (all ones)"],"The result wraps; two positives producing a negative signals overflow.","a"],
["07-16","E","XORing a value with itself always gives 0.",true,"a ⊕ a = 0 and a ⊕ 0 = a, the basis of parity and prefix-XOR tricks."],
["07-17","M","Sign-extending the 4-bit value 1101 to 8 bits gives:",["1111 1101","0000 1101","1101 0000","1000 1101"],"Copy the sign bit into the new upper bits, so −3 stays −3.","a"],
["07-18","E","In Gray code, how many bits change between consecutive values?",["Exactly one","Exactly two","All of them","It varies"],"That is why Gray-coded counters are safe to read across clock domains."],
["07-19","M","When adding two 8-bit two's-complement numbers, signed overflow has occurred when:",["The carry into the sign bit differs from the carry out of it","There is any carry out of the sign bit","The result's sign bit is 1 (negative)","Both inputs are negative, whatever the result"],"Equivalently, two operands of the same sign produce a result of the opposite sign.","a"],
["07-22","E","Shifting an unsigned binary number left by 3 bits:",["Multiplies it by 8","Divides it by 8","Adds 3 to it","Multiplies it by 3"],"Each left shift multiplies by 2.","a"],
["07-23","M","An arithmetic right shift of the 8-bit value 1111 0000 (−16) by 2 gives:",["1111 1100 (−4)","0011 1100 (60)","1100 0000","0000 1111"],"Arithmetic shifts copy the sign bit in from the left, so a right shift divides signed values by 2 per step.","a"],
["07-24","M","In BCD (binary-coded decimal), decimal 59 is stored as:",["0101 1001","0011 1011","0101 0101","1001 0101"],"BCD stores each decimal digit in its own 4 bits instead of converting the whole number to binary. 5 = 0101 and 9 = 1001, so 59 = 0101 1001. It wastes some codes (1010 to 1111 are unused) but makes decimal displays easy.","ab"],
["07-25","M","A mask with 1s in bits 3 through 6 (bit 0 is the LSB) is:",["0x78","0x3C","0xF0","0x7F"],"Width 6 − 3 + 1 = 4, so ((1 << 4) − 1) << 3 = 1111 000₂ = 0x78.","r"],
["07-26","M","Which address is on the same 4 KB page as 0x00005ABC?",["0x00005000","0x00006ABC","0x00004FFF","0x00015ABC"],"The page number is the address shifted right by 12 bits: 0x5 for both 0x00005ABC and 0x00005000. The others are on pages 0x6, 0x4 and 0x15.","r"],
// 8 CMOS gates
["08-01","H","An inverter is built with its NMOS connected to VDD and its PMOS to ground. With input 1, the output is about:",["VDD − Vtn (a weak 1)","0 V (a strong 0)","|Vtp| (a weak 0)","VDD (a strong 1)"],"The top NMOS pulls up but passes a weak 1, so the output stops one threshold below VDD.","o"],
["08-02","H","The swapped inverter (NMOS to VDD, PMOS to ground) behaves as:",["A non-inverting buffer with degraded output levels","An inverter with full rail-to-rail swing","A ring oscillator that never settles","A gate whose output is stuck at 0"],"Input 1 gives VDD − Vtn and input 0 gives |Vtp|. Each transistor acts as a source follower: weak drive, small noise margins, and gain below 1.","o"],
["08-03","M","In the swapped inverter, input 0 gives an output of about:",["|Vtp|","0 V","VDD","VDD − Vtn"],"The bottom PMOS pulls down but passes a weak 0.","v"],
["08-04","H","Chaining several swapped inverters restores clean logic levels, the way normal inverters do.",false,"Their gain is below 1 and each stage loses another threshold, so the levels collapse.","v"],
["08-05","E","A static CMOS 2-input NAND gate has:",["PMOS in parallel and NMOS in series","PMOS in series and NMOS in parallel","All transistors in series","All transistors in parallel"],"The output is 0 only when A AND B are 1: series NMOS. The pull-up is the dual: parallel PMOS."],
["08-06","E","A static CMOS 2-input NOR gate has:",["PMOS in series and NMOS in parallel","PMOS in parallel and NMOS in series","NMOS only","PMOS only"],"The output is 0 when A OR B is 1: parallel NMOS. Series PMOS pull up."],
["08-07","E","How many transistors are in a static CMOS AND2 gate?",["6","4","2","8"],"A NAND2 (4 transistors) followed by an inverter (2)."],
["08-08","M","Why are NAND gates generally preferred over NOR gates?",["NOR puts slow PMOS transistors in series","NAND has fewer transistors","NOR is not universal","NAND has no parasitic capacitance"],"Series PMOS is slow, or must be made very wide to compensate."],
["08-09","M","A single static CMOS stage can implement a non-inverting function such as AND.",false,"A rising input can only turn on NMOS pull-downs, so a single stage is always inverting. AND needs NAND plus an inverter."],
["08-10","H","The pull-down network for F = NOT(AB + C) is:",["NMOS A in series with NMOS B, and that pair in parallel with NMOS C","A in parallel with B, and that pair in series with C","All three in series","All three in parallel"],"In the pull-down, series means AND and parallel means OR. The PMOS pull-up is the dual."],
["08-11","M","If an inverter's PMOS is made much stronger than its NMOS, the switching threshold VM:",["Moves up","Moves down","Stays at VDD/2","Becomes 0"],"The stronger pull-up wins the tug-of-war until the input is higher."],
["08-12","M","The high noise margin is defined as:",["NMH = VOH − VIH","NMH = VIH − VOH","NMH = VDD − VOL","NMH = VIL − VOL"],"It is the slack between the 1 a driver produces and the 1 a receiver needs."],
["08-13","M","A transmission gate passes both 0 and 1 at full strength because:",["Its NMOS passes a strong 0 and its PMOS passes a strong 1","It uses two NMOS transistors in parallel","It has gain, which restores the levels","It is clocked, so it refreshes each cycle"],"An NMOS and a PMOS sit in parallel, driven by opposite control signals."],
["08-14","E","An ideal static CMOS inverter with a steady input draws:",["Essentially no current, only leakage","Its full saturation current","A current proportional to frequency","A large current when the input is 1"],"One transistor is always off, so there is no path from the supply to ground."],
["08-15","M","A drawback of pseudo-NMOS logic (an always-on PMOS load) is:",["Static current flows whenever the output is low","It cannot implement NOR or other wide gates","It needs a clock to precharge its output","It needs more transistors than static CMOS"],"The output low is set by a voltage-divider tug-of-war."],
["08-16","M","A drawback of dynamic (precharge and evaluate) logic is:",["Its output is held only by stored charge, so it is fragile","It is slower than static CMOS for the same function","It cannot be clocked, so it cannot be pipelined","It has no precharge phase, so its outputs float"],"It is fast and compact, but fragile and power-hungry."],
["08-17","H","With μn = 2μp and a reference inverter of NMOS width 1 and PMOS width 2, a NOR2 sized for equal strength has:",["Each NMOS 1, each PMOS 4","Each NMOS 2, each PMOS 2","Each NMOS 2, each PMOS 4","Each NMOS 1, each PMOS 2"],"Two PMOS in series must each be twice the reference PMOS width."],
["08-18","E","A disabled tristate buffer's output is:",["High impedance (Z), disconnected","Logic 0","Logic 1","Unknown but actively driven"],"Both its pull-up and pull-down are off, so another driver can use the bus."],
["08-19","M","VDD = 1.0 V, Vtn = 0.3 V and |Vtp| = 0.35 V. An inverter is built with the NMOS on the VDD side and the PMOS on the ground side. With input 1, the output is:",["0.7 V","1.0 V","0 V","0.35 V"],"The top NMOS passes a weak 1, VDD − Vtn = 0.7 V, and the bottom PMOS is off.","v"],
["08-20","M","Same swapped inverter (VDD = 1.0 V, Vtn = 0.3 V, |Vtp| = 0.35 V). With input 0, the output is:",["0.35 V","0 V","1.0 V","0.65 V"],"The PMOS on the ground side passes a weak 0, stopping at |Vtp|.","v"],
["08-21","M","As a logic function, the swapped inverter (NMOS to VDD, PMOS to ground) computes:",["Y = A (non-inverting)","Y = NOT A (inverting)","Y = 0 for any input","Y = 1 for any input"],"Input 1 turns on the top NMOS (pulling up) and input 0 turns on the bottom PMOS (pulling down), so the output follows the input.","v"],
["08-22","H","Why is the swapped inverter slow to reach its final output level?",["Each transistor's VGS shrinks as the output nears its final value","Its gate capacitance is zero, so nothing drives the output","It has positive feedback that fights every transition","Its transistors are fully on, so they short the supply"],"Near VDD − Vtn the NMOS is barely on, so the end of the transition crawls.","v"],
["08-23","H","The swapped inverter's weak high (VDD − Vtn) drives a normal CMOS inverter. What problem can this cause in the second inverter?",["Its PMOS may not fully turn off, so static current flows","None; the second inverter restores a perfect output","Its NMOS cannot turn on, so its output stays high","It latches up immediately and shorts the supply"],"With its input at VDD − Vtn, the second inverter's PMOS sees VSG = Vtn, which can be near or above |Vtp|, so both transistors conduct.","v"],
["08-24","M","In the swapped inverter with a steady input of 1, once the output settles at VDD − Vtn, the static current is:",["About zero: both transistors end up off","Large, from VDD through both transistors to ground","Equal to the NMOS saturation current","Negative, flowing back into the supply"],"Neither transistor provides a DC path once the output sits one threshold below VDD.","v"],
["08-25","M","The voltage gain of the swapped inverter is:",["Less than 1, so it cannot restore noisy logic levels","Much greater than 1, like a normal inverter","Exactly −1, an inverting unity buffer","Infinite at the switching threshold"],"Source followers have gain below 1. A normal inverter's high gain near its switching point is what cleans up noise.","v"],
["08-26","H","On a real chip, the VDD and ground pins are accidentally swapped. The most likely result is:",["Forward-biased well and ESD diodes conduct large current","The chip runs as a non-inverting version of itself","Nothing; CMOS is symmetric, so it runs normally","The logic runs with every output inverted"],"The p-substrate is tied to the ground pin and the n-wells to VDD. Reversing the supply forward-biases those junctions.","vb"],
["08-27","E","A normal CMOS inverter has its PMOS connected to ___ and its NMOS connected to ___.",["VDD; ground","ground; VDD","VDD; VDD","ground; ground"],"PMOS passes a strong 1 and NMOS a strong 0, so each sits on the rail it passes well.","v"],
["08-28","M","An NMOS on the VDD side of a circuit pulls its output up as a source follower. The output can rise at most to:",["VG − Vtn","VG","VDD + Vtn","0 V"],"The output is the source, and the transistor turns off when VGS falls to Vtn.","v"],
["08-29","M","A ring oscillator has 5 inverters, each with 20 ps of delay. Its frequency is:",["5 GHz","10 GHz","2.5 GHz","1 GHz"],"f = 1/(2·N·td) = 1/(2 × 5 × 20 ps) = 5 GHz. An edge must travel around the ring twice per period.","b"],
["08-30","M","A ring oscillator needs an odd number of inverters because:",["An even number has a stable state and will not oscillate","An even number oscillates too fast to measure","Odd numbers use less power per stage","It is only a convention; any count oscillates"],"With an odd count, the signal returns inverted and can never settle.","b"],
["08-31","M","On an inverter's voltage transfer curve, the switching threshold VM is the point where:",["Vout = Vin","Vout = VDD","Vin = 0","Vout = 0"],"It is also where the gain is highest."],
["08-32","M","VOH = 0.95 V, VIH = 0.65 V, VIL = 0.35 V and VOL = 0.05 V. The low noise margin NML is:",["0.30 V","0.60 V","0.35 V","0.05 V"],"NML = VIL − VOL = 0.35 − 0.05 = 0.30 V."],
["08-33","E","How many transistors are in a static CMOS 3-input NAND gate?",["6","8","3","9"],"Three NMOS in series and three PMOS in parallel."],
["08-34","H","The pull-up network of F = NOT(A·(B + C)) is:",["PMOS A in parallel with (PMOS B in series with PMOS C)","PMOS A in series with (B in parallel with C)","All three PMOS in parallel","All three PMOS in series"],"The pull-down is A in series with (B parallel C). The pull-up is its dual: swap series and parallel."],
["08-35","M","A dynamic (domino) gate uses a weak keeper transistor to:",["Hold the precharged output against leakage and noise","Speed up evaluation by adding pull-down strength","Precharge the output at the start of each cycle","Generate the evaluate clock for the next stage"],"The keeper replaces charge lost to leakage while the inputs stay low.","b"],
["08-36","M","A 2:1 mux built from two transmission gates, plus an inverter to make the complement of the select, uses how many transistors?",["6","4","12","8"],"Two transmission gates of 2 transistors each, plus 2 for the inverter."],
["08-37","E","A static CMOS gate draws significant supply current mainly:",["While its output is switching","When idle with all inputs at 0","When idle with all inputs at 1","Never"],"At rest it only leaks; current flows while capacitances charge and discharge."],
["08-38","H","Logic built only from NMOS pass transistors usually needs a level-restoring stage because:",["NMOS passes a weak 1, which slows the next gate and can cause static current","NMOS passes a weak 0, which leaves the next gate partly on","It has too much gain, so the outputs overshoot","Pass transistors draw gate current from the previous stage"],"An inverter with a feedback PMOS restores the full 1.","v"],
["08-39","M","Custom (full-custom) circuit design is used for blocks such as SRAM bitcells, SerDes and PLLs because:",["Hand-drawn transistors beat standard cells for these blocks","It is always the faster way to finish a design","It needs no simulation before tape-out","Standard cells cannot implement logic at all"],"Most logic uses synthesized standard cells; the most critical or analog blocks are designed transistor by transistor.","b"],
// 9 Combinational logic and adders
["09-01","M","Which 2:1 mux connection implements OR (A + B)? The mux outputs I1 when S = 1 and I0 when S = 0.",["S = B, I0 = A, I1 = 1","S = B, I0 = 0, I1 = A","S = A, I0 = 1, I1 = 0","S = A, I0 = B, I1 = 0"],"B = 1 gives 1 and B = 0 gives A, so Y = B′A + B = A + B.","o"],
["09-02","M","A 2:1 mux with S = A, I0 = B, I1 = 1 also implements A + B.",true,"OR treats its two inputs the same way, so selecting on A works as well as selecting on B.","o"],
["09-03","E","Which 2:1 mux connection implements AND (A·B)?",["S = A, I0 = 0, I1 = B","S = A, I0 = B, I1 = 1","S = A, I0 = 1, I1 = B","S = B, I0 = 1, I1 = 0"],"A = 0 gives 0 and A = 1 gives B.","v"],
["09-04","E","Which 2:1 mux connection implements NOT A?",["S = A, I0 = 1, I1 = 0","S = A, I0 = 0, I1 = 1","S = 1, I0 = A, I1 = 0","S = 0, I0 = A, I1 = 1"],"A = 0 selects 1 and A = 1 selects 0.","v"],
["09-05","H","What is the minimum number of 2:1 muxes needed to build XOR, if only A, B, 0 and 1 are available?",["2","1","3","4"],"One mux makes B′; the second uses S = A, I0 = B, I1 = B′.","v"],
["09-06","E","How many 2:1 muxes make an 8:1 mux?",["7","8","4","3"],"An N:1 mux takes N − 1 two-input muxes arranged as a tree."],
["09-07","H","To build the 3-input majority function from a 4:1 mux with A and B on the selects, the data inputs for AB = 00, 01, 10, 11 are:",["0, C, C, 1","C, 0, 1, C","0, 0, 1, 1","1, C, C, 0"],"With both 0 there is no majority; with one 1 it depends on C; with both 1 it is already a majority."],
["09-08","M","How many 2-input NAND gates does an OR gate take?",["3","2","1","4"],"Invert each input with a NAND, then NAND them: (A′B′)′ = A + B."],
["09-09","E","A full adder's carry-out is the:",["Majority of A, B and Cin","XOR of A, B and Cin","AND of A, B and Cin","OR of A and B"],"Cout = AB + Cin(A ⊕ B). The sum bit is the XOR of all three.","a"],
["09-10","M","A 32-bit ripple-carry adder has a 50 ps carry delay per stage. What is the worst-case carry delay?",["1.6 ns","50 ps","250 ps","3.2 ns"],"In the worst case the carry ripples through all 32 stages.","a"],
["09-11","M","An adder computes A − B in two's complement as:",["A + B′ + 1","A + B′","A′ + B + 1","A + B − 1"],"Invert B and set the carry-in to 1.","a"],
["09-12","H","50 XOR gates are chained. Gate 1 has inputs 1 and X; every later gate has the previous gate's output and X. What does gate 50 output?",["1","X","X′","0"],"Gate 1 gives X′, gate 2 gives X′ ⊕ X = 1, and the pattern repeats: odd gates X′, even gates 1.","r"],
["09-13","H","In the same 50-gate XOR chain, gate 37's output passed through an inverter is:",["X","X′","1","0"],"Odd gates output X′; inverted, that is X.","r"],
["09-14","H","With an inverter of 1 ns delay, Z = A + A′ glitches low when A rises from 0 to 1.",false,"When A rises, the OR gate already sees A = 1. The glitch happens when A falls: for 1 ns both OR inputs are 0."],
["09-15","E","The propagation delay of a combinational block is set by its:",["Critical (slowest) path","Fastest (shortest) path","Average path delay","Number of inputs"],"The fastest path sets the contamination delay instead."],
["09-16","M","A 2ⁿ:1 multiplexer with the n inputs on its selects can implement any n-input Boolean function.",true,"Put the truth table's outputs on the data inputs. That is a lookup table, like an FPGA LUT."],
["09-17","H","Z = A·B′, where B′ comes from an inverter (rise 2 ns, fall 1 ns) and the AND gate has rise 3 ns and fall 2 ns. Initially A = 0 and B = 1; at t = 0, A rises and B falls. When does Z become 1?",["5 ns","3 ns","4 ns","2 ns"],"B′ rises at 2 ns (the inverter's rise delay), and the AND output rises 3 ns after that.","r"],
["09-18","M","Which 2:1 mux connection implements NAND, NOT(A·B)? (I0 is chosen when S = 0.)",["S = A, I0 = 1, I1 = B′","S = A, I0 = 0, I1 = B′","S = A, I0 = 1, I1 = B","S = A, I0 = B′, I1 = 1"],"When A = 0, NAND is 1; when A = 1, it is NOT B.","v"],
["09-19","M","Which 2:1 mux connection implements NOR, NOT(A + B)?",["S = A, I0 = B′, I1 = 0","S = A, I0 = B′, I1 = 1","S = A, I0 = B, I1 = 0","S = A, I0 = 0, I1 = B′"],"When A = 0, NOR is NOT B; when A = 1, it is 0.","v"],
["09-20","M","A 2:1 mux has S = A, I0 = B and I1 = B′. It implements:",["A XOR B","A XNOR B","A OR B","A AND B"],"A = 0 passes B, and A = 1 passes NOT B: exactly XOR.","v"],
["09-21","M","A 2:1 mux has S = A, I0 = B′ and I1 = B. It implements:",["A XNOR B","A XOR B","NOT B","A NAND B"],"A = 0 gives NOT B and A = 1 gives B, which is 1 exactly when A = B.","v"],
["09-22","M","A 2:1 mux has S = B, I0 = A and I1 = 0. It implements:",["A·B′","A + B","A·B","A + B′"],"B = 0 passes A, and B = 1 forces 0. Putting 0 instead of 1 on I1 is a common wrong answer to the OR question.","v"],
["09-23","H","Which of these 2:1 mux connections does NOT implement A + B?",["S = A, I0 = 1, I1 = B","S = A, I0 = B, I1 = 1","S = B, I0 = A, I1 = 1","All three implement A + B"],"S = A, I0 = 1, I1 = B gives A′ + B, because A = 0 forces a 1. The OR connections put the constant 1 on I1.","v"],
["09-24","E","A 2:1 mux has S = A, I0 = 0 and I1 = 1. Its output is:",["A","NOT A","Always 0","Always 1"],"It passes the select signal itself, acting as a buffer.","v"],
["09-25","M","The Shannon expansion F = S′·F(S = 0) + S·F(S = 1) explains why:",["Any Boolean function can be built from 2:1 muxes","Adders need carry chains to add multi-bit numbers","Flip-flops need clocks to store values","NAND gates are faster than NOR gates"],"Put a variable on the select and the two cofactors on I0 and I1, and repeat until only constants and variables remain.","v"],
["09-26","M","A 2:1 mux is functionally complete (it can build any function) when the constants 0 and 1 are available.",true,"It can build NOT (S = A, I0 = 1, I1 = 0) and AND (S = A, I0 = 0, I1 = B), and NOT with AND is complete.","v"],
["09-27","E","How many 2:1 muxes does a 4:1 mux take?",["3","2","4","6"],"Two muxes select on S0, and a third selects between their outputs on S1.","v"],
["09-28","H","With only A, B, 0 and 1 available, how many 2:1 muxes does XNOR need?",["2","1","3","4"],"One mux makes B′ (S = B, I0 = 1, I1 = 0); the second uses S = A, I0 = B′, I1 = B.","v"],
["09-29","M","A 2:1 mux has S = A, I0 = B and I1 = C. As a Boolean expression, its output is:",["A′B + AC","AB + A′C","A + B + C","ABC"],"I0 is chosen when A = 0, so B is ANDed with A′.","v"],
["09-30","H","50 XNOR gates are chained. Gate 1 has inputs 0 and X; every later gate takes the previous output and X. What does gate 50 output?",["0","1","X","X′"],"Gate 1: 0 XNOR X = X′. Gate 2: X′ XNOR X = 0. The pattern repeats, so odd gates give X′ and even gates give 0.","r"],
["09-31","H","N XOR gates are chained. Gate 1 has inputs X and X; every later gate takes the previous output and X. Gate N outputs:",["0 for odd N, X for even N","X for every N","0 for every N","X for odd N, 0 for even N"],"Gate 1: X ⊕ X = 0. Gate 2: 0 ⊕ X = X. Gate 3: X ⊕ X = 0, and so on.","r"],
["09-32","H","Y = A·B, where the AND gate has a 3 ns rise delay and a 2 ns fall delay. B stays at 1. A falls at t = 0 and rises again at t = 1 ns. Ignoring pulse filtering, when does Y return to 1?",["4 ns","3 ns","2 ns","5 ns"],"Y falls at 0 + 2 = 2 ns. The rising input at 1 ns makes Y rise 3 ns later, at 4 ns.","r"],
["09-33","E","When a 3-to-8 decoder is enabled, how many of its outputs are active at once?",["Exactly one","Three","All eight","None"],"Each 3-bit input code selects one output line."],
["09-34","H","A carry-lookahead adder computes carries from generate (G = A·B) and propagate (P = A ⊕ B) signals. The carry into bit 2 is:",["G1 + P1·G0 + P1·P0·C0","G1 + P1·G0 + P0·C0","G1·P1 + G0·P0 + C0","P1·P0·C0 + G1·G0"],"C2 = G1 + P1·C1, and C1 = G0 + P0·C0.","a"],
["09-35","M","A carry-select adder speeds up addition by:",["Computing upper blocks for both carry-ins, then picking one","Removing the carry chain by adding each bit independently","Using only XOR gates, which are faster than AND-OR logic","Adding the bits one at a time, which needs less hardware"],"It spends extra area on duplicate adders to gain speed.","a"],
["09-36","M","An unsigned 4-bit × 4-bit multiplier produces a product of how many bits?",["8","4","16","5"],"The largest product, 15 × 15 = 225, needs 8 bits. In general, n × m bits gives n + m bits.","a"],
["09-38","M","An equality comparator for two 8-bit values can be built from:",["Eight XNOR gates (one per bit pair) feeding an AND","Eight AND gates (one per bit pair) feeding an OR","Eight XOR gates (one per bit pair) feeding an AND","A single 8:1 multiplexer on the bit lines"],"Each XNOR outputs 1 when its bits match, and all eight must match.","a"],
["09-39","M","A priority encoder has inputs I3..I0 = 0110. It outputs:",["2 (binary 10)","1 (binary 01)","3 (binary 11)","6 (binary 110)"],"I2 is the highest-numbered input that is 1."],
["09-40","M","16 XOR gates are chained. Gate 1 has inputs 1 and X; every later gate takes the previous output and X. Gate 16 outputs:",["1","0","X","X′"],"Gate 1 gives X′, gate 2 gives X′ ⊕ X = 1, and the pattern repeats: odd gates X′, even gates 1.","r"],
["09-41","M","10 XOR gates are chained. Gate 1 has inputs 0 and X; every later gate takes the previous output and X. Gate 10 outputs:",["0","X","1","X′"],"Gate 1 gives X, gate 2 gives X ⊕ X = 0, gate 3 gives X again: odd gates X, even gates 0.","r"],
["09-42","M","Y = NOT(A·B) comes from a NAND with a rise delay of 2 ns and a fall delay of 1 ns. B = 1 and A = 0, so Y = 1. A rises at t = 0. When does Y fall?",["1 ns","2 ns","3 ns","0 ns"],"Y's falling transition takes the fall delay: 1 ns.","r"],
["09-43","H","Three inverters are in series, each with a rise delay of 3 ns and a fall delay of 2 ns. The input rises at t = 0. When does the final output change, and how?",["It falls at 7 ns","It rises at 8 ns","It falls at 6 ns","It rises at 9 ns"],"Inverter 1 falls at 2 ns, inverter 2 rises at 2 + 3 = 5 ns, inverter 3 falls at 5 + 2 = 7 ns. Track the direction at every stage.","r"],
["09-44","E","Which tie-offs make each gate pass B unchanged?",["AND with A = 1, OR with A = 0, XOR with A = 0","AND with A = 0, OR with A = 1, XOR with A = 1","AND with A = 1, OR with A = 1, XOR with A = 1","AND with A = 0, OR with A = 0, XOR with A = 0"],"1 is the identity for AND (1·B = B) and 0 is the identity for OR (0 + B = B). XOR with 0 passes B and XOR with 1 inverts it, which makes XOR a controlled inverter. The other tie-offs force the output: AND with 0 always gives 0, and OR with 1 always gives 1."],
["09-45","H","Counting the 1s in a 7-bit input gives a 3-bit result. Using only full adders, how many does it take?",["4","7","3","6"],"A full adder counts three bits of the same weight into a sum (weight 1) and a carry (weight 2). Two adders take six of the inputs. A third adds their two sums and the seventh input, giving bit 0. A fourth adds the three carries, giving bits 1 and 2. With 8 inputs the count can reach 8, which needs a 4-bit result and more adders.","b"],
["09-46","H","A sorting network orders 4 values with compare-and-swap units, each of which outputs the smaller and the larger of its two inputs. The smallest such network uses:",["5 units in 3 levels","6 units in 6 levels","4 units in 2 levels","3 units in 3 levels"],"Compare (0,1) and (2,3), then (0,2) and (1,3), which puts the minimum and maximum in place, then (1,2). That is 5 units in 3 levels, and no 4-input network uses fewer. Sorting networks suit hardware because the comparisons are fixed in advance: the network is pure combinational logic and can be pipelined one level per stage.","b"],
// 10 Latches and flip-flops
["10-01","M","One advantage of a flip-flop over a latch is that a flip-flop:",["Captures its input only at the clock edge, so data cannot race through","Uses fewer transistors, so it is smaller and cheaper","Allows time borrowing between pipeline stages","Has a lower clock load, so it saves clock power"],"It is never transparent, so glitches are ignored and timing is predictable. The other options are latch advantages.","o"],
["10-02","M","An advantage of a latch over a flip-flop is that a latch:",["Is smaller and allows time borrowing","Ignores glitches on its input while it is enabled","Is edge-triggered, so its timing is predictable","Can never go metastable, since it is level-sensitive"],"It is about half the size, uses less power, and lets a slow stage borrow time while it is transparent.","v"],
["10-03","E","A D latch with its enable high:",["Is transparent: Q follows D","Holds its value","Captures D only on the rising edge","Outputs Z"],"While enabled, every change on D passes through to Q."],
["10-04","M","In a positive-edge master–slave D flip-flop, the master latch is transparent when:",["CLK = 0","CLK = 1","Only at the rising edge","Never"],"At the rising edge the master closes and the slave opens, passing on the captured value."],
["10-05","H","A D latch built from a 2:1 mux (select = EN) feeds its own output back to:",["The data input selected when EN = 0","The data input selected when EN = 1","The select line","Ground"],"Q = EN ? D : Q. When disabled, the mux passes its own output back, holding the value."],
["10-06","M","Two 2:1 mux latches in series, enabled in opposite clock phases, form an edge-triggered D flip-flop.",true,"That is the master–slave structure, built from muxes.","r"],
["10-07","M","A NOR-based SR latch with S = R = 1:",["Forbidden: Q and Q′ both go to 0","Sets Q = 1 and Q′ = 0","Resets Q = 0 and Q′ = 1","Holds its previous value"],"Q and Q′ stop being complements."],
["10-08","E","A T flip-flop with T = 1 is clocked at 25 kHz. Its output frequency is:",["12.5 kHz","25 kHz","50 kHz","6.25 kHz"],"It toggles once per clock edge, dividing the frequency by 2."],
["10-09","E","On each clock edge, a JK flip-flop with J = K = 1:",["Toggles","Holds","Sets","Resets"],"Q⁺ = JQ′ + K′Q = Q′."],
["10-10","M","A flip-flop's active-low asynchronous clear is tied to 0. Q is:",["Stuck at 0","Working normally","Stuck at 1","Toggling"],"Active-low means 0 asserts it, so it clears permanently. Active-high inputs tied to 0 would be harmlessly inactive."],
["10-11","M","With careful design, metastability can be completely eliminated.",false,"It can only be made exponentially unlikely, by giving the flip-flop time to resolve."],
["10-12","E","A D flip-flop with Q′ fed back to D is clocked at 100 MHz. Q has a frequency of:",["50 MHz","100 MHz","200 MHz","25 MHz"],"It toggles every clock edge, completing one cycle every two clock periods."],
["10-13","H","A T flip-flop is built from a D flip-flop by setting D =",["T ⊕ Q","T · Q","T + Q","T′"],"T = 1 inverts Q and T = 0 keeps it."],
["10-14","M","Which storage element passes glitches on its input through to its output?",["A latch while it is transparent","An edge-triggered flip-flop","Neither","Both equally"],"A flip-flop only samples its input near the clock edge.","v"],
["10-15","M","Metastability occurs when:",["The input changes inside the setup/hold window around the clock edge","The clock is too slow for the flip-flop's internal nodes","The supply voltage is above the flip-flop's rating","The flip-flop has an asynchronous reset input"],"The feedback loop can be caught balanced between its two states for an unpredictable time."],
["10-16","M","Which is NOT an advantage of a flip-flop over a latch?",["It allows time borrowing between stages","Data cannot race through it while the clock is high","Glitches on its input outside the sampling window are ignored","Its timing is simpler to analyze"],"Time borrowing is a latch advantage, because a latch stays transparent for a whole clock phase.","v"],
["10-17","M","A positive-edge D flip-flop and a positive-level D latch share a clock. D changes while CLK is high, after the rising edge. Which output changes?",["Only the latch's","Only the flip-flop's","Both","Neither"],"The latch is transparent while CLK is high; the flip-flop waits for the next rising edge.","v"],
["10-18","H","Two positive-level latches are connected back to back, with no logic between them, on the same clock. While CLK is high:",["Data races through both latches in the same phase","Each latch holds its value","The pair acts as an edge-triggered flip-flop","Both outputs go to Z"],"Both are transparent at once. A flip-flop uses opposite clock phases to prevent exactly this.","v"],
["10-19","M","A positive-edge flip-flop is built from two latches. The master is transparent while CLK is low; the slave is transparent while CLK is:",["High","Low","Never","Both high and low"],"At the rising edge, the master closes and the slave opens.","v"],
["10-20","E","A typical static flip-flop has about how many times as many transistors as a latch?",["2×","The same","10×","Half"],"A flip-flop is two latches, which is why the latch is smaller and lower power.","v"],
["10-21","H","Integrated clock-gating (ICG) cells hold the enable in a latch rather than a flip-flop because:",["A latch open while CLK is low holds the enable steady through the high phase","Latches are edge-triggered, so they sample the enable cleanly","Flip-flops cannot store an enable for more than one cycle","Latches have zero delay, so the enable arrives instantly"],"The enable may change while CLK is low; the latch freezes it during the high phase, so the gated clock cannot glitch.","v"],
["10-22","M","In a latch-based pipeline, a stage's logic finishes 200 ps late, but the next latch is still transparent. This is called:",["Time borrowing","A hold violation","Metastability","Clock skew"],"The late data flows through the open latch, and the next stage starts late, borrowing its time.","v"],
["10-23","E","A flip-flop's setup and hold times define a window around:",["The active clock edge","The middle of the high phase","The falling edge, for every flip-flop","Reset"],"Data must be stable from tsu before the capturing edge until th after it.","v"],
["10-24","H","A synchronizer flip-flop resolves metastability with a time constant τ = 20 ps. Giving it 100 ps more resolution time multiplies the MTBF by about:",["About 148 (e⁵)","About 5 (linear in time)","About 25 (quadratic in time)","About 2 (one doubling)"],"MTBF grows as e^(t/τ), and 100/20 = 5 time constants."],
["10-25","M","An SR latch built from NAND gates is set by driving:",["S′ low (inputs are active-low)","S′ high (inputs are active-high)","Both inputs high at once","Both inputs low at once"],"NAND latches have active-low set and reset, and both low is the forbidden state."],
["10-26","M","A scan flip-flop adds a 2:1 mux in front of D so that, in test mode, it can:",["Shift in a test value from the previous flip-flop in the chain","Run at double speed during manufacturing test","Ignore the clock and hold its value during test","Store two bits, one functional and one for test"],"The scan-enable selects between functional data and the scan input."],
["10-27","E","A flip-flop with a synchronous reset clears its output:",["At the next active clock edge while reset is asserted","Immediately, without waiting for a clock edge","Only once, at power-up","At the first falling edge after reset is released"],"An asynchronous reset acts immediately, independent of the clock."],
["10-28","H","In a two-phase latch design with non-overlapping clocks φ1 and φ2, the non-overlap gap exists to:",["Keep the two latches from being transparent at the same time","Raise the clock frequency by shortening each phase","Save leakage power while both clocks are low","Give the latches time to go metastable and recover"],"Two-phase latch designs clock alternate latches with φ1 and φ2. If both were transparent at once, data could race through two latches in one phase. Leaving a gap where neither clock is high keeps one latch closed while the other opens, even with some clock skew.","vb"],
["10-29","E","A glitch on a latch's D input while the latch is transparent:",["Appears on Q","Is always filtered out","Resets the latch","Is stored only at the falling edge"],"A transparent latch behaves like a buffer from D to Q.","v"],
["10-30","M","Compared with a flip-flop-based design, a latch-based design is generally:",["Smaller and lower power, but harder to time","Easier to design and verify than flip-flop designs","Impossible to synthesize with standard tools","Free of hold constraints, since latches are transparent"],"Transparent phases complicate timing analysis, but time borrowing and smaller cells help fast and low-power designs.","v"],
["10-31","E","How many 2:1 muxes does a positive-edge D flip-flop built from mux latches need?",["2","1","4","3"],"Two mux latches, master and slave, enabled on opposite clock phases.","r"],
["10-32","M","A mux latch computes Q = EN ? D : Q. To make it the master of a positive-edge flip-flop (transparent while CLK is low), connect:",["EN to NOT CLK","EN to CLK","D to NOT CLK","Q to NOT EN"],"The master is open while CLK is low and closes at the rising edge; the slave uses EN = CLK.","r"],
// 11 Timing
["11-01","M","tcq = 50 ps, the slowest logic path is 400 ps, tsu = 30 ps, and there is no skew. What is the maximum clock frequency?",["About 2.08 GHz","2.5 GHz","About 2.27 GHz","2 GHz"],"T = 50 + 400 + 30 = 480 ps."],
["11-02","M","tcq = 80 ps, tpd = 600 ps and tsu = 40 ps. What is the maximum frequency?",["About 1.39 GHz","About 1.47 GHz","1.25 GHz","About 1.67 GHz"],"T = 80 + 600 + 40 = 720 ps."],
["11-03","H","tcq = 30 ps, the shortest logic path is 5 ps, and th = 50 ps. The hold check:",["Fails by 15 ps","Passes with 15 ps to spare","Fails by 45 ps","Depends on the clock period"],"The data arrives at 35 ps but must not arrive before 50 ps."],
["11-04","M","A hold violation can be fixed by lowering the clock frequency.",false,"The hold check involves only one clock edge, so the period does not appear in it. Add delay to the short path instead."],
["11-05","H","The capture flip-flop's clock arrives 40 ps after the launch flip-flop's. This:",["Helps setup and hurts hold","Hurts setup and helps hold","Helps both","Affects neither"],"Positive skew gives data more time before the capture edge, but keeps the hold window open later."],
["11-06","M","At 1 GHz with tcq = 100 ps and tsu = 50 ps, the maximum logic delay is:",["850 ps","1000 ps","950 ps","900 ps"],"1000 − 100 − 50 = 850 ps."],
["11-07","M","Setup timing is signed off at the ___ corner, and hold timing at the ___ corner.",["slow; fast","fast; slow","fast; fast","typical; typical"],"Long paths are longest at the slow corner; short paths are shortest at the fast corner."],
["11-08","M","A 1200 ps logic block with 100 ps of register overhead is split into 4 equal pipeline stages. What is the new clock frequency?",["2.5 GHz","About 0.77 GHz","3.33 GHz","4 GHz"],"Each stage needs 300 + 100 = 400 ps."],
["11-09","E","Pipelining a design generally:",["Raises throughput and increases latency","Lowers both throughput and latency","Raises throughput and lowers latency","Removes register overhead"],"Each item passes through more registers, and each adds tcq + tsu."],
["11-10","H","A shift register's flip-flops have tcq = 50 ps and th = 20 ps, and each capture clock arrives 40 ps late. The hold check:",["Fails by 10 ps","Passes with 30 ps to spare","Passes with 10 ps to spare","Cannot be determined"],"It needs tcq ≥ th + skew, and 50 ≥ 60 is false.","r"],
["11-11","H","A 50 mm wire has 0.08 ns/mm of delay, the clock period is 1 ns, and flip-flop overhead is 0.2 ns. How many retiming flip-flops are needed?",["4","5","3","40"],"Each segment may use 0.8 ns, which covers 10 mm. That is 5 segments, with 4 flip-flops between them.","r"],
["11-12","M","The clock period does not appear in the hold-time constraint.",true,"The hold constraint is tcq + tcd ≥ th + skew."],
["11-13","E","A path reports −30 ps of setup slack. This means:",["It misses timing by 30 ps","It has 30 ps of margin","It is a hold violation","It is a false path"],"Negative slack is a violation."],
["11-14","M","Time borrowing is possible with:",["Latches","Edge-triggered flip-flops","Neither","Only with asynchronous resets"],"Late data passes through a transparent latch, and the next stage simply starts late.","v"],
["11-15","E","Clock jitter:",["Reduces the timing margin available","Lengthens the clock period","Fixes hold violations","Has no effect on setup"],"Unpredictable edge timing effectively shortens the period for setup."],
["11-16","H","tcq = 60 ps, logic delay = 700 ps and tsu = 40 ps. The capture clock arrives 50 ps after the launch clock. What is the minimum clock period?",["750 ps","800 ps","850 ps","700 ps"],"Positive skew helps setup: T ≥ tcq + tpd + tsu − skew = 800 − 50 = 750 ps."],
["11-17","H","On the same path, tcq = 60 ps, the shortest logic is 10 ps, th = 30 ps, and the capture clock is 50 ps late. The hold check:",["Fails by 10 ps","Passes with 40 ps to spare","Passes with 10 ps to spare","Fails by 40 ps"],"It needs tcq + tcd ≥ th + skew, and 70 ≥ 80 fails by 10 ps. Add delay to the short path."],
["11-18","M","A multicycle path constraint tells timing analysis that:",["The path is allowed more than one clock cycle to settle","The path is never used, so it can be ignored","The clock on that path runs at several frequencies","Hold checks on that path can be skipped"],"For example, a result read only every second cycle can be given two cycles."],
["11-19","M","A false path is:",["A path that can never be exercised in real operation","A path that currently has negative slack","Any path that passes through a latch","A path between two flip-flops with no logic"],"Paths between mutually exclusive test and functional modes are typical examples."],
["11-20","H","During a setup check, on-chip variation (OCV) derates make the launch path slower and the capture clock path faster because:",["Parts of one chip vary, and the check must cover the worst combination","The capture clock is always faster than the launch clock","It simplifies the arithmetic of the timing check","It removes clock skew from the analysis entirely"],"Derating applies pessimistic delays to each side of the check."],
["11-21","H","Clock reconvergence pessimism removal (CRPR) corrects timing analysis when:",["Launch and capture clocks share a path segment","The design has no clock tree, so skew is ignored","A path has been marked false by the designer","A flip-flop's hold time is zero and needs no check"],"The pessimism added to the shared segment is given back as credit.","b"],
["11-22","H","Two flip-flops are 60 mm apart. The data wire delay is 0.1 ns/mm, the clock period is 5 ns, and flip-flop overhead is 0.5 ns. With no skew, how many retiming flip-flops are needed between them?",["1","2","0","12"],"Each segment may use 4.5 ns, covering 45 mm. 60 mm needs 2 segments, so 1 flip-flop between them.","r"],
["11-23","H","In a long pipelined link, the clock is routed alongside the data in the same direction, so each downstream flip-flop's clock arrives later. For setup, this:",["Gives each segment extra setup time, but eats into hold margin","Always hurts setup, so more flip-flops are needed","Doubles the number of retiming flip-flops needed","Has no effect, since the skew cancels along the link"],"Positive skew in the data direction extends each segment's setup window and eats into its hold margin.","r"],
["11-24","M","Inserting buffers to fix a hold violation:",["Delays the short path so data changes later","Speeds up the long path so that it meets setup","Changes the clock frequency to widen the hold window","Removes the setup constraint on that path"],"Hold fixes slow the fastest paths, and must not create setup violations on those paths."],
["11-25","M","Through a latch, data can borrow up to about:",["The latch's transparent phase, minus its setup time","Any amount of time, with no limit","No time at all, just like a flip-flop","Two full clock cycles, then it must stop"],"Data may arrive any time while the latch is open, until its setup time before it closes."],
["11-26","E","Setup slack is the required time minus the arrival time. Positive slack means:",["The path meets timing with margin","The path fails","It is a hold violation","The clock is too slow to measure"],"Negative slack is a violation."],
["11-27","M","A 4-bit shift register uses D flip-flops with a positive clock-to-Q delay and zero hold time, and has no skew. Does it shift correctly?",["Yes; any positive tcq meets zero hold","No; it always has a hold violation","Only if tcq exceeds the clock period","Only if latches replace the flip-flops"],"The hold check is tcq ≥ th + skew = 0, which any positive tcq satisfies.","r"],
["11-28","H","In a shift register, the flip-flops have tcq = 40 ps and th = 25 ps, and each later stage's clock arrives 20 ps after the previous stage's. The hold check:",["Fails by 5 ps","Passes with 15 ps to spare","Passes with 5 ps to spare","Fails by 15 ps"],"With no logic between stages, hold needs tcq ≥ th + skew: 40 ≥ 45 fails by 5 ps.","r"],
["11-29","M","A shift register fails hold because of clock skew. Which fix works without touching the clock?",["Add a delay buffer between each pair of flip-flops","Lower the clock frequency","Raise the flip-flops' supply voltage","Remove the flip-flops' reset"],"Hold does not depend on the period, and a faster flip-flop makes hold worse. Delaying the data path fixes it.","r"],
["11-30","M","In SDC, create_clock -period 2.0 [get_ports clk] defines a clock of:",["500 MHz","2 GHz","200 MHz","50 MHz"],"The period is in nanoseconds: 1/2.0 ns = 500 MHz.","b"],
["11-31","M","set_input_delay 0.3 -clock clk on an input port tells timing analysis that:",["The signal arrives 0.3 ns after the edge, leaving less time inside","A buffer adding 0.3 ns is inserted on the input","The clock itself arrives 0.3 ns late at the port","The input no longer needs any timing check"],"Input and output delays model the logic outside the chip so the boundary paths are checked.","b"],
["11-32","M","set_clock_groups -asynchronous between two clocks causes timing tools to:",["Skip timing between them; synchronizers handle the crossing","Check paths between them more strictly than others","Merge the two clocks into one for analysis","Stop both clocks while the other is active"],"Unrelated clocks cannot be timed meaningfully; the crossing must be handled by CDC design and checked by CDC tools.","b"],
["11-33","H","A path has a setup multicycle of 2. To keep the hold check at the usual edge, you also set:",["A hold multicycle of 1","A hold multicycle of 2","Nothing; hold moves correctly by itself","A false path on hold"],"By default, moving the setup edge also moves the hold edge forward; the hold multicycle of 1 moves it back.","b"],
// 12 Counters, dividers and FSMs
["12-01","M","A binary counter's clock period is 2 ns, and its most significant bit has a 128 ns period. How many flip-flops does it have?",["6","64","7","5"],"The MSB period is 2ⁿ × the clock period, so 2ⁿ = 64.","r"],
["12-02","E","A 3-bit ripple counter is clocked at 16 MHz. The frequency of its MSB is:",["2 MHz","8 MHz","4 MHz","5.33 MHz"],"16 MHz / 2³ = 2 MHz."],
["12-03","E","How many flip-flops does a mod-10 counter need?",["4","10","3","5"],"⌈log₂ 10⌉ = 4."],
["12-04","H","A divide-by-3 clock with a 50% duty cycle requires:",["Using both clock edges","Only rising edges","A PLL","An asynchronous reset"],"Make a signal that is high 1 of every 3 cycles, delay it half a cycle with a falling-edge flip-flop, and OR the two.","r"],
["12-05","H","Which gives a glitch-free divide-by-2 output with a 25% duty cycle? (Q is the divide-by-2 output.)",["Q AND NOT CLK","Q AND CLK","Q OR NOT CLK","Q XOR CLK"],"Q changes just after each rising edge. NOT CLK has already fallen by then, so there is no glitch; Q AND CLK glitches for tcq.","r"],
["12-06","M","A 12-state FSM needs how many flip-flops with binary encoding, and with one-hot encoding?",["4 and 12","12 and 4","3 and 12","4 and 4"],"⌈log₂ 12⌉ = 4; one-hot uses one flip-flop per state."],
["12-07","M","Which kind of FSM usually needs more states for the same sequence detector?",["Moore","Mealy","They always need the same number","It depends on the clock"],"Moore outputs are tied to states, so a 'just matched' state is extra."],
["12-08","M","A Mealy machine that detects 1011 with overlap needs how many states?",["4","5","3","8"],"One state per prefix length, 0 through 3."],
["12-09","H","An overlapping 1011 detector watches the input 1011011. How many matches does it report?",["2","1","3","0"],"Matches end at bits 4 and 7; the last 1 of the first match begins the second."],
["12-10","H","A traffic light stays green 20 s, yellow 4 s and red 24 s. The clock period is 2 s and the FSM counts time itself. What is the minimum number of flip-flops?",["5","6","3","24"],"48 s / 2 s = 24 states, and ⌈log₂ 24⌉ = 5.","r"],
["12-11","M","A 4-flip-flop Johnson counter has how many states?",["8","4","16","15"],"A Johnson counter has 2N states."],
["12-12","M","A maximal-length 4-bit LFSR cycles through how many states?",["15","16","8","4"],"2ⁿ − 1: every state except all zeros."],
["12-13","M","A divide-by-6 clock with a 50% duty cycle can be built by:",["Toggling the output every 3 clock cycles","Toggling the output every cycle","Holding a 6-state output high for 1 cycle","ANDing three clocks together"],"The output is then high for 3 cycles and low for 3.","r"],
["12-14","E","In a Mealy machine, the outputs depend only on the current state.",false,"That describes a Moore machine. Mealy outputs depend on the state and the current inputs."],
["12-15","H","A divide-by-5 clock with a 50% duty cycle can be built by:",["Combining a rising-edge 2-of-5 waveform with a half-cycle-delayed copy","Toggling the output every 5 cycles with a rising-edge counter","Using only rising-edge flip-flops and a 5-state one-hot ring","ANDing the clock with a divide-by-5 counter's top bit"],"Odd division needs both edges to put a transition halfway through a cycle. The output is then high for 2.5 cycles.","r"],
["12-16","M","Using only rising-edge flip-flops, a divide-by-3 counter can produce an output with a duty cycle of:",["33% or 67%, not 50%","Exactly 50%","25% or 75%","Any duty cycle you choose"],"The output is high for 1 or 2 of every 3 cycles. A 50% duty cycle needs the falling edge too.","r"],
["12-17","E","A divide-by-4 clock with a 50% duty cycle can be made from:",["Two toggle flip-flops in series","One toggle flip-flop","Only a falling-edge flip-flop","Only a PLL"],"Each toggle stage halves the frequency.","r"],
["12-18","M","Gating a clock with ordinary logic, such as an AND gate with a changing enable, risks:",["Glitches and runt pulses on the clock","Lower power with no downside","A higher frequency","Eliminating metastability"],"Use integrated clock-gating cells or glitch-free clock switches.","r"],
["12-19","M","FPGA designs often use one-hot FSM encoding because:",["Next-state logic is simple and fast, and FPGAs have plenty of flip-flops","It uses the fewest flip-flops of any encoding","It can never reach an illegal state, even after upsets","It needs no reset, since one bit is always set"],"Binary encoding uses fewer flip-flops but more decoding logic."],
["12-20","E","A 4-bit ring counter (one circulating 1) has how many states?",["4","8","16","15"],"A ring counter has N states; a Johnson counter has 2N."],
["12-21","M","A ripple counter's outputs can briefly show wrong values when it counts from 0111 to 1000 because:",["Each flip-flop clocks the next, so the bits change in turn","It counts in Gray code, which reorders the bits","Its clock is too slow for the flip-flops to settle","All its flip-flops share one clock, which skews them"],"A synchronous counter changes all its bits on the same edge."],
["12-22","H","A non-overlapping detector for 101 sees the input 10101. How many matches does it report?",["1","2","0","3"],"After the first 101 it starts over, so the remaining 01 cannot complete a second match. An overlapping detector would report 2."],
["12-23","M","A traffic light stays green 30 s, yellow 5 s and red 25 s. The clock period is 5 s, and the FSM counts time with its own states. What is the minimum number of flip-flops?",["4","6","3","12"],"(30 + 5 + 25)/5 = 12 states, and ⌈log₂ 12⌉ = 4.","r"],
["12-24","M","A binary counter's clock period is 4 ns, and its most significant bit has a 256 ns period. How many flip-flops does it have?",["6","7","8","64"],"256/4 = 64 = 2⁶.","r"],
["12-25","M","A traffic light stays green 45 s, yellow 5 s and red 50 s. The clock period is 5 s and the FSM counts time with its own states. What is the minimum number of flip-flops?",["5","20","4","100"],"(45 + 5 + 50)/5 = 20 states, and ⌈log₂ 20⌉ = 5.","r"],
["12-26","M","A traffic-light controller uses a separate timer, so its FSM has just three states: green, yellow and red. With binary encoding it needs:",["2 flip-flops","3 flip-flops","1 flip-flop","8 flip-flops"],"⌈log₂ 3⌉ = 2. One-hot encoding would use 3.","r"],
// 13 Delay
["13-01","M","A gate with 5 kΩ of effective resistance drives 20 fF. Its delay is about:",["69 ps","100 ps","35 ps","4 ps"],"0.69 × 5 kΩ × 20 fF ≈ 69 ps."],
["13-02","H","A unit inverter must drive 64 times its own input capacitance. A tapered buffer chain should have about:",["3 stages (sizes 1, 4, 16)","6 stages (sizes 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32)","1 stage (one big inverter)","2 stages (sizes 1, 8)"],"The best ratio is about 4 per stage, and log₄ 64 = 3."],
["13-03","M","The logical effort of a 2-input NAND (with PMOS twice the NMOS width) is:",["4/3","1","5/3","2"],"A NOR2 is 5/3: worse, because of its series PMOS."],
["13-04","M","An unbuffered wire's length triples. Its delay grows by about:",["9×","3×","6×","27×"],"Wire delay ∝ RC ∝ L²."],
["13-05","M","Inserting repeaters along a long wire makes its total delay grow:",["Linearly with length","With the square of length","Exponentially","Not at all"],"Each short segment has a small delay, and the segments add."],
["13-06","H","An RC ladder has R1 = R2 = 1 kΩ and C1 = C2 = 10 fF (C1 after R1, C2 at the end). What is the Elmore delay to the end?",["30 ps","20 ps","40 ps","10 ps"],"R1(C1 + C2) + R2·C2 = 20 ps + 10 ps."],
["13-07","M","Making a gate twice as wide:",["Halves its resistance but doubles its input load","Is free speed, with no cost anywhere else","Doubles its resistance and halves its load","Halves its input capacitance and its delay"],"The delay moves backward to the previous stage."],
["13-08","H","In a series NMOS stack, the latest-arriving input should drive the transistor:",["Nearest the output","Nearest ground","In the middle","It makes no difference"],"By then the other transistors are on and the internal nodes are already discharged."],
["13-09","E","The FO4 delay is:",["An inverter's delay driving four copies of itself","Four gate delays in a row, end to end","The delay of a single 4-input gate","The clock period divided by four"],"It is a standard yardstick for comparing process speed."],
["13-10","E","Raising the supply voltage makes gates:",["Faster, at the cost of power","Slower","No different in speed","Leak less"],"More overdrive means more drive current."],
["13-11","M","A path has a total effort of F = 64 over 3 stages. The best effort per stage is:",["4","21.3","8","64"],"f = F^(1/N) = 64^(1/3) = 4."],
["13-12","E","Gate delay is fundamentally the time to charge or discharge capacitance through transistor resistance.",true,"That is why t ≈ 0.69RC."],
["13-13","M","With μn = 2μp, the logical effort of a 3-input NAND is:",["5/3","4/3","7/3","1"],"Each input drives an NMOS of width 3 and a PMOS of width 2, so 5 units against the inverter's 3."],
["13-14","M","With μn = 2μp, the logical effort of a 2-input NOR is:",["5/3","4/3","2","1"],"Each input drives an NMOS of width 1 and a PMOS of width 4, so 5 units against the inverter's 3."],
["13-15","H","A path has logical effort G = 2, electrical effort H = 8 and branching effort B = 2. Its path effort F is:",["32","12","16","4"],"F = G·B·H = 2 × 2 × 8 = 32."],
["13-16","M","Gate delay is modelled as d = g·h + p. An inverter has g = 1 and p = 1. Driving four copies of itself (h = 4), its delay is:",["5 units","4 units","1 unit","8 units"],"d = 1 × 4 + 1 = 5. This is the FO4 delay."],
["13-17","M","Lowering VDD close to Vt makes gate delay:",["Rise sharply","Fall","Stay the same","Drop to zero"],"Drive current depends on the overdrive VDD − Vt, which shrinks."],
["13-18","M","Inserting repeaters along a long wire helps mainly because it:",["Turns quadratic wire delay into linear delay","Removes the wire's capacitance from the path","Eliminates crosstalk from neighbouring wires","Adds no delay of its own along the wire"],"Each short segment has a small RC delay, plus a fixed repeater delay."],
["13-19","E","A small gate must drive a very large load. The fastest approach is usually:",["A chain of increasingly larger buffers","Making the small gate's inputs slower","Routing a longer wire to spread the load","Lowering VDD to reduce the load's charge"],"Tapering keeps each stage's effort near 4."],
["13-20","M","Wire delay matters more relative to gate delay at advanced nodes because:",["Thin wires gain resistance per length, while transistors keep getting faster","Wires only got longer; their cross-section stayed the same","Gates stopped getting faster after about 2005","Copper replaced aluminum, which raised resistance"],"Narrow wires also suffer extra scattering at surfaces and grain boundaries."],
// 14 Power
["14-01","M","A chip's clock frequency and supply voltage both double. Its dynamic power becomes:",["8×","4×","2×","16×"],"P = αCV²f, so 2 × 2² = 8.","o"],
["14-02","H","Supply voltage rises 10% and frequency rises 20%. Dynamic power rises by about:",["45%","30%","32%","21%"],"1.1² × 1.2 = 1.452.","r"],
["14-03","H","A chip moves from frequency f at voltage 2V to frequency 4f at voltage 3V. Dynamic power changes by a factor of:",["9","6","12","4.5"],"4 × (3/2)² = 9. The voltage factor is 1.5, not 3.","r"],
["14-04","M","Voltage and frequency both drop by 10%. How much dynamic power remains?",["About 73%","90%","81%","About 66%"],"0.9³ = 0.729.","v"],
["14-05","M","α = 0.2, C = 1 nF, VDD = 1.0 V and f = 1 GHz. The dynamic power is:",["0.2 W","2 W","0.1 W","1 W"],"0.2 × 10⁻⁹ × 1² × 10⁹ = 0.2 W.","v"],
["14-06","H","A processor runs a program twice as fast at the same voltage. The energy the program uses:",["Stays the same","Doubles","Halves","Quadruples"],"Energy per operation is αCV², with no f in it. Power doubles, but the work finishes in half the time.","v"],
["14-07","M","Clock gating mainly reduces ___ power, and power gating mainly reduces ___ power.",["dynamic; leakage","leakage; dynamic","short-circuit; dynamic","leakage; leakage"],"Stopping the clock stops switching; cutting the supply stops leakage."],
["14-08","E","The activity factor α of a clock net is:",["1","0.5","0.25","0"],"A clock rises every single cycle."],
["14-09","E","As temperature rises, leakage power:",["Increases strongly","Decreases","Stays constant","Drops to zero"],"Subthreshold leakage grows exponentially with temperature."],
["14-10","H","One core running at f and 1.0 V is replaced by two cores at f/2 and 0.6 V each. What is the power ratio, ignoring overhead?",["0.36","0.6","0.72","1.2"],"2 × 0.6² × 0.5 = 0.36."],
["14-11","M","Why not gate a clock by simply ANDing it with an enable signal?",["An enable that changes while the clock is high creates glitches","AND gates are too slow to pass a clock edge cleanly","It increases leakage throughout the clock tree","It cannot actually stop the clock from toggling"],"An integrated clock-gating cell latches the enable while the clock is low."],
["14-12","M","Short-circuit power increases when:",["Input transitions are slow","Inputs switch quickly","The supply voltage is lower","The chip is idle"],"Both transistors are partly on for longer during a slow transition."],
["14-13","H","When a capacitor charges from 0 to VDD, half the energy drawn from the supply ends up stored in it.",true,"CV² comes from the supply: ½CV² is stored and ½CV² is burned in the PMOS."],
["14-14","E","The single most effective knob for cutting dynamic power is:",["Lowering the supply voltage","Lowering the frequency","Lowering the capacitance","Adding decoupling capacitors"],"Dynamic power scales with V²."],
["14-15","M","Dennard scaling broke down around 2005 mainly because:",["Supply voltage could no longer drop without leakage exploding","Transistors stopped shrinking, so density stopped rising","Copper wires replaced aluminum and slowed every chip","Clock trees got too small to distribute fast clocks"],"Power density began to rise, so clock frequencies plateaued and designs went parallel."],
["14-16","E","Supply voltage halves and frequency stays the same. Dynamic power becomes:",["1/4","1/2","1/8","2×"],"P ∝ V², and (1/2)² = 1/4.","v"],
["14-17","E","Frequency doubles and voltage stays the same. Dynamic power becomes:",["2×","4×","8×","Unchanged"],"P ∝ f.","v"],
["14-18","E","Supply voltage doubles and frequency stays the same. Dynamic power becomes:",["4×","2×","8×","16×"],"P ∝ V².","v"],
["14-19","H","Clock frequency and supply voltage both double. The average supply current for dynamic power becomes:",["4×","8×","2×","16×"],"I = P/V = 8/2 = 4. Equivalently, I = αCVf, and both V and f double.","v"],
["14-20","H","Clock frequency and supply voltage both double. The energy per clock cycle, αCV², becomes:",["4×","8×","2×","Unchanged"],"Energy per cycle has no f in it; only V² matters. Power is 8× because there are also twice as many cycles per second.","v"],
["14-21","M","Frequency triples and voltage drops to 0.8×. Dynamic power becomes about:",["1.92×","2.4×","3×","0.64×"],"3 × 0.8² = 1.92.","v"],
["14-22","H","A chip burns 100 W: 70 W dynamic and 30 W leakage. Frequency doubles at the same voltage and temperature. Total power becomes:",["170 W","200 W","140 W","130 W"],"Only dynamic power scales with f: 140 + 30 = 170 W.","v"],
["14-23","M","Frequency doubles while the activity factor halves (half as many nodes switch each cycle). Dynamic power:",["Stays the same","Doubles","Halves","Quadruples"],"P ∝ αf, and 0.5 × 2 = 1.","v"],
["14-24","H","Frequency and voltage both double. Ignoring any temperature change, leakage power:",["Rises by more than 2×","Stays the same","Doubles exactly","Rises 8×, like dynamic power"],"Leakage power is V × I_leak. V doubles, and I_leak also rises with V (for example through DIBL). Frequency does not enter.","vb"],
["14-25","H","Standard cells burn 40 W dynamic and 10 W leakage when active. 10% of the cells are always on; the rest are power gated with 80% efficiency (gating removes 80% of their leakage). With the gated cells off, the standard cells burn:",["6.8 W","5 W","4 W","14 W"],"Always-on cells keep 4 W dynamic and 1 W leakage. Gated cells keep 9 W × 20% = 1.8 W of leakage. 4 + 1 + 1.8 = 6.8 W.","r"],
["14-26","M","RAMs burn 20 W dynamic and 6 W leakage. All of them are power gated with 90% efficiency. While gated, they burn:",["0.6 W","2.6 W","6 W","0 W"],"Gated RAMs do no switching, and 10% of their leakage remains: 0.6 W.","r"],
["14-27","H","Standard cells burn 30 W dynamic and 20 W leakage. 20% are always on; the rest are power gated with 75% efficiency. With the gated cells off, the standard cells burn:",["14 W","8 W","6 W","20 W"],"Leakage: 20 × [0.2 + 0.8 × 0.25] = 8 W. Dynamic: 0.2 × 30 = 6 W. Total 14 W.","r"],
["14-28","M","A clock network switches 500 pF at 0.8 V and 2 GHz. Its power is:",["640 mW","320 mW","1.28 W","800 mW"],"A clock has α = 1: 500 pF × 0.64 V² × 2 GHz = 0.64 W."],
["14-29","M","A 32-bit bus carries random data. On average, what fraction of its wires change value each cycle?",["About half","All of them","None","One in 32"],"Each random bit differs from the previous one with probability 1/2. Counting only rising transitions, the activity factor is 0.25."],
["14-30","E","A level shifter is needed when a signal:",["Crosses between blocks running at different supply voltages","Crosses between two different clock domains","Travels down a long wire that needs repeaters","Drives the reset input of a flip-flop"],"A low-voltage 1 may not turn the higher-voltage block's PMOS fully off."],
["14-31","M","Isolation cells on the outputs of a power-gated block:",["Clamp its outputs to known values while it is off","Speed up its outputs when it powers back on","Shrink its area by sharing output drivers","Generate its clock while the main supply is off"],"Without isolation, floating outputs cause static current and wrong logic downstream."],
["14-32","M","Retention flip-flops in a power-gated block:",["Keep their state on a small always-on supply","Store two bits each, one for each power state","Run at a higher frequency to save state quickly","Never lose power, because they ignore the gating"],"The block's state can then be restored quickly on wake-up."],
["14-33","M","DVFS (dynamic voltage and frequency scaling) saves energy mainly because:",["A lower frequency allows a lower voltage, and power falls with V²f","Frequency alone sets the energy needed per task","It removes leakage entirely whenever it is active","It raises the activity factor, finishing work sooner"],"Each operation at a lower voltage costs less energy."],
["14-34","M","Glitch power is wasted by:",["Spurious transitions from unequal path delays","The clock tree alone, on every cycle","Leakage while the block is idle","Power-gating switches turning on and off"],"Balancing path delays, or pipelining, reduces it."],
["14-35","M","Which statement about power and energy is correct?",["Halving frequency halves power, but a fixed task's energy stays about the same","Halving frequency halves the energy a fixed task needs","Power and energy are the same thing, measured differently","Lowering voltage raises the energy of each operation"],"Energy per operation is αCV²; only the voltage changes it (ignoring leakage over the longer run time).","v"],
["14-36","H","A process shrink scales C to 0.7×, V to 0.7× and f to 1/0.7×. Dynamic power per circuit becomes about:",["0.49×","0.7×","1×","0.34×"],"0.7 × 0.7² / 0.7 = 0.49. The circuit also occupies half the area, so power density stays constant: Dennard scaling."],
["14-37","E","A block leaks 10 W. It is power gated with 95% efficiency. How much leakage remains while it is gated?",["0.5 W","9.5 W","0 W","5 W"],"Gating removes 95% of the leakage, leaving 5% of 10 W.","r"],
["14-38","H","A level shifter from a 0.8 V domain to 1.8 V uses two cross-coupled PMOS on the 1.8 V supply and two NMOS pull-downs driven by the input and its complement. Why are the NMOS made much stronger than the PMOS?",["Their gates see only 0.8 V, yet they must overpower PMOS driven by the full 1.8 V","So the circuit works without a 1.8 V supply","To make the output switch at 0.4 V","Because PMOS transistors cannot be made small"],"A plain inverter on 1.8 V cannot take a 0.8 V input: a 0.8 V high leaves its PMOS partly on, so it burns static current and may not switch. In the cross-coupled shifter, the NMOS on the side going low must pull its node down against a PMOS that is still fully on, with only 0.8 V of gate drive. Once that node falls, the other PMOS turns on, the latch completes, and no static current flows.","b"],
["14-39","H","In a dual active bridge converter, zero-voltage switching (ZVS) means a switch turns on:",["After current in the transformer's leakage inductance has discharged its output capacitance to 0 V","Only when the load current is zero","Only when the input voltage is zero","With its gate driven at 0 V"],"During the dead time, the inductor current flows into the switch's output capacitance, pulls its voltage to zero and then flows through its body diode. Turning the switch on then wastes none of the ½CV² energy that hard switching dumps into the channel. A DAB gets this from its leakage inductance when the load is heavy enough to store enough energy there; at light load it can lose ZVS.","b"],
// 15 PVT and temperature
["15-01","M","As temperature increases, carrier mobility ___ and metal wire resistance ___.",["decreases; increases","increases; increases","increases; decreases","decreases; decreases"],"Both come from more lattice scattering, and both slow the circuit down.","o"],
["15-02","M","As temperature rises, a MOSFET's threshold voltage:",["Decreases","Increases","Stays fixed","Becomes negative"],"Roughly on the order of 1 mV per °C. This pushes transistors toward being faster.","v"],
["15-03","H","Temperature inversion means that at low supply voltage:",["Circuits can be faster when hot","Circuits stop working when cold","Wires get faster when hot","Leakage falls when hot"],"With a small overdrive, the drop in Vt outweighs the loss of mobility.","v"],
["15-04","H","Every chip is slowest at its highest operating temperature.",false,"Not for modern low-voltage designs, because of temperature inversion. Wires, however, are always slower when hot.","v"],
["15-05","H","A copper wire measures 100 Ω at 25 °C. At 85 °C it is about:",["123 Ω","100 Ω","160 Ω","106 Ω"],"100 × (1 + 0.0039 × 60) ≈ 123 Ω.","v"],
["15-06","M","The FS process corner means:",["Fast NMOS, slow PMOS","Fast and slow wires","Fast setup, slow hold","Full speed"],"Skewed corners stress balanced circuits such as inverter thresholds and SRAM margins."],
["15-07","M","Worst-case leakage is checked at:",["Fast process, high voltage and high temperature","Slow process, low voltage and low temperature","Typical everything","Slow process and high temperature"],"Fast transistors and heat both raise leakage."],
["15-08","M","A 0.80 V supply suffers 5% IR drop. The transistors see:",["0.76 V, and run slower","0.84 V, and run faster","0.76 V, and run faster","0.80 V"],"Less overdrive means less drive current."],
["15-09","E","Monte Carlo simulation is mainly used to estimate the effect of:",["Random local variation between transistors","Changes in clock frequency across a design","Supply-voltage changes from board to board","Differences in wire length between blocks"],"Simulating many randomly varied copies reveals the spread."],
["15-10","E","Metal wires are always slower when hot.",true,"Wire resistance only increases with temperature.","v"],
["15-11","E","Sorting finished chips by how fast they run is called:",["Binning","Dicing","Scanning","Tape-out"],"Faster parts become higher-end products."],
["15-12","M","Designers add an aging guardband because, over years of use:",["Threshold voltages drift up over time, slowing transistors","Wires get shorter as the metal migrates","The clock frequency drifts upward by itself","Leakage gradually disappears, changing timing"],"The chip must still meet its frequency at end of life."],
["15-13","M","At a high supply voltage, the slowest corner for setup checks is:",["Slow process, low voltage, high temperature","Slow process, low voltage, low temperature","Fast process, high voltage, low temperature","Typical everything"],"At high VDD, mobility loss dominates, so hot is slow.","v"],
["15-14","H","At a low supply voltage, with temperature inversion, the slowest corner for setup checks is:",["Slow process, low voltage, low temperature","Slow process, low voltage, high temperature","Fast process, high voltage, high temperature","Typical, nominal, 25 °C"],"Under inversion, the higher Vt when cold dominates, so cells are slowest when cold.","v"],
["15-15","M","As temperature rises, which two effects compete in a transistor's drive current?",["Falling mobility and falling threshold voltage","Rising mobility and rising threshold voltage","Rising wire resistance and falling capacitance","Rising oxide thickness and shrinking channel length"],"Which one wins depends on the overdrive, VGS − Vt.","v"],
["15-16","M","Under temperature inversion, wires also get faster when hot.",false,"Inversion is a transistor effect. Wire resistance always rises with temperature.","v"],
["15-17","M","A path is dominated by long wires rather than gates. As temperature rises, its delay:",["Increases, because wire resistance rises","Decreases, because of temperature inversion","Stays exactly constant","Drops to zero"],"Inversion can speed up transistors at low voltage, but wire-dominated paths still slow down when hot.","v"],
["15-18","M","Hold checks look for the fastest data paths, typically at:",["Fast process, high voltage, fastest temperature","Slow process, low voltage, hottest temperature","The typical corner only, at 25 °C","Slow process at the slowest temperature"],"Hold fails when data arrives too early.","v"],
["15-19","M","Copper's resistivity rises about 0.4% per °C. From 25 °C to 105 °C, a wire's resistance rises by about:",["32%","4%","80%","8%"],"0.004 × 80 = 0.32.","v"],
["15-20","M","DRAM must be refreshed more often when hot because:",["Leakage drains the storage capacitor faster when hot","The storage capacitor grows when hot","Mobility increases, so reads disturb cells","Wires get faster, so refresh can keep up"],"Many DRAMs halve their refresh interval above 85 °C.","v"],
["15-21","M","A sudden current step makes the on-die supply sag briefly (a droop). This matters mainly because:",["The lower voltage slows transistors, so paths can fail timing","It raises the clock frequency during the droop","It cools the chip, which shifts the timing","It fixes hold violations, which then return"],"Decoupling capacitance and adaptive clocking guard against it."],
["15-22","M","Static IR drop differs from dynamic IR drop in that static IR drop:",["Comes from average current, while dynamic drop comes from sudden bursts","Only happens at power-up, while dynamic drop is constant","Is caused by crosstalk, while dynamic drop is from current","Only affects clock wires, while dynamic drop affects logic"],"Both reduce the voltage that transistors see."],
["15-23","H","Hot carrier injection (HCI), an aging mechanism, is traditionally worst at:",["Low temperature and high drain voltage","High temperature and low voltage","High temperature in PMOS only","Power-off"],"When cold, carriers scatter less and gain more energy before hitting the oxide. NBTI, in contrast, is worse when hot."],
["15-24","E","The TT, FF and SS process corners describe:",["Transistor speed from manufacturing variation","Operating temperatures (typical, hot, cold)","Supply voltages (typical, high, low)","Clock frequencies (target, fast, slow)"],"The skewed corners FS and SF pair a fast NMOS with a slow PMOS, and vice versa."],
["15-25","M","A GPU's thermal sensor reads 105 °C under load. Which gets worse as a direct result?",["Leakage power and electromigration lifetime","Nothing; only wires are affected","Mobility improves","Leakage falls"],"Hot silicon leaks more, and electromigration accelerates exponentially with temperature.","v"],
// 16 Wires and links
["16-01","M","A wire is 200 μm long and 0.1 μm wide, on a layer with 0.1 Ω per square. Its resistance is:",["200 Ω","20 Ω","2000 Ω","2 Ω"],"2000 squares × 0.1 Ω = 200 Ω."],
["16-02","H","Cc = 5 fF, Cg = 15 fF and VDD = 0.8 V. What is the worst-case crosstalk glitch on a quiet victim?",["0.2 V","0.27 V","0.6 V","0.05 V"],"ΔV ≈ VDD × Cc/(Cc + Cg) = 0.8 × 5/20 = 0.2 V."],
["16-03","M","A neighbouring wire switching in the opposite direction makes the victim:",["Slower (coupling acts like about 2Cc)","Faster (coupling acts like about 0)","Unchanged (coupling only adds glitches)","Slower only if both wires are long"],"Switching in the same direction makes it faster."],
["16-04","H","Net A can switch 1.0–1.6 ns after the clock, and adjacent net B 1.4–2.2 ns. Crosstalk between them is possible during:",["1.4–1.6 ns","1.0–2.2 ns","1.6–2.2 ns","Never"],"Only where the two switching windows overlap.","r"],
["16-05","E","Which reduces crosstalk between two sensitive wires?",["A grounded shield wire between them","Moving them closer together","Making them thinner","Only lowering the clock frequency"],"Spacing, different layers, stronger drivers and buffers also help."],
["16-06","E","The thick top metal layers are typically used for:",["Power, ground, clock and long global wires","The shortest local connections","Transistor gates","Dummy fill only"],"Thick, wide wires have much lower resistance."],
["16-07","M","A 32-bit DDR bus with a 1.6 GHz clock has a bandwidth of:",["12.8 GB/s","6.4 GB/s","51.2 GB/s","102.4 GB/s"],"2 transfers per cycle × 1.6 GHz × 32 bits / 8 = 12.8 GB/s."],
["16-08","H","A PCIe link with 8 lanes at 8 GT/s and 128b/130b encoding carries about:",["7.9 GB/s per direction","8 GB/s per direction","64 GB/s per direction","6.4 GB/s per direction"],"8 × 8 × 128/130 / 8 ≈ 7.9 GB/s."],
["16-09","E","8b/10b encoding has an efficiency of:",["80%","98.5%","90%","50%"],"Every 8 data bits are sent as 10."],
["16-10","H","A 202-byte read starts at address 0x31121133. Replies carry at most 64 bytes and may not cross 64-byte boundaries. How many replies?",["4","3","5","6"],"The offset is 0x33 = 51, so the first reply carries 13 bytes, then 64, 64 and 61.","r"],
["16-11","H","A 100-byte read starts at 0x1030, with 64-byte payloads that may not cross 64-byte boundaries. How many packets?",["3","2","4","1"],"The offset is 48, so: 16 bytes, then 64, then 20.","r"],
["16-12","M","Differential signaling resists noise because:",["The receiver looks only at the difference, so shared noise cancels","It uses higher voltages that rise above the noise","It uses a single wire with no return path","It needs no termination, so reflections vanish"],"Common-mode noise cancels, so small swings work at high speed."],
["16-13","M","An HBM stack with a 1024-bit interface at 6.4 Gb/s per pin provides about:",["819 GB/s","6.4 GB/s","102 GB/s","6.5 TB/s"],"1024 × 6.4 / 8 ≈ 819 GB/s."],
["16-14","E","Unbuffered on-chip wire delay grows with the square of the wire's length.",true,"Both R and C grow with length, so RC grows with length squared."],
["16-15","M","An aggressor wire switches in the same direction as the victim, at the same time. The effective coupling capacitance is about:",["0","2Cc","Cc","4Cc"],"No voltage changes across the coupling capacitor, so it draws no charge. Opposite switching gives about 2Cc. This is the Miller effect."],
["16-16","H","Net A can switch 0.5–1.2 ns after the clock, and net B 1.3–1.8 ns. Can switching on one add crosstalk delay to the other?",["No; the windows do not overlap","Yes, from 0.5 to 1.8 ns","Yes, from 1.2 to 1.3 ns","Yes, whenever both nets are long"],"Timing windows that never overlap cannot interact for delay. Glitches on a quiet victim are a separate check.","r"],
["16-17","H","A 256-byte read starts at address 0x10000020. Replies carry at most 64 bytes and may not cross 64-byte boundaries. How many replies?",["5","4","6","3"],"The offset is 0x20 = 32, so the first reply carries 32 bytes, then three of 64 (192), then the last 32.","r"],
["16-18","E","A 128-byte read starts on a 64-byte boundary, with 64-byte maximum payloads. How many replies?",["2","3","1","4"],"Aligned, it is exactly two full payloads.","r"],
["16-19","M","Narrow copper wires at advanced nodes have higher resistivity than bulk copper mainly because:",["Electrons scatter off surfaces and grain boundaries","Copper turns into a semiconductor when narrow","Narrow wires run colder, which raises resistance","Narrow wires are always longer than wide ones"],"This is one reason metals such as cobalt and ruthenium are being explored."],
["16-20","M","Vias between metal layers matter for timing and reliability because:",["Each adds resistance and can fail by electromigration","They are perfect conductors with no resistance","They store charge that delays every signal","They exist only in the top metal layer"],"Designers double up vias on critical and high-current nets."],
["16-21","M","A 16-lane PCIe link at 32 GT/s with 128b/130b encoding carries about how much per direction?",["63 GB/s","64 GB/s","512 GB/s","32 GB/s"],"16 × 32 Gb/s × 128/130 / 8 ≈ 63 GB/s."],
["16-22","E","Running grounded shield wires on both sides of a clock wire mainly:",["Reduces coupling noise and makes delay predictable","Speeds up the clock source driving the wire","Lowers the clock wire's own resistance","Removes the need for a clock tree entirely"],"The cost is extra routing tracks."],
["16-23","M","Net A switches 2.0–3.5 ns after the clock, and its neighbour, net B, 3.0–4.2 ns after the clock. During which window can A's switching add crosstalk delay to B?",["3.0–3.5 ns","2.0–4.2 ns","3.5–4.2 ns","Never; they do not overlap"],"Only the overlap of the two switching windows matters.","r"],
["16-24","H","Net A's edge reaches a shared stretch of wire 1.0–1.4 ns after the clock (driver delay plus wire delay). Net B switches 1.2–1.5 ns after the clock there. The crosstalk window is:",["1.2–1.4 ns","1.0–1.5 ns","0.8–1.5 ns","1.4–1.5 ns"],"Intersect the arrival windows where the wires run side by side: 1.2 to 1.4 ns.","r"],
["16-25","M","An eye diagram that is closing (shrinking in height and width) indicates:",["Growing noise, jitter or interference between bits","A perfectly clean signal with extra margin","A lower supply voltage and nothing else","Too much termination at the receiver"],"The eye overlays every bit period; the receiver needs an open region to sample correctly."],
["16-26","M","A chip draws a 50 A current step and its supply may move by at most 25 mV. The power delivery network's target impedance is:",["0.5 mΩ","2 Ω","50 mΩ","1.25 mΩ"],"Z_target = ΔV/ΔI = 0.025/50 = 0.5 mΩ, across the frequency range of the current step."],
["16-27","M","A via stub on a high-speed board trace mainly causes:",["Reflections that degrade the signal","Lower DC resistance on the net","Better shielding from neighbours","Less crosstalk between layers"],"The unused via length acts as a small open transmission line; back-drilling removes it."],
["16-28","M","A clock-and-data-recovery (CDR) circuit in a SerDes receiver:",["Recovers the sampling clock from the data's own edges","Generates the data that the transmitter sends","Adds error-correcting codes to each packet","Stores received packets until software reads them"],"No separate clock wire is sent; the receiver locks onto the data edges, which is why line codes guarantee transitions."],
["16-29","E","Board traces for high-speed signals are designed with a controlled impedance (for example 50 Ω single-ended or 85–100 Ω differential) to:",["Match drivers and terminations, avoiding reflections","Reduce the board's weight and its cost","Lower the chip's leakage current","Make every high-speed trace shorter"],"Trace width, dielectric thickness and material set the impedance."],
["16-30","M","Pre-emphasis or equalization on a long link compensates for:",["Frequency-dependent loss that smears fast edges","A supply voltage that is set too high","Clock skew between flip-flops inside the chip","Antenna effects during board manufacture"],"Boosting high frequencies (or attenuating low ones) reopens the eye."],
["16-31", "M", "A 50 Ω trace drives a 75 Ω load. The reflection coefficient at the load is:", ["0.2", "0.5", "−0.2", "0.33"], "Γ = (Z_L − Z₀)/(Z_L + Z₀) = (75 − 50)/(75 + 50) = 0.2, so 20% of the arriving voltage reflects back toward the driver."],
["16-32", "E", "At the open (unterminated) end of a transmission line, the reflection coefficient is:", ["+1: the whole wave reflects with the same polarity", "0: nothing reflects", "−1: the wave reflects inverted", "0.5: half of it reflects"], "With Z_L infinite, Γ = (∞ − Z₀)/(∞ + Z₀) = 1. A short gives −1, and a matched load gives 0."],
["16-33", "E", "A board trace's characteristic impedance is set by:", ["Its width, its height above the ground plane and the board material", "Its length", "The signal's frequency alone", "The load at its far end"], "Z₀ depends on the cross-section of the line and the dielectric, not on its length. That is why boards are built to an impedance target such as 50 Ω."],
["16-34", "M", "A 0.8 V rail may move 3% when the chip's current steps by 60 A. The power network's target impedance is:", ["0.4 mΩ", "13 mΩ", "2.5 mΩ", "48 mΩ"], "ΔV = 0.03 × 0.8 = 24 mV, and Z = ΔV/ΔI = 24 mV/60 A = 0.4 mΩ, which must hold at every frequency the current step contains."],
["16-35", "M", "Inter-symbol interference on a long serial link is caused mainly by:", ["Loss that grows with frequency, smearing each bit into the following bits", "The receiver's clock running slightly fast", "Too many transitions in the encoded data", "Using differential signaling"], "Copper and dielectric losses remove high frequencies, so edges arrive slow and rounded and each bit's energy spills into its neighbors. Equalization undoes it."],
["16-36", "M", "A decision-feedback equalizer (DFE) in a SerDes receiver:", ["Subtracts the expected leftover of bits already received from the current bit", "Boosts the transmitted signal at every transition", "Encodes the data to guarantee transitions", "Matches the trace impedance at the receiver"], "Once the receiver has decided a bit, it knows how much that bit will spill into the next ones, and subtracts it. Transmitter pre-emphasis and receiver CTLE are the other equalizers."],
["16-37", "M", "Back-drilling the vias on a high-speed board:", ["Removes the unused via stub, which would otherwise resonate and notch out a band of frequencies", "Adds a ground return path beside the signal", "Lowers the trace's characteristic impedance", "Connects the via to every layer"], "The unused part of a through-hole via is an open-ended stub. Near the frequency where it is a quarter wavelength long, it reflects strongly and cancels the signal. Drilling it out removes the problem."],
["16-38", "E", "How many bytes is an Ethernet header without a VLAN tag?", ["14", "20", "8", "64"], "Destination MAC (6) + source MAC (6) + EtherType (2) = 14 bytes. 20 is the IPv4 header, 8 the UDP header, and 64 the minimum frame size."],
["16-39", "M", "With no VLAN tag and no IP options, the UDP payload begins at which byte of an Ethernet frame (counting from the destination MAC as byte 0)?", ["42", "34", "28", "50"], "Ethernet 14 + IPv4 20 + UDP 8 = 42. Byte 34 is where the UDP header starts."],
["16-40", "M", "An Ethernet frame arrives on a 64-bit (8-byte) datapath, starting with the destination MAC. Byte 42 arrives in which word?", ["The sixth word (bytes 40–47)", "The fifth word (bytes 32–39)", "The seventh word (bytes 48–55)", "The forty-second word"], "Word k holds bytes 8k to 8k + 7. Byte 42 is in word 5 counting from 0, which is the sixth word, at byte position 2."],
["16-41", "E", "Network protocol headers send multi-byte fields:", ["Most significant byte first (big-endian, 'network byte order')", "Least significant byte first (little-endian)", "In whichever order the sending CPU uses", "With the bits of each byte reversed"], "All IP-suite headers are big-endian. A little-endian CPU must swap bytes (ntohs, ntohl); hardware just wires the bytes in the right order."],
["16-42", "M", "Which statement about UDP is true?", ["It has no connection, acknowledgments or retransmission, and it supports multicast", "It guarantees delivery in order, retransmitting lost packets", "It works only within a single Ethernet segment", "It uses MAC addresses to route between networks"], "UDP adds only ports, a length and a checksum to IP. A lost datagram is simply gone. That simplicity, plus multicast, is why exchanges publish market data over UDP."],
["16-43", "M", "TCP is much harder than UDP to implement in FPGA logic because it:", ["Keeps state for every connection: sequence numbers, acknowledgments, retransmission buffers and flow control", "Has a header longer than an Ethernet frame", "Cannot be carried over Ethernet", "Needs floating-point arithmetic for its checksum"], "TCP must remember and possibly resend everything not yet acknowledged, track the window, and handle timeouts. Hardware TCP exists, as dedicated offload engines, but it is far larger than a UDP parser."],
["16-44", "M", "At 10 Gb/s, how long does it take just to receive a minimum-size 64-byte frame?", ["51.2 ns", "6.4 ns", "512 ns", "5.12 ns"], "64 bytes × 8 = 512 bits; 512 / 10 Gb/s = 51.2 ns (the preamble and gap add more). A byte takes 0.8 ns."],
["16-45", "M", "The frame check sequence at the end of each Ethernet frame is:", ["A CRC-32 over the frame, so the receiver can drop corrupted frames", "A count of the bytes in the frame", "The sender's IP address", "A timestamp for measuring latency"], "The receiver recomputes the CRC and discards the frame on a mismatch. Because it comes last, a design that waits for it pays the full reception time."],
["16-46", "H", "A low-latency FPGA parser starts acting on a frame's fields before the frame's CRC has arrived. This is safe only if:", ["Whatever it starts can be cancelled or ignored if the CRC later fails", "The frame is shorter than 64 bytes", "Software recomputes the CRC afterward", "The frame carries a VLAN tag"], "Cut-through designs speculate: they act on early fields, then drop or abort the result if the frame turns out to be corrupt. Without a way to undo, a corrupted frame could trigger a real action."],
["16-47", "M", "An IPv4 header's protocol field is 17. The packet carries:", ["UDP", "TCP", "ICMP", "IPv6"], "Protocol numbers: 1 = ICMP, 6 = TCP, 17 = UDP."],
// 17 Manufacturing and reliability
["17-01","M","For the antenna rule, as long as the metal wire is safe, a small connected transistor is good.",false,"The risk depends on the ratio of metal area to gate area. A smaller gate means a larger ratio, so more charge per unit of oxide.","o"],
["17-02","M","The antenna ratio compares:",["The metal area connected to a gate with that gate's area","Wire length with wire width","Gate length with gate width","The number of vias with the number of wires"],"More metal collects more charge; more gate area spreads it out.","v"],
["17-03","M","Which fixes an antenna violation?",["Adding a reverse-biased diode near the gate","Making the gate smaller","Lengthening the lower-layer wire","Raising VDD"],"The diode drains plasma charge to the substrate during etching. Jumping to a higher layer and inserting a buffer also work.","v"],
["17-04","H","Why does jumping a long wire up to a higher metal layer fix an antenna problem?",["While the lower layer is etched, the gate sees only a short wire","Higher metal layers see no plasma during etching","Higher metal layers are made of insulating material","Jumping layers makes the connected gate larger"],"Once the full connection exists, the collected charge has a safe drain.","v"],
["17-05","E","Where does antenna charge come from?",["Plasma etching during manufacturing","Normal circuit operation","Clock switching","Static from handling the package"],"Exposed metal collects charge from the plasma.","v"],
["17-06","M","Defect density is 0.2 per cm². The yield of a 2 cm² die is about:",["67%","80%","40%","96%"],"Y = e^(−DA) = e^(−0.4) ≈ 67%."],
["17-07","M","Electromigration gets worse with:",["Higher current density and temperature","Lower temperature and thinner oxide","Wider wires and lower current","More vias and shorter wires"],"Black's equation: MTTF ∝ J⁻ⁿ·e^(Ea/kT)."],
["17-08","M","Latch-up is caused by:",["A parasitic p-n-p-n thyristor turning on","Using too many latches in one block","Clock skew between neighbouring flip-flops","Antenna charge left on the gates"],"It latches into a short from supply to ground. Well and substrate taps and guard rings prevent it."],
["17-09","E","LVS checks that:",["The layout matches the intended netlist","Layout shapes obey spacing rules","Every path meets its timing","The chip stays under its thermal limit"],"DRC is the check for geometric rules."],
["17-10","E","Layout tools add dummy metal fill to:",["Keep metal density uniform for polishing","Carry extra current to hot blocks","Store charge for decoupling","Shield clock wires from noise"],"Flat layers need an even density of metal."],
["17-11","M","A chip's input pins are protected from static electricity by:",["Diodes to the supply and ground, plus clamp circuits","Thicker gate oxide on every transistor","Pull-up resistors","Antenna diodes only"],"ESD structures steer the discharge current around the core."],
["17-12","M","NBTI mainly affects:",["PMOS transistors, raising their Vt over time","Copper wires, raising their resistance","Capacitors, lowering their value","NMOS transistors at low temperature"],"It is an aging mechanism that slows chips over years."],
["17-13","E","A larger die area increases manufacturing yield.",false,"Yield falls exponentially with area, which is one reason for chiplets."],
["17-14","E","Scan chains are used to:",["Shift test patterns in and results out","Distribute the clock to every flip-flop","Measure on-die temperature at each block","Encrypt data stored in the flip-flops"],"They make manufacturing defects such as stuck-at faults testable."],
["17-15","M","A gate with 0.05 μm² of oxide connects to 40 μm² of metal on the layer being etched. The antenna ratio limit is 400. The net:",["Violates the rule, with a ratio of 800","Passes, since 800 is under the limit","Passes, with a ratio of 0.00125","Cannot be judged"],"40 / 0.05 = 800, which is above 400.","v"],
["17-16","M","For the same 40 μm² of metal and a limit of 400, what is the smallest total gate area that passes?",["0.1 μm²","0.05 μm²","0.01 μm²","1 μm²"],"40 / 400 = 0.1 μm².","v"],
["17-17","M","A long lower-metal wire to a gate violates the antenna rule. Making that wire wider:",["Makes it worse, by adding metal area","Fixes it, by lowering resistance","Has no effect on the ratio","Fixes it, by spreading the charge"],"More exposed metal collects more charge.","v"],
["17-18","M","Connecting a second gate to the same net:",["Lowers the ratio, since gate area grows","Raises the ratio, since metal grows","Has no effect on the ratio","Always creates a new violation"],"The collected charge spreads over more oxide.","v"],
["17-19","H","Once a net's metal connects to a source/drain diffusion, such as the driving cell's output, the antenna risk:",["Goes away; the diffusion drains the charge","Doubles, since two junctions collect charge","Stays the same until the top layer is done","Moves to the diffusion, which can now break down"],"The danger exists only while a gate is connected to metal that has no path to a diffusion yet.","v"],
["17-20","M","An antenna diode is:",["A reverse-biased diffusion diode attached to the gate's net","A forward-biased diode in series with the gate","A capacitor","A fuse"],"It stays reverse biased, and harmless, in normal operation, but bleeds plasma charge away during manufacturing.","v"],
["17-21","M","A cost of adding an antenna diode is:",["A little extra capacitance and leakage","The circuit can no longer switch","It needs a separate supply rail","It doubles the gate's area"],"The cost is usually small, which is why diode insertion is a common fix.","v"],
["17-22","M","Thinner gate oxides make antenna damage:",["More likely, since less charge is needed","Impossible, since thin oxide conducts","Less likely, since less metal is used","Irrelevant, since only area matters"],"Thin oxide tolerates less voltage and tunneling stress.","v"],
["17-23","M","Antenna damage to a gate can show up later as:",["A Vt shift, gate leakage, or early breakdown","Faster switching from thinner oxide","A short between neighbouring wires","Lower resistance in the attached wire"],"Damaged oxide traps charge and wears out sooner.","v"],
["17-24","M","Antenna rules are checked:",["Layer by layer, as each metal layer is etched","Only on the top metal layer","Only after the chip is packaged","Only by simulating the running chip"],"Some rule decks also limit the cumulative ratio across layers.","v"],
["17-25","E","The antenna effect is a hazard that occurs during:",["Manufacturing, not normal operation","Normal operation at high frequency","Packaging and wire bonding only","Production testing only"],"Layout rules prevent this fabrication-time hazard.","v"],
["17-26","E","Antenna violations are most likely on small transistors' gates connected to long wires.",true,"A small gate area and a large metal area both raise the ratio.","v"],
["17-27","M","Electromigration is mainly a concern for wires that carry:",["High average current in one direction","No current, only a fixed voltage","Only light, in optical links","Only bidirectional signal currents"],"Wires with bidirectional current partly recover; power grids see steady flow."],
["17-28","E","A common way to reduce electromigration risk on a power wire is to:",["Widen it to lower current density","Make it thinner to lower capacitance","Run it hotter to anneal the metal","Use fewer vias to reduce joints"],"J = I/A, so more cross-section means lower current density."],
["17-29","M","Layouts prevent latch-up mainly with:",["Frequent well and substrate taps and guard rings","Fewer flip-flops in each block","Thicker gate oxide on every transistor","Antenna diodes on every gate"],"Low-resistance taps stop the well and substrate voltages from shifting enough to trigger the parasitic thyristor."],
["17-30","M","The stuck-at-0 fault model assumes that a node:",["Is permanently tied to 0","Switches too slowly to 0","Is metastable near 0","Is pulled toward 0 by crosstalk"],"ATPG tools generate patterns that make each such fault visible at an output."],
["17-31","M","Chemical-mechanical polishing (CMP) is used to:",["Flatten each layer before the next","Etch the transistors' channels","Add dopants to the silicon","Test each die for defects"],"Uneven metal density causes dishing, which is why fill is added."],
["17-32","E","DRC (design rule checking) verifies that:",["Shapes obey width and spacing rules","The netlist matches the schematic","Every path meets its timing","Power stays within its budget"],"LVS is the check that compares layout with netlist."],
["17-33","M","A scan chain lets a tester:",["Load and read back every flip-flop directly","Measure each transistor's threshold voltage","Run the functional clock at a higher speed","Repair broken wires found on the die"],"In test mode the flip-flops are connected into shift registers, turning a sequential circuit into easily testable combinational logic."],
["17-34","M","At-speed (transition-fault) testing exists because:",["Some defects make a node slow rather than stuck","Stuck-at tests are not allowed on modern chips","Testers cannot apply patterns at low speed","Scan chains only shift at the full clock rate"],"Launch a transition and capture it one functional clock period later; a slow-to-rise or slow-to-fall node fails.","b"],
["17-35","M","Memory BIST (MBIST) is:",["On-chip logic that tests the memories by itself","A manual inspection of memory under a microscope","A special kind of DRAM used for testing","A software checker that runs on the host"],"Memories are too dense to test through scan, so built-in test engines run march algorithms on them."],
["17-36","M","JTAG (IEEE 1149.1) boundary scan is used to:",["Test board connections through a few test pins","Raise the chip's maximum clock speed","Program the DRAM timing parameters","Measure the chip's power consumption"],"Boundary-scan cells at each pin can drive and capture values, checking solder joints without physical probes."],
["17-37","M","Fault coverage of 99% means:",["The test patterns detect 99% of the modeled faults","99% of manufactured chips pass the test","The design is 99% functionally verified","99% of the scan chains are working"],"Escapes (bad chips that pass) are reduced by high coverage of stuck-at and transition faults.","b"],
["17-38","E","The usual order of the physical design flow is:",["Floorplan, placement, clock-tree synthesis, routing, sign-off","Routing, placement, floorplan, sign-off","Sign-off, routing, synthesis, floorplan","Clock-tree synthesis, floorplan, synthesis, routing"],"Synthesis produces the netlist first; after routing come extraction, timing, power and physical verification."],
["17-39","M","Clock-tree synthesis (CTS) builds a buffered clock network that:",["Delivers the clock to every flip-flop with small skew","Removes the need for flip-flops in the design","Generates the clock frequency from a crystal","Routes the data signals between blocks"],"Before CTS, timing assumes an ideal clock; after it, real skew and clock latency are included."],
["17-40","M","High placement utilization (cells packed very densely) mainly risks:",["Routing congestion","Lower leakage","Faster timing","Fewer DRC checks"],"Typical utilization targets leave room for routing, buffers and later fixes.","b"],
["17-41","M","After routing, parasitic extraction produces:",["Each wire's resistance and capacitance (SPEF)","The gate-level netlist from RTL","The floorplan and its block outlines","The manufacturing test patterns"],"Sign-off timing uses extracted parasitics instead of estimates.","b"],
["17-42","M","An ECO (engineering change order) late in a project is:",["A small, targeted fix to the netlist or layout","The start of a new chip project","A design rule check on the layout","A change to the target clock frequency"],"Metal-only ECOs can even be made after tape-out by changing only a few mask layers.","b"],
["17-43","M","A standard-cell library's .lib (Liberty) file describes:",["Each cell's timing, power and pin capacitance","Each cell's exact layout shapes","The chip's floorplan and pin placement","The manufacturing test patterns"],"Timing tools read .lib for delays; LEF describes the cells' physical outlines for placement and routing.","b"],
["17-44", "M", "Boundary scan (JTAG) testing of an assembled board finds faults such as:", ["Open or shorted solder joints between chips", "Setup-time violations inside a chip's logic", "Bugs in the RTL", "Aging from hot carrier injection"], "A boundary-scan cell beside every pin lets the tester drive one chip's outputs and read the next chip's inputs, checking each board connection without physical probes."],
["17-45", "E", "Which signals make up the basic JTAG test port?", ["TCK, TMS, TDI and TDO", "SCL and SDA", "MOSI, MISO, SCK and CS", "TX and RX"], "Test clock, test mode select, test data in and test data out, plus an optional reset. SCL/SDA is I²C, MOSI/MISO is SPI, TX/RX is a UART."],
["17-46", "M", "Besides board testing, the JTAG port is commonly used to:", ["Program FPGAs during development and connect debuggers to processors", "Carry the chip's main data traffic", "Distribute the clock to every flip-flop", "Supply power during wafer test"], "JTAG gives access to internal registers through a few pins, so it is also the standard way to load an FPGA bitstream from a laptop and to halt, step and inspect a CPU."],
// 18 Memory circuits
["18-01","H","For a 6T SRAM cell to be read without flipping, which must be stronger?",["The pull-down NMOS, compared with the access NMOS","The access NMOS, compared with the pull-down","The pull-up PMOS, compared with the pull-down","All transistors equally"],"During a read, the node storing 0 is a voltage divider between them."],
["18-02","H","For a 6T SRAM cell to be writable, which must be stronger?",["The access NMOS, compared with the pull-up PMOS","The pull-up PMOS, compared with the access NMOS","The pull-down, compared with the access transistor","None of them"],"The access transistor must overpower the PMOS that holds the node at 1."],
["18-03","H","A 30 fF DRAM cell holding 1.0 V is read onto a 120 fF bitline precharged to 0.5 V. The bitline changes by:",["0.1 V","0.5 V","0.25 V","0.02 V"],"(1.0 − 0.5) × 30/150 = 0.1 V."],
["18-04","E","DRAM needs periodic refresh because:",["The capacitor's charge leaks away","Reads are too slow without it","The clock stops between accesses","Nearby SRAM disturbs the cells"],"Each row is typically refreshed about every 64 ms."],
["18-05","E","A DRAM read is destructive, so the value must be written back.",true,"Charge sharing disturbs the cell, and the sense amplifier restores it."],
["18-06","H","A 256 Kb SRAM array has 512 rows and 32-bit words. How many row and column address bits?",["9 row, 4 column","8 row, 5 column","9 row, 5 column","10 row, 3 column"],"Each row holds 512 bits, which is 16 words."],
["18-07","M","SECDED error correction for 64 data bits uses how many check bits?",["8","1","6","16"],"That is why server and GPU memory often uses 72-bit words."],
["18-08","M","Why do memories use sense amplifiers?",["Bitlines swing slowly, so a small difference is amplified","To increase the array's density by sharing bitlines","To refresh DRAM cells while the array is idle","To store the bits while the array is powered off"],"A cross-coupled latch amplifies the small difference to a full logic level."],
["18-09","E","Which memory keeps its data with the power off?",["Flash","SRAM","DRAM","A register file"],"Charge trapped on an isolated gate stays for years."],
["18-10","M","A CAM (content-addressable memory):",["Searches all entries in parallel for a match","Is a kind of DRAM that needs refresh","Stores only addresses, never data","Has no comparators, only decoders"],"Used in TLBs and network routers; fast but power-hungry."],
["18-11","M","Adding read and write ports to a register file:",["Adds lines and transistors to every cell, growing area","Is free, since the ports share bitlines","Shrinks its area by splitting cells","Removes the need for address decoders"],"Ports are expensive."],
["18-12","E","Before an SRAM read, both bitlines are precharged high.",true,"The side storing 0 then pulls its bitline slightly down."],
["18-13","M","The main advantage of an 8T SRAM cell over a 6T cell is:",["A read port that cannot disturb the cell","A smaller area than the 6T cell","No access transistors at all","It needs no supply to hold data"],"It removes the read-stability problem, at a cost in area."],
["18-14","M","A 6T SRAM's cell ratio (pull-down width over access width) is made greater than 1 to ensure:",["Read stability","Writability","Low leakage","Fast refresh"],"A strong pull-down stops the 0 node from rising enough to flip the cell during a read."],
["18-15","M","During an SRAM write, the bitline on the side storing 1 is driven to 0. It must overpower the cell's:",["Pull-up PMOS","Pull-down NMOS","Sense amplifier","Wordline driver"],"Writability needs a strong access transistor relative to the pull-up."],
["18-16","M","A column multiplexer lets several SRAM columns share one:",["Sense amplifier and write driver","Wordline and row decoder","Storage cell and bitline pair","Clock and precharge circuit"],"It also lets the array be laid out closer to square."],
["18-17","H","An SRAM's static noise margin shrinks at low supply voltage. This sets:",["The array's minimum voltage (Vmin)","The array's maximum capacity","The array's refresh rate","The array's address width"],"Memories often get a separate, slightly higher supply for this reason."],
["18-18","E","Which is denser: SRAM (6 transistors per bit) or DRAM (1 transistor and 1 capacitor per bit)?",["DRAM","SRAM","They are equal","Neither stores bits"],"DRAM is denser and cheaper per bit, but slower, and it needs refresh."],
["18-19","M","A Hamming SECDED code on a 64-bit word can:",["Correct single-bit errors and detect double-bit errors","Correct any double-bit error in the word","Only detect errors, never correct them","Correct any number of errors per word"],"Single-error correct, double-error detect."],
["18-20","M","SRAM characterization across PVT corners checks:",["Read/write margins, speed and leakage at every corner","Only the nominal corner, at room temperature","Only the package and its bond wires","The RTL code coverage of the memory array"],"A memory must work at the worst process, lowest voltage and both temperature extremes, which sets Vmin.","b"],
["18-21","M","Memory test finds a few failing cells in a large SRAM. Repair saves the die by:",["Mapping the failing rows or columns to spare ones, set by fuses","Lowering the supply voltage until the cells pass","Marking those addresses read-only","Running the memory at a slower clock"],"Large memories include spare rows and columns. BIST finds the failing cells, redundancy analysis (BIRA, often on chip) picks the spares that cover them, and fuses, or a repair register loaded at boot, steer those addresses to the spares. Without repair, one bad cell among billions would scrap the die, so repair is a large part of memory yield.","b"],
["18-22","H","A synchronous FIFO stores its data in a single-port SRAM. Can it accept a write and a read in the same clock cycle?",["Not directly; it needs two banks, a wider word, or a dual-port SRAM","Yes; a single port does one read and one write every cycle","Yes, as long as the read and write addresses differ","Only when the FIFO is empty"],"A single port does one access, a read or a write, per cycle, whatever the addresses. To sustain a write and a read every cycle, designs split the storage into two banks (even and odd entries) so the two go to different banks, or store two entries per wider word and alternate, or pay the area for a two-port SRAM.","b"],
// 19 RTL, CDC, FIFOs and verification
["19-01","M","a = 3 and b = 7. After one clock edge of: always @(posedge clk) begin a = b; b = a; end (blocking), a and b are:",["7 and 7","7 and 3","3 and 7","3 and 3"],"The second statement reads the new value of a.","r"],
["19-02","M","a = 3 and b = 7. After one edge of a <= b; b <= a; (nonblocking), a and b are:",["7 and 3","7 and 7","3 and 3","3 and 7"],"All the right-hand sides use the old values, so the registers swap.","r"],
["19-03","M","A $display after nonblocking assignments in the same clocked block prints the values from before the update.",true,"Nonblocking updates take effect at the end of the time step.","r"],
["19-04","M","always @(*) if (sel) y = a; with no else, synthesizes:",["A latch","A flip-flop","Pure combinational logic","Nothing"],"y must hold its value when sel = 0. Add an else or a default."],
["19-05","E","A two-flop synchronizer is the right way to cross:",["A single-bit level signal","A 32-bit data bus","The clock itself","A binary counter value"],"Multi-bit values need Gray code, a handshake, or an asynchronous FIFO."],
["19-06","M","A multi-bit counter value that crosses clock domains should be encoded in:",["Gray code","Two's complement","One-hot","BCD"],"Only one bit changes per increment, so a sample taken mid-change is the old or the new value."],
["19-07","H","A writer at 150 MHz sends bursts of 300 words, and a reader at 100 MHz reads one word per cycle. What is the minimum FIFO depth?",["100","200","300","50"],"The burst lasts 2 μs, and 200 words are read in that time.","r"],
["19-08","H","A writer at 200 MHz sends a burst of 100 words. The reader runs at 100 MHz but takes one word every 2 cycles. What FIFO depth is needed?",["75","50","25","100"],"The burst lasts 0.5 μs; at 50 M words/s, 25 are read, leaving 75.","r"],
["19-09","M","When a FIFO's read and write pointers match, full is told apart from empty by:",["An extra pointer bit (or a count)","A parity bit on each pointer","The ratio of the clock frequencies","The state of the reset signal"],"Equal including the extra bit means empty; different only in it means full."],
["19-10","H","Glitches up to 15 ns must be filtered out of a reset line using 2 ns buffers. How many buffers are needed?",["8","7","15","4"],"The delayed path must be longer than the glitch: 8 × 2 = 16 ns.","r"],
["19-11","M","A recommended reset scheme is:",["Asynchronous assert, synchronous deassert","Synchronous assert, asynchronous deassert","Asynchronous both ways, with no synchronizer","No reset at all"],"Release the reset through flip-flops on the destination clock."],
["19-12","M","A one-cycle pulse from a fast clock domain into a slow one:",["Can be missed, so stretch it or use a toggle handshake","Always arrives, since synchronizers catch edges","Becomes two pulses in the slow domain","Needs Gray code to cross safely"],"It can fall entirely between the slow clock's edges."],
["19-13","E","Use nonblocking (<=) assignments in clocked always blocks.",true,"Use blocking (=) assignments in combinational always @(*) blocks."],
["19-14","M","The advantage of a round-robin arbiter over fixed priority is that it:",["Avoids starving low-priority requesters","Is smaller than a fixed-priority arbiter","Has no internal state to reset","Grants two requests in the same cycle"],"Priority rotates after each grant."],
["19-15","E","In a valid/ready handshake, a transfer happens:",["When valid and ready are both high at a clock edge","Whenever valid is high, ignoring ready","Whenever ready is high, ignoring valid","Only on the first edge after reset"],"Either side can pause the other."],
["19-16","H","Initially a = 1 and b = 2. At a clock edge, the block runs begin a = b; b <= a; end. Afterwards, a and b are:",["a = 2, b = 2","a = 2, b = 1","a = 1, b = 2","a = 1, b = 1"],"The blocking a = b makes a 2 immediately. b <= a then samples the new a, so b also becomes 2.","r"],
["19-17","H","Initially a = 1 and b = 2. At a clock edge, the block runs begin a <= b; b = a; end. Afterwards, a and b are:",["a = 2, b = 1","a = 2, b = 2","a = 1, b = 1","a = 1, b = 2"],"a <= b samples 2 and updates later. b = a runs immediately with the old a = 1. Then a becomes 2.","r"],
["19-18","H","Initially a = 0. At each rising edge, the block runs begin a <= a + 1; $display(a); end. What prints at the first and second edges?",["0, then 1","1, then 2","0, then 0","1, then 1"],"$display runs before the nonblocking update takes effect, so it shows the old value each time.","r"],
["19-19","M","In Verilog, a variable assigned inside an always block must be declared as:",["reg (or logic in SystemVerilog)","wire (or net in SystemVerilog)","parameter (a constant)","tri (a tristate net)"],"wire is for continuous assignments and connections; SystemVerilog's logic works in both places."],
["19-20","M","A case statement in a combinational always block that leaves some input values uncovered, with no default:",["Infers a latch","Infers a flip-flop","Is always a compile error","Becomes a mux that outputs Z"],"The output must hold its old value for the missing cases."],
["19-21","M","SystemVerilog's always_ff and always_comb blocks help because:",["Tools check the code really is flip-flops or pure logic","They simulate faster than plain always blocks","They remove the need for a clock signal","They make the code unsynthesizable on purpose"],"always_ff tells tools the block must be flip-flops, and always_comb that it must be pure combinational logic. The tools then check the code really does that; for example, always_comb warns if a missing else or default would infer a latch."],
["19-22","H","Glitches up to 25 ns wide must be filtered by ORing a signal with a delayed copy of itself. Buffers delay 2 ns each, and the OR gate delays 4 ns. How many buffers are needed?",["13","11","15","12"],"The OR gate sits on both paths, so only the buffers count. The delay must exceed 25 ns: 13 × 2 = 26 ns.","r"],
["19-23","H","A writer at 100 MHz sends a 120-word burst, back to back. The reader at 80 MHz reads one word per cycle. What is the minimum FIFO depth?",["24","120","96","20"],"The burst lasts 1.2 μs, during which the reader takes 96 words, leaving 24.","r"],
["19-24","H","A writer at 50 MHz sends a 64-word burst. The reader at 100 MHz reads one word every 4 cycles. What is the minimum FIFO depth?",["32","64","16","48"],"The burst lasts 64 × 20 ns = 1.28 μs. At 25 M words/s, the reader takes 32 in that time, leaving 32.","r"],
["19-25","M","Asynchronous FIFOs pass their read and write pointers across clock domains in Gray code because:",["Only one bit changes per increment","Gray code needs fewer bits than binary","It removes the need for synchronizers","Gray counters count faster than binary"],"A binary pointer changing several bits at once could be sampled as a value far from either one.","r"],
["19-26","M","In an asynchronous FIFO, full and empty are flagged conservatively (slightly early) because:",["Each side sees a lagging copy of the other's pointer","Gray code rounds the pointers to a power of 2","The FIFO is deeper than needed, so it reports early","Both clocks run faster than the data arrives"],"Lagging pointers make full and empty pessimistic, which is safe."],
["19-27","M","In a verification testbench, a scoreboard:",["Compares outputs against a reference model","Generates the clock and reset signals","Synthesizes the design into gates","Measures the design's power draw"],"Checkers and scoreboards decide pass or fail automatically."],
["19-28","M","Constrained-random stimulus is used in verification to:",["Hit legal but unexpected input cases","Make each simulation run shorter","Avoid the need to write any checkers","Replace synthesis with random gates"],"The constraints keep the random inputs legal."],
["19-29","M","Functional coverage measures:",["Which features and scenarios the tests exercised","How many lines of RTL the design has","The chip area each feature uses","The clock frequency each block reaches"],"Verification closes when coverage goals are met and no bugs remain open."],
["19-30","M","The SystemVerilog assertion assert property (@(posedge clk) req |-> ##[1:3] ack); checks that:",["Each req is followed by ack within 1–3 cycles","ack is never high while req is high","req and ack are always equal","The clock period is 3 cycles long"],"Assertions watch the design continuously, in simulation and in formal tools."],
["19-31","H","Formal verification differs from simulation in that it:",["Proves properties for all inputs, not just simulated ones","Runs only on gate-level netlists, never on RTL","Needs no properties or assertions to be written","Only works for analog and mixed-signal circuits"],"It can find corner-case bugs that random simulation might never hit."],
["19-32","M","100% code coverage (every line executed) guarantees the design has no bugs.",false,"It only shows that code ran, not that results were checked or every scenario was tried. Functional coverage and checkers are also needed."],
["19-33","H","A multi-bit value crosses clock domains safely with a data-plus-valid handshake by:",["Hold the data, synchronize only one valid bit, then sample","Put every data bit through its own synchronizer","Clock the receiver faster than the sender","Gray-code the data before it crosses"],"The data is stable when sampled, so only the one control bit needs synchronizing."],
["19-34","E","A self-checking testbench:",["Computes the expected results and compares them with the design automatically","Is checked by an engineer reading the waveforms after each run","Is synthesized into the chip so it can test itself in the field","Runs only on the gate-level netlist, never on the RTL"],"It uses a reference model to produce expected outputs and reports any mismatch, so millions of cycles can be checked without anyone looking at waveforms."],
["19-35","M","The main strength of a directed test over a random test is that it:",["Hits a specific corner case on purpose and is easy to debug","Explores many combinations that nobody thought to write","Needs no checker, because the inputs are chosen by hand","Gives full functional coverage on its own"],"Directed tests target known cases; constrained-random tests find the unexpected ones. Real test plans use both."],
["19-36","M","Each random test run prints its seed so that:",["A failing run can be reproduced exactly","The test runs faster on the next attempt","Coverage is collected automatically","The design synthesizes correctly"],"The seed fixes the random sequence, so the same failure can be rerun, debugged and confirmed fixed."],
["19-37","M","An immediate assertion differs from a concurrent assertion in that it:",["Checks a condition at one instant, like an if statement","Checks a property across several clock cycles","Can only be used by formal tools, not in simulation","Is synthesized into the hardware as a checker"],"Concurrent assertions (SVA properties) describe behaviour over clock cycles, such as req |-> ##[1:3] gnt."],
["19-38","H","Which SVA property states that gnt is never high unless req is high in the same cycle?",["gnt |-> req","req |-> gnt","req |-> ##1 gnt","gnt |-> ##1 req"],"gnt |-> req: whenever gnt is high, req must be high in that same cycle. req |-> gnt would instead require every request to be granted immediately."],
["19-39","M","Equivalence checking is used to:",["Prove two versions of a design behave identically","Measure how much functional coverage the tests reached","Generate constrained-random stimulus for a block","Find the critical path through the design"],"It is a formal technique, run for example after synthesis or after a late fix to the netlist."],
["19-40","M","In UVM, the component that turns transactions into pin-level activity on the design's inputs is the:",["Driver","Monitor","Scoreboard","Sequencer"],"The sequencer produces transactions, the driver drives them onto pins, the monitor turns pin activity back into transactions, and the scoreboard compares results."],
["19-41","M","Chip teams use emulators and FPGA prototypes during verification because they:",["Run far faster than simulation, so real software can run early","Remove the need for any testbench or checker","Measure the final chip's power consumption exactly","Fix timing violations in the netlist automatically"],"Full-chip simulation is far too slow to boot an operating system or run a driver; emulation is fast enough."],
["19-42","E","A testbench compares outputs with !== rather than != because !==:",["Also treats X and Z values as mismatches","Simulates faster than the != operator","Ignores the upper bits of the result","Compares only the sign bit"],"With !=, a comparison involving X gives X, which an if treats as false, so an unknown output could slip through as a pass."],
["19-43","M","Glitches up to 9 ns wide must be filtered using 2 ns buffers. How many buffers are needed?",["5","4","9","18"],"The delay must exceed the glitch width: 5 × 2 = 10 ns > 9 ns.","r"],
["19-44","M","A filter ANDs a signal with a copy of itself delayed by 12 ns. Which glitches does it remove?",["High pulses shorter than about 12 ns","Low pulses shorter than about 12 ns","All pulses longer than 12 ns","Only pulses exactly 12 ns wide"],"A short high pulse is over before its delayed copy arrives, so the two are never high together. (ORing the copies removes short low pulses instead.)","r"],
["19-45","M","In UVM, the component that turns pin activity back into transactions for checking is the:",["Monitor","Driver","Sequencer","Sequence"],"Driver: transactions to pins. Monitor: pins to transactions. The scoreboard compares monitored transactions with expected ones.","b"],
["19-46","M","UVM's build, connect and run phases exist so that:",["Components are built, then connected, then run, in order","Tests run in parallel on many machines","Synthesis tools can read the testbench","Coverage is turned off during each phase"],"Phasing coordinates hundreds of components without each one managing the order itself.","b"],
["19-47","M","cocotb lets you:",["Write testbenches in Python for a Verilog simulation","Synthesize Python code directly into gates","Run Verilog designs without any simulator","Replace SystemVerilog assertions with C code"],"The design runs in a normal simulator; Python coroutines await clock edges and check values.","b"],
["19-48","M","A CDC (clock domain crossing) checking tool is used to:",["Find unsynchronized clock domain crossings","Measure each clock's frequency","Generate the clock trees","Replace static timing analysis"],"Simulation rarely catches CDC bugs because metastability is not modeled; structural CDC tools do.","b"],
["19-49","M","A regression's pass rate is 100%, but functional coverage is 60%. The right conclusion is:",["40% of the planned scenarios are untested","The design is now fully verified","The coverage tool must be broken","The passing tests should be removed"],"Passing only says nothing failed in what ran. Coverage closure needs new or better-steered tests."],
["19-50","M","A lint tool (for example Verilator --lint-only or Spyglass) on RTL finds:",["Width mismatches, latches and undriven signals","Timing violations found after routing","Manufacturing defects on the wafer","Thermal hotspots in the floorplan"],"Lint is fast static checking, run on every change before simulation.","b"],
["19-51","M","In Verilog, casez treats ___ as don't-care, while casex treats ___ as don't-care.",["z (and ?) only; both x and z","x only; z only","both x and z; z only","nothing; everything"],"casex also matches an x on the case expression, so an unknown input can silently pick a branch in simulation and hide a bug that the synthesized logic will not share. Most style guides use casez, with ? for the don't-care bits, or SystemVerilog's case inside."],
["19-52","M","In SystemVerilog, logic [7:0] mem [0:255]; declares:",["256 unpacked elements, each an 8-bit packed vector","8 unpacked elements, each a 256-bit packed vector","One 2048-bit packed vector","A 256-bit vector of 8 unpacked bits"],"Dimensions before the name are packed: they form one contiguous vector that can be sliced and used in arithmetic. Dimensions after the name are unpacked: an array of separate elements, like the words of a memory. mem[3] is one byte, and mem[3][0] is its low bit."],
["19-53","E","Compared with building the same design as an ASIC, putting it on an FPGA usually:",["Runs at a lower clock and uses more power, but can be changed after it is built, with no mask cost","Runs faster and uses less power, but cannot be changed once built","Gives the same speed and power at a lower cost per chip in high volume","Needs no synthesis or timing closure"],"An FPGA builds logic from lookup tables and programmable routing, so the same design is slower, larger and more power-hungry than custom cells. But it needs no masks, so a fix is a new bitstream instead of a new tapeout. FPGAs suit prototypes, low volumes and changing standards; ASICs win in high volume, where the mask cost is spread over millions of chips."],
["19-54", "E", "SystemVerilog's logic type replaces:", ["Both wire and reg, for signals with a single driver", "Only wire, so reg is still needed for flip-flops", "Only reg, so wire is still needed for assign statements", "integer, for loop counters and array indices"], "A logic signal can be driven by an assign or by one procedural block. It removes Verilog's confusing rule that anything assigned in an always block must be a reg, whether or not it becomes a register. (Signals with several drivers, such as tristate buses, still use wire.)"],
["19-55", "M", "An always_comb block differs from always @(*) in that it:", ["Needs no sensitivity list, runs once at time zero, and lets tools flag a latch it would infer", "Builds flip-flops whenever the block contains an if without an else", "Is accepted by simulators but ignored by synthesis tools", "Updates its outputs only at the next rising clock edge"], "always_comb is sensitive to everything it reads, evaluates once at time zero so its outputs start correct, and states the intent 'pure combinational logic', so tools warn when a path leaves an output unassigned."],
["19-56", "M", "typedef enum logic [1:0] {IDLE, LOAD, RUN, DONE} state_t; With no values given, RUN is encoded as:", ["2'b10", "2'b11", "2'b01", "Nothing until a value is assigned"], "Enum values start at 0 and count up in order: IDLE = 0, LOAD = 1, RUN = 2, DONE = 3."],
["19-57", "M", "The main benefit of declaring FSM states with an enum type is that:", ["States appear by name in waveforms, and assigning an arbitrary number to the state is a compile error", "Synthesis always produces a smaller state register than with parameters", "The state machine no longer needs a reset to start in a known state", "The next-state logic is guaranteed to be free of glitches"], "An enum is a named, checked type. Waveforms and messages show RUN instead of 2'b10, and the compiler rejects state = 3 without a cast, catching typos. The hardware is the same as with parameters."],
["19-58", "H", "In an always_comb next-state block, writing next = state; as the first statement:", ["Assigns next on every path, so no latch is inferred, and makes 'stay in this state' the default", "Adds a flip-flop that holds next for one extra cycle", "Creates a combinational loop, because next depends on itself", "Has no effect, because the case statement overwrites next on every path"], "A default at the top of a combinational block means every path assigns next, the standard cure for accidental latches. It is not a loop: next depends on state, a register output, not on itself. Each case branch then only says where to go when the machine moves."],
["19-59", "M", "unique case tells the tools that:", ["Exactly one item matches, so the branches can be built as parallel logic, and simulation warns otherwise", "The first matching item wins, so the items form a priority chain", "Each item value appears only once in the whole design", "The statement must be synthesized as a ROM"], "unique asserts that the items are mutually exclusive and complete. Synthesis may then build a parallel mux, and simulation reports a violation if no item or several items match. priority case is the version where the first match wins."],
["19-60", "M", "The old // synopsys full_case parallel_case comments are considered dangerous because they:", ["Change the synthesized hardware without changing the simulation, so the two can disagree", "Are ignored by every modern synthesis tool", "Make simulation treat every x as 0, hiding reset bugs", "Force every case statement into a slow priority chain"], "A comment cannot affect a simulator, but these ones told synthesis to assume things (no other values occur, items never overlap). If the assumption was false, the gates behaved differently from the RTL that passed simulation. unique and priority express the same intent in the language itself, so simulation checks it."],
["19-61", "M", "Which casez item matches every 4-bit value whose top bit is 1?", ["4'b1???", "4'b1000", "4'b?001", "4'b1111"], "In casez, ? (or z) in an item is a don't-care, so 4'b1??? matches 1000 through 1111. This is how priority encoders are usually written."],
["19-62", "H", "casex is usually avoided because:", ["An x on the value being tested also counts as don't-care, so an unknown input silently matches a branch", "It cannot describe a priority encoder", "Synthesis tools reject it", "It simulates far more slowly than an if-else chain"], "With casex, an uninitialized (x) select can match the first item, so simulation shows sensible behavior while the real hardware does something else. casez only treats z/? as don't-care, so x still shows up as a problem."],
["19-63", "M", "typedef struct packed { logic [3:0] kind; logic [3:0] id; logic [31:0] data; } pkt_t; Which bits of a pkt_t hold kind?", ["39:36", "3:0", "7:4", "35:32"], "A packed struct is one bit vector of 4 + 4 + 32 = 40 bits, and the first field is the most significant: kind is bits 39:36, id 35:32, data 31:0."],
["19-64", "M", "logic [3:0][7:0] w; declares:", ["One 32-bit vector made of four bytes, where w[2] is bits 23:16", "Four separate 8-bit registers that cannot be used as one value", "A memory of 8 entries, each 4 bits wide", "A memory of 4 entries that maps to block RAM"], "Both dimensions come before the name, so both are packed: it is a single 32-bit vector, indexed by byte. w[0] is bits 7:0, w[2] bits 23:16. Dimensions after the name, as in logic [7:0] mem [0:255], are unpacked and make a memory."],
["19-65", "E", "$clog2(17) returns:", ["5", "4", "17", "8"], "$clog2 rounds the base-2 logarithm up: 4 bits hold 16 values, so 17 values need 5 bits. $clog2(16) is 4."],
["19-66", "M", "A FIFO has DEPTH = 32 entries. Its pointers carry one extra wrap bit so full can be told from empty. How wide is each pointer?", ["6 bits", "5 bits", "32 bits", "4 bits"], "Addressing 32 entries takes $clog2(32) = 5 bits, plus 1 wrap bit, so 6. When the pointers are equal except for the wrap bit, the FIFO is full."],
["19-67", "M", "A generate for loop in a module:", ["Builds one copy of the hardware per iteration when the design is elaborated", "Runs its body once per clock cycle, like a software loop", "Can only appear in testbenches", "Builds the hardware once and reuses it on successive cycles"], "generate loops are unrolled at elaboration: a loop of 8 iterations makes 8 instances or 8 sets of logic, all working in parallel."],
["19-68", "M", "A for loop inside always_comb ORs together the 16 bits of a bus. It synthesizes to:", ["Parallel logic: one 16-input OR, evaluated continuously", "A sequential circuit that takes 16 cycles, with a counter", "Nothing, because loops cannot be synthesized", "One latch per loop iteration"], "Loops in RTL describe structure, not time. The tool unrolls the loop into 16 OR terms and builds a tree of gates (or LUTs)."],
["19-69", "M", "A SystemVerilog interface with modports is used to:", ["Bundle the signals of one connection and give each side its own directions", "Move data safely between two clock domains", "Describe the timing constraints of a port", "Store a module's parameters in a separate file"], "An interface groups, say, valid, ready and data into one port. A modport for the source makes valid and data outputs and ready an input; the sink's modport is the reverse."],
["19-70", "E", "A SystemVerilog package is used to:", ["Share types, parameters and functions among many modules", "Combine several modules into one synthesized netlist", "Pack a struct's fields into one bit vector", "Bundle a design's files for delivery to a customer"], "Modules import a package (import my_pkg::*;) so that, for example, every block uses the same packet struct and opcode enum."],
["19-71", "H", "A two-stage pipeline computes y = a*b + c. Stage 1 registers prod <= a*b; stage 2 does y <= prod + c, using the input c directly. What is wrong?", ["c is not delayed alongside prod, so each product is added to the next cycle's c", "Nonblocking assignments cannot be used for arithmetic", "Stage 2 must use a blocking assignment", "Nothing: c does not change within a cycle"], "prod holds a*b from the previous cycle, but c is the current input, one cycle newer. Every value used in a later stage must be carried forward in its own register (c1 <= c in stage 1) so it lines up with the data computed from it."],
["19-72", "M", "A 4-stage pipeline accepts a new input every cycle. Its latency and throughput are:", ["4 cycles, and one result per cycle", "1 cycle, and one result every 4 cycles", "4 cycles, and one result every 4 cycles", "1 cycle, and four results per cycle"], "Each input takes 4 cycles to come out (latency), but because 4 inputs are in flight at once, one result emerges every cycle (throughput)."],
["19-73", "E", "A VHDL design is written as:", ["An entity, which declares the ports, and an architecture, which describes the behavior", "A module and an endmodule, as in Verilog", "A header file and an implementation file", "A package and a testbench"], "The entity is the outside view (ports and generics); the architecture is the implementation. One entity can have several architectures."],
["19-74", "M", "Inside a clocked VHDL process, a and b are signals. After a <= b; b <= a; runs at a clock edge:", ["a and b have swapped", "Both hold b's old value", "Both hold a's old value", "Nothing changes until the next edge"], "Signal assignments take effect when the process finishes, so both right-hand sides use the old values, exactly like Verilog's nonblocking <=."],
["19-75", "M", "A VHDL variable assigned with := inside a process:", ["Takes its new value immediately, like a blocking assignment", "Updates when the process finishes, like a nonblocking assignment", "Always becomes a flip-flop", "Can only be read outside the process"], "Variables are local to the process and update at once, so the next statement sees the new value, like Verilog's blocking =. Signals (<=) update at the end of the process."],
["19-76", "E", "The VHDL construct if rising_edge(clk) then ... inside a process corresponds to which SystemVerilog block?", ["always_ff @(posedge clk)", "always_comb", "always @(clk)", "always_latch"], "Both describe flip-flops that capture on the rising edge of clk."],
["19-77", "M", "The VHDL statement y <= a when s = '1' else b; is equivalent to:", ["assign y = s ? a : b;", "assign y = s ? b : a;", "always_ff @(posedge s) y <= a;", "assign y = s & b;"], "It is a conditional signal assignment, VHDL's form of a mux outside any process: a when s is 1, otherwise b."],
["19-78", "M", "VHDL rejects adding 1 to a std_logic_vector. The usual fix is to:", ["Do the arithmetic on unsigned from numeric_std, for example cnt <= cnt + 1 with cnt declared unsigned", "Declare the signal as std_logic instead", "Use a variable instead of a signal", "Add the value one bit at a time"], "std_logic_vector is just a group of bits with no numeric meaning. numeric_std's unsigned and signed types define arithmetic, and explicit conversions such as std_logic_vector(cnt) move between them."],
["19-79", "E", "In a VHDL simulation, a register shows 'U' in the waveform. This usually means it:", ["Was never initialized or reset, so it has never been given a value", "Is being driven by two sources at once", "Is in the high-impedance state", "Holds an unsigned number"], "'U' (uninitialized) is std_logic's starting value. A register still at 'U' after reset is missing from the reset logic, or its clock or enable never arrived. A conflict between drivers shows as 'X'; high impedance as 'Z'."],
["19-80", "E", "A 6-input LUT on an FPGA can implement:", ["Any Boolean function of its 6 inputs, all with the same delay", "Only AND, OR and XOR of its inputs", "Any function of up to 64 inputs", "Only functions that fit in one 2:1 multiplexer"], "A LUT is a 64-bit memory addressed by its 6 inputs. Its contents are the truth table, so any 6-input function fits, and the delay does not depend on the function."],
["19-81", "M", "How many configuration bits does one 6-input LUT store?", ["64", "6", "36", "12"], "One bit per row of the truth table: 2⁶ = 64."],
["19-82", "H", "On an FPGA with 6-input LUTs, how many LUT levels does a 32-input AND need at minimum?", ["2", "1", "3", "6"], "Level 1: ⌈32/6⌉ = 6 LUTs reduce 32 signals to 6. Level 2: one LUT combines those 6. A function of up to 36 inputs fits in two levels."],
["19-83", "M", "A 32-bit adder on an FPGA is fast mainly because synthesis uses:", ["The dedicated carry chain in the logic blocks", "The multiplier in a DSP slice", "Block RAM as a lookup table", "A single very wide LUT"], "Each logic block has hard-wired carry logic linking it to its neighbor, so the carry does not travel through LUTs and general routing. That makes adders and counters much faster than generic logic of the same size."],
["19-84", "M", "RTL describes a 1024 × 32 memory whose read data comes straight from the address, with no register (an asynchronous read). On most FPGAs this:", ["Cannot use block RAM, whose reads are synchronous, so it is built from LUTs or flip-flops instead", "Maps to block RAM unchanged", "Stops synthesis with an error", "Is built from DSP slices"], "Block RAM registers its address, so data appears the cycle after. A combinational read can only be built from distributed (LUT) RAM or registers, which is much bigger at this size. Registering the read lets the tool use block RAM."],
["19-85", "M", "An 18 × 18-bit multiply written as a * b in FPGA RTL is normally implemented in:", ["A DSP slice", "The carry chain", "Block RAM", "An I/O cell"], "DSP slices are hard multiply-accumulate blocks. Using their internal pipeline registers lets a multiply run at the full clock rate."],
["19-86", "E", "The usual order of the FPGA implementation flow is:", ["Synthesis, placement, routing, timing analysis, bitstream generation", "Placement, synthesis, bitstream generation, routing, timing analysis", "Routing, placement, synthesis, timing analysis, bitstream generation", "Synthesis, bitstream generation, placement, routing, timing analysis"], "RTL becomes a netlist of FPGA primitives (synthesis), each primitive gets a site (placement), the wires are connected (routing), the result is checked against the constraints (timing), and finally the configuration file is written."],
["19-87", "M", "An FPGA path misses timing by 0.4 ns, with 7 LUT levels between two registers. The first fix to try is usually:", ["Add a pipeline register partway through the logic", "Lower the FPGA's core voltage", "Delay the capturing clock by routing it through a LUT", "Use LUTs with more inputs"], "Every LUT already has a spare flip-flop beside it, so pipelining is cheap on an FPGA: cutting 7 levels to two stages of 3 or 4 removes far more than 0.4 ns. Routing clocks through logic causes skew and is never the answer."],
["19-88", "M", "A reset net drives 20,000 flip-flops and fails timing. A standard fix is to:", ["Register and duplicate the reset, so each copy drives fewer loads", "Route the reset through block RAM", "Remove the reset synchronizer", "Generate the reset from a combinational OR of the clock enables"], "A huge fanout means a long, slow net. Replicated registers, each placed near its group of loads, split it into short nets. Better still, reset only the flip-flops that need it."],
["19-89", "M", "On an FPGA, the declaration logic [3:0] cnt = 4'd5; (an initializer):", ["Sets the register's power-up value, because configuration loads every flip-flop", "Is ignored by synthesis, just as on an ASIC", "Resets cnt to 5 on every clock edge", "Creates a latch"], "FPGA configuration writes a known value into every flip-flop, so initial values are real hardware there. On an ASIC they are ignored and a reset is needed."],
["19-90", "M", "In an FPGA constraints file, create_clock:", ["Tells timing analysis the period of a clock arriving on a port or net", "Builds a new clock signal inside the FPGA", "Sets the frequency of the board's oscillator", "Places the clock on a global buffer"], "Constraints describe, they do not build. create_clock tells the timing tools how fast a clock is so they can check every path it drives. New clocks are made by PLLs or MMCMs."],
["19-91", "H", "Two clocks in an FPGA design are unrelated, and the timing report shows failing paths between them. The right response is:", ["Check that each crossing uses a synchronizer or asynchronous FIFO, then declare the clocks asynchronous so those paths are not timed", "Raise both clock frequencies until the paths pass", "Add a multicycle constraint of 2 to the crossing paths and leave them unsynchronized", "Ignore the report, because crossing paths never fail in hardware"], "Paths between unrelated clocks can never meet timing, because the phase between the clocks is arbitrary. They must be made safe by design (Section 19.6) and then excluded with set_clock_groups -asynchronous. Excluding them without synchronizers hides real bugs."],
["19-92", "E", "On an FPGA, the programmable routing between logic blocks:", ["Is often more than half of each path's delay", "Is negligible compared with the LUT delay", "Has no delay, because the switches are already set", "Matters only for clock signals"], "Each connection passes through several programmable switches and wire segments, so routing typically dominates FPGA path delay. Keeping related logic close helps."],
["19-93", "M", "Low-latency trading firms use FPGAs mainly because they:", ["Can parse and act on network packets in hardware, without a CPU, in well under a microsecond", "Run at higher clock frequencies than CPUs", "Cost less per chip than CPUs at any volume", "Need no timing analysis"], "An FPGA can decode market data as the bytes arrive and send a response in tens to hundreds of nanoseconds, with no operating system, interrupts or cache misses in the path, and the logic can be changed whenever the strategy or the exchange's protocol changes."],
// 20 Architecture
["20-01","E","The stages of the classic 5-stage pipeline, in order, are:",["IF, ID, EX, MEM, WB","ID, IF, EX, WB, MEM","IF, EX, ID, MEM, WB","IF, ID, MEM, EX, WB"],"Fetch, decode and read registers, execute, access memory, write back.","r"],
["20-02","M","A load followed immediately by an instruction that uses its result needs:",["A one-cycle stall, even with forwarding","No stall, thanks to forwarding","A pipeline flush","Two cycles of branch delay"],"The loaded data arrives at the end of MEM, too late for the next instruction's EX."],
["20-03","M","Forwarding (bypassing) solves:",["Most read-after-write hazards","Most structural hazards","Most control hazards","Most cache misses"],"Results go straight from EX to the next instruction's EX input."],
["20-04","E","Control hazards are reduced mainly by:",["Branch prediction","Forwarding","Larger caches","More registers"],"Wrong guesses are flushed from the pipeline."],
["20-05","M","90% of a task can run in parallel. What is the speedup on 10 cores?",["About 5.3","10","9","About 1.9"],"1/(0.1 + 0.9/10) ≈ 5.3. Even unlimited cores give at most 10."],
["20-06","H","A 32 KB direct-mapped cache has 64-byte lines and 32-bit addresses. Its offset / index / tag widths are:",["6 / 9 / 17","6 / 10 / 16","5 / 9 / 18","6 / 8 / 18"],"512 lines need 9 index bits."],
["20-07","H","A 1 MB, 4-way set-associative cache has 64-byte lines and 32-bit addresses. Its tag is:",["14 bits","12 bits","16 bits","20 bits"],"16384 lines / 4 = 4096 sets, so 12 index bits; 32 − 12 − 6 = 14."],
["20-08","M","A cache has a 1 ns hit time, a 5% miss rate and a 100 ns miss penalty. What is the average memory access time?",["6 ns","5 ns","101 ns","1.05 ns"],"1 + 0.05 × 100 = 6 ns."],
["20-09","H","L1 takes 10 ns and hits 80%. L2 takes 30 ns and hits 40% of what reaches it. Memory takes 100 ns. What is the AMAT, if each miss adds the next level's time?",["28 ns","22.4 ns","40 ns","18 ns"],"10 + 0.2 × (30 + 0.6 × 100) = 28 ns."],
["20-10","M","Full associativity eliminates which kind of miss?",["Conflict","Compulsory","Capacity","Coherence"],"Addresses no longer compete for a single set."],
["20-11","M","0xA1B2C3D4 is stored at address 0 on a little-endian machine. Which byte is at address 0?",["D4","A1","B2","C3"],"Little-endian stores the least significant byte first."],
["20-12","M","How long do 2 × 10⁹ instructions at CPI 1.2 take on a 3 GHz processor?",["0.8 s","1.2 s","0.67 s","2.4 s"],"Instructions × CPI × clock period."],
["20-13","H","Non-pipelined: 2.5 GHz with CPI 5. Pipelined: 2 GHz with CPI 1. What is the throughput speedup?",["4×","5×","0.8×","2.5×"],"0.5 versus 2 billion instructions per second."],
["20-14","H","Base CPI is 1.0; 40% of instructions are loads or stores; their miss rate is 5%; a miss costs 100 cycles. What is the effective CPI?",["3.0","5.0","1.4","2.0"],"1 + 0.4 × 0.05 × 100 = 3.0."],
["20-15","E","A TLB caches:",["Virtual-to-physical translations","Recent branch targets","Recently used instructions","Dirty cache lines"],"It makes virtual memory fast."],
["20-16","E","A write-back cache marks modified lines with:",["A dirty bit","Only a valid bit","A parity bit","The LRU bit"],"Dirty lines are written back to memory when they are evicted."],
["20-17","M","A 2-bit saturating-counter branch predictor in the 'strongly taken' state must mispredict how many times in a row before it predicts 'not taken'?",["2","1","3","4"],"Strongly taken → weakly taken → weakly not taken."],
["20-18","M","In the MESI coherence protocol, the state meaning 'only this cache holds the line, and it has been modified' is:",["M (Modified)","E (Exclusive)","S (Shared)","I (Invalid)"],"E means the only copy and still clean; S means it may be in several caches."],
["20-19","M","Out-of-order processors use register renaming to remove:",["False dependences (WAR, WAW)","True dependences (RAW)","Cache misses on loads","Branch mispredictions"],"Each new result gets a fresh physical register."],
["20-20","E","A page table maps:",["Virtual page numbers to physical frame numbers","Physical addresses to cache lines","Instructions to opcodes","Threads to cores"],"The TLB caches recently used entries."],
["20-21","M","With 4 KB pages and 32-bit virtual addresses, how many virtual pages are there?",["2²⁰","2¹²","2³²","2¹⁶"],"12 offset bits leave 20 bits of page number."],
["20-22","E","A write-through cache differs from a write-back cache in that it:",["Updates memory on every write","Never updates main memory","Needs a dirty bit on every line","Is always faster on writes"],"A write-back cache updates memory only when it evicts a dirty line."],
["20-23","M","A program spends 60% of its time in code you make 3× faster. The overall speedup is about:",["1.67×","3×","1.8×","2×"],"1/(0.4 + 0.6/3) = 1/0.6 ≈ 1.67."],
["20-24","E","A structural hazard occurs when:",["Two instructions need the same resource at once","An instruction needs a result not yet ready","A branch goes the unpredicted way","A load misses in the cache"],"For example, a single memory port shared by instruction fetch and a load."],
["20-25","M","With total cache size fixed, using larger cache lines tends to:",["Cut compulsory misses but raise the miss penalty","Reduce every kind of miss with no downside","Increase the number of lines in the cache","Remove the need for address tags entirely"],"Fewer, larger lines exploit locality but cost more per miss."],
["20-26","M","On a roofline plot, a kernel is limited by memory bandwidth when:",["Its arithmetic intensity is below the balance point","Its arithmetic intensity is very high","Its clock frequency is above nominal","Its cache is disabled by the driver"],"Above the balance point, compute is the limit."],
["20-27","M","Little's law says that to sustain bandwidth B with memory latency L, the number of requests in flight must be about:",["B × L","B / L","L / B","B + L"],"For example 1 TB/s with 500 ns latency and 64-byte requests needs about (10¹² × 500×10⁻⁹)/64 ≈ 7,800 outstanding requests. That is why GPUs keep so many warps.","b"],
["20-28","M","A cycle-accurate performance model differs from an analytical model in that it:",["Simulates cycle by cycle, catching contention, but runs slowly","Uses only a formula, so it is instant but approximate","Runs on the real chip to measure performance","Ignores the memory system entirely"],"Architects use fast analytical models to explore and cycle-accurate models to confirm.","b"],
["20-29","M","IPC (instructions per cycle) and CPI are related by:",["IPC = 1 / CPI","IPC = CPI","IPC = CPI × clock frequency","IPC = CPI − 1"],"A processor finishing 2 instructions per cycle has CPI 0.5."],
["20-30","M","A CPI stack breaks the cycles per instruction into:",["Base cycles plus stalls from each cause","A list of the separate instructions","The pipeline stages and nothing else","The clock domains on the chip"],"It shows which bottleneck to attack first.","b"],
["20-31","E","On an AMBA AXI bus, the masters (managers) are:",["The components that start transactions, such as CPU cores and DMA engines","The memories and peripherals that answer requests","The interconnect's clock and reset generators","Whichever component has the fastest clock"],"A master issues reads and writes; a slave (subordinate), such as a memory controller or a UART, responds. The interconnect routes each request by address and arbitrates when several masters want the same slave. A DMA engine is a master on the bus it copies over, even though the CPU programs it through its slave registers."],
["20-32", "M", "In MESI, a core holds a line in the Exclusive state and writes to it. What coherence traffic is needed?", ["None: the line changes from E to M without telling anyone", "An invalidate sent to every other cache", "A write-back of the line to memory first", "A fresh read of the line from memory"], "Exclusive means no other cache has the line, so nobody needs to be told. That silent upgrade is the reason the E state exists."],
["20-33", "M", "A core wants to write a line it holds in the Shared state. It must first:", ["Invalidate the other caches' copies, then move to Modified", "Write the line back to memory", "Wait until the line is evicted", "Nothing: Shared lines may be written"], "Shared copies are read-only. Writing requires ownership, so the core broadcasts an invalidate (or sends it through the directory), every other copy becomes Invalid, and the line becomes Modified."],
["20-34", "M", "Another core reads a line that this core holds in the Modified state. Afterward:", ["The owner supplies the data (or writes it back) and both copies are Shared", "Both copies are Modified", "The reader gets the stale copy from memory", "The owner's copy becomes Exclusive"], "Only the Modified copy is correct, so it must be supplied to the reader and the line can no longer be owned privately: both end up Shared (in MOESI, the owner can keep a dirty Owned copy)."],
["20-35", "M", "Large many-core chips use directory-based coherence rather than snooping because:", ["Snooping sends every request to every cache, which does not scale with core count", "Directories need no storage", "Snooping cannot handle writes", "Directories remove the need for invalidations"], "A directory records which caches hold each line, so requests go only to those caches. Broadcasting to every cache on each miss saturates the interconnect as cores are added."],
["20-36", "H", "Two threads on different cores each increment their own counter, but the two counters sit in the same 64-byte cache line. The likely result is:", ["Slow progress, as the line bounces between the caches on every write (false sharing)", "Wrong counts, because coherence fails for neighboring variables", "No effect, because the threads share no data", "A deadlock between the two caches"], "Coherence works on whole lines, so each write invalidates the other core's copy. The answers stay correct, but every increment becomes a cache miss. Padding each counter to its own line fixes it."],
["20-37", "M", "In an out-of-order core, the reorder buffer:", ["Holds instructions in program order so their results become permanent in order", "Holds instructions that are waiting for their operands", "Predicts branch directions", "Replaces the register file"], "Instructions may finish in any order, but they retire from the head of the reorder buffer in program order, which makes speculation and precise exceptions possible."],
["20-38", "M", "An out-of-order core can run instructions past a predicted branch safely because:", ["Results become permanent only at in-order retirement, so wrong-path work can be thrown away", "Branch predictors are always right inside loops", "It executes both paths and keeps both results", "It stops fetching until the branch resolves"], "On a misprediction, every instruction younger than the branch is still unretired in the reorder buffer and is simply discarded, with no effect on the architectural state."],
["20-39", "E", "In an out-of-order core, an instruction issues from the reservation stations when:", ["Its operands are ready and an execution unit is free", "Every older instruction has retired", "The branch predictor approves it", "It reaches the head of the reorder buffer"], "Issue is driven by data readiness, not program order. Retirement is what happens in order."],
["20-40", "M", "Register renaming maps each new write of an architectural register to:", ["A fresh physical register from a larger pool", "The same register, after a stall", "A location in the L1 cache", "The entry at the head of the reorder buffer"], "Giving every write its own physical register removes write-after-read and write-after-write hazards, leaving only true read-after-write dependences."],
["20-41", "M", "On an AXI channel, a transfer happens on a clock edge where:", ["VALID and READY are both high", "VALID is high, whatever READY is", "READY goes from low to high", "VALID goes from high to low"], "Every AXI channel is a valid/ready handshake: the source offers with VALID, the destination accepts with READY, and data moves only on an edge where both are high."],
["20-42", "M", "Which AXI rule prevents a handshake deadlock?", ["A source must not wait for READY before asserting VALID", "A destination must assert READY before VALID", "VALID may be withdrawn before the transfer if the data changes", "READY and VALID must rise in the same cycle"], "If the source waited for READY and the destination waited for VALID, neither would move. So the source raises VALID as soon as it has data and holds it, with the data stable, until the transfer; the destination may wait for VALID."],
["20-43", "E", "AXI4-Stream is used to:", ["Pass a stream of data between blocks with no addresses, marking packet ends with TLAST", "Read and write control registers one word at a time", "Connect a CPU to DRAM with bursts and transaction IDs", "Carry the clock between chips"], "AXI4-Stream keeps only the handshake, the data and TLAST. It is the usual way to move packets through an FPGA pipeline. AXI4-Lite is the register-access version, full AXI4 the memory-mapped one with bursts."],
["20-44", "M", "A 2-bit saturating counter predicts a loop branch that is taken 9 times, then not taken once, and the loop runs again and again. In steady state, how many mispredictions happen per run of the loop?", ["1", "2", "0", "10"], "The single not-taken exit only moves the counter from 3 to 2, still predicting taken, so only the exit itself is mispredicted. A 1-bit predictor would mispredict twice: the exit, then the first iteration of the next run."],
["20-45", "M", "A core has a base CPI of 1.0. 25% of instructions are branches, the predictor is 96% accurate, and a misprediction costs 12 cycles. What is the CPI?", ["1.12", "1.48", "1.03", "1.30"], "Mispredictions per instruction = 0.25 × 0.04 = 0.01, each costing 12 cycles: CPI = 1 + 0.12 = 1.12."],
["20-46", "E", "The branch target buffer (BTB) stores:", ["The target address of recently taken branches, so fetch can redirect immediately", "The outcome counters of every branch in the program", "The return addresses of function calls only", "The instructions on the wrong path after a misprediction"], "The direction predictor says taken or not; the BTB says where to. Returns are predicted by a separate return address stack."],
["20-47", "M", "A stride prefetcher helps most when the program:", ["Walks an array of structs, touching one field in each", "Follows a linked list whose nodes are scattered in memory", "Reads a single variable over and over", "Makes random lookups into a large hash table"], "Accesses at a constant distance are easy to predict and fetch ahead. Pointer chasing and random lookups give the prefetcher nothing to learn from."],
["20-48", "E", "A 512-bit SIMD register can add how many 32-bit integers in one instruction?", ["16", "8", "32", "64"], "512 / 32 = 16 lanes. SIMD applies one operation to every lane at once."],
["20-49", "H", "Core 0 runs data = 42; flag = 1;. Core 1 waits until flag == 1, then reads data. On a weakly ordered processor (Arm, RISC-V) with no barriers, core 1:", ["Can read the old value of data, because the two stores may become visible in either order", "Always reads 42, because cache coherence orders all memory accesses", "Can never see flag == 1", "Reads 42 only if both variables share a cache line"], "Coherence orders writes to one address; the order of writes to different addresses is set by the consistency model. A release barrier before the flag store and an acquire barrier after the flag load fix it."],
["20-50", "M", "On x86 (total store order), which reordering can a core's own memory operations show to other cores?", ["A load can complete before an earlier store to a different address is visible", "Two stores can become visible in the opposite order", "Two loads can complete in the opposite order", "None: x86 is sequentially consistent"], "Stores wait in the store buffer while later loads go ahead. That is the one relaxation TSO allows; an mfence (or a locked instruction) prevents it."],
["20-51", "E", "Simultaneous multithreading (SMT) improves throughput by:", ["Letting a second thread use execution slots left idle when one thread stalls", "Giving each thread its own private set of execution units", "Doubling the clock frequency of the core", "Running the same thread twice and comparing the results"], "Both threads share one core's execution units; when one waits on memory, the other's instructions fill the gaps."],
// 21 GPUs and number formats
["21-01","E","How many threads are in an NVIDIA warp?",["32","16","64","1024"],"All 32 execute the same instruction together (SIMT)."],
["21-02","E","Which best contrasts CPUs with GPUs?",["CPUs cut latency; GPUs maximize throughput","GPUs have bigger caches per thread","CPUs have thousands of simple cores","GPUs predict branches much better"],"GPUs spend their transistors on many arithmetic units."],
["21-03","M","Half the threads in a warp take the if branch and half take the else. During that code, throughput is about:",["50% of peak","100% of peak","25% of peak","200% of peak"],"Both paths run one after the other, with half the lanes masked off each time."],
["21-04","M","If thread i reads a[i × 64] (4-byte floats), the warp's memory accesses are well coalesced.",false,"Neighbouring threads are 256 bytes apart, so each needs its own memory transaction."],
["21-05","H","How many floating-point operations does multiplying two 1024 × 1024 matrices take?",["About 2.15 × 10⁹","About 1.07 × 10⁹","About 1.05 × 10⁶","About 3.2 × 10¹⁰"],"2MNK = 2 × 1024³."],
["21-06","H","In FP32, y[i] = a × x[i] + y[i] is:",["Memory-bound (~0.17 FLOP/byte)","Compute-bound (~6 FLOP/byte)","Latency-bound (one long chain)","Branch-bound (divergent warps)"],"2 FLOPs for every 12 bytes moved."],
["21-07","H","The FP32 bit pattern 0x40400000 represents:",["3.0","2.0","1.5","4.0"],"Sign 0, exponent 128 − 127 = 1, significand 1.5, so 1.5 × 2 = 3.0.","a"],
["21-08","M","BF16 keeps which part of FP32?",["Its 8-bit exponent (same range)","Its 23-bit fraction (same precision)","Neither its exponent nor its fraction","Both its exponent and its fraction"],"BF16 has 7 fraction bits: less precision, same range.","a"],
["21-09","M","FP8 E4M3 divides its 8 bits as:",["1 sign, 4 exponent, 3 fraction","1 sign, 3 exponent, 4 fraction","4 exponent, 4 fraction","2 sign, 3 exponent, 3 fraction"],"E5M2 trades precision for more range.","a"],
["21-10","M","A GPU hides long memory latency mainly by:",["Switching to other ready warps","A large branch predictor","Out-of-order execution of one thread","Bigger L1 caches alone"],"Every resident warp's registers stay on chip, so switching costs nothing."],
["21-11","H","A GPU delivers 100 TFLOP/s and 2 TB/s. Its balance point is:",["50 FLOP per byte","200 FLOP per byte","0.02 FLOP per byte","2 FLOP per byte"],"Kernels with lower arithmetic intensity are memory-bound."],
["21-12","M","In CUDA, a thread's global index in a 1-D launch is:",["blockIdx.x × blockDim.x + threadIdx.x","threadIdx.x × blockDim.x + blockIdx.x","blockIdx.x × gridDim.x + threadIdx.x","blockDim.x × threadIdx.x + blockIdx.x"],"Guard it with if (i < n) for the last block."],
["21-13","E","Tensor cores accelerate:",["Small matrix multiply-accumulates","Branch prediction for warps","DRAM refresh scheduling","Texture filtering only"],"They compute D = A × B + C on small tiles in one instruction."],
["21-14","M","FP16 has the same numeric range as FP32.",false,"FP16 has only a 5-bit exponent, so a much smaller range. BF16 keeps FP32's range.","a"],
["21-15","E","In CUDA, __syncthreads() synchronizes:",["All threads in one block","All threads on the GPU","One warp of 32 threads","The CPU and the GPU"],"Threads in different blocks cannot synchronize with it."],
["21-16","E","CUDA shared memory is:",["Fast on-chip memory for one block","Off-chip DRAM for all blocks","The CPU's main memory","An on-chip instruction cache"],"Kernels tile data into shared memory to reuse it many times."],
["21-17","H","Shared memory has 32 banks of 4-byte words. Each thread i of a warp reads float s[2 × i]. How many threads hit each bank that is used?",["2 (a 2-way conflict)","1 (no conflict)","32 (fully serialized)","16 (a 16-way conflict)"],"Word 2i falls in bank (2i mod 32), so only the 16 even banks are used, two threads each.","b"],
["21-18","M","Occupancy is the ratio of:",["Active warps to the SM's maximum","Used memory to total memory","Busy cycles to total cycles","Threads to blocks in a launch"],"Register and shared-memory use per thread limit it."],
["21-19","M","A kernel that uses many registers per thread can:",["Lower occupancy, hiding less latency","Raise occupancy, hiding more latency","Leave scheduling exactly unchanged","Disable the L1 cache for that kernel"],"The register file is divided among all resident threads."],
["21-20","M","INT8 quantization speeds up neural-network inference mainly because:",["Smaller values cut bandwidth and allow more operations per cycle","It is more accurate than FP32 for every network layer","It removes the need for matrix multiplication altogether","It runs only on CPUs, which avoids slow PCIe transfers"],"Four INT8 values fit in the space of one FP32."],
["21-21","H","Multiplying two N × N matrices does 2N³ operations but moves only about 3N² values. As N grows, the kernel becomes:",["More compute-bound","More memory-bound","Exactly balanced","More latency-bound"],"Arithmetic intensity grows with N, which is why large matrix multiplies suit GPUs and tensor cores."],
["21-22","E","CUDA's execution hierarchy, from smallest to largest, is:",["Thread, warp, block, grid","Block, thread, grid, warp","Grid, block, warp, thread","Warp, thread, grid, block"],"A warp is a group of 32 threads within a block."],
["21-26","M","The exponent bias in IEEE 754 FP32 is:",["127","128","255","1023"],"FP64 uses 1023.","a"],
["21-27","M","FP32 stores 23 fraction bits. Its precision is about:",["7 decimal digits","3 decimal digits","16 decimal digits","32 decimal digits"],"2⁻²⁴ ≈ 6 × 10⁻⁸.","a"],
["21-28","M","A grid-stride loop in a CUDA kernel  for (i = idx; i < n; i += blockDim.x * gridDim.x)  lets:",["A fixed-size grid to cover an array of any length","Each thread to run exactly once and stop","Threads to skip all synchronization","The kernel to use less shared memory"],"Each thread handles several elements, one grid-width apart, keeping accesses coalesced.","b"],
["21-29","M","cudaMemcpy from host to device is often the bottleneck because:",["PCIe is far slower than GPU memory","GPUs cannot read host memory at all","It runs on the GPU's slowest core","It always copies the data twice"],"Keep data on the GPU across kernels, and overlap copies with compute using streams and pinned memory.","b"],
["21-30","M","CUDA streams allow:",["Copies and kernels to overlap in time","Threads in a warp to diverge freely","Kernels to run on the CPU instead","Shared memory to grow beyond its limit"],"Work in different streams can run concurrently, hiding transfer time.","b"],
["21-31","H","A warp shuffle (__shfl_down_sync) lets threads:",["Swap register values within a warp, no shared memory","Move warps from one SM to another","Write into another block's shared memory","Reorder global memory transactions"],"Warp-level reductions with shuffles avoid shared-memory traffic and many __syncthreads() calls.","b"],
["21-32","M","Profiling a CUDA kernel with Nsight Compute mainly tells you:",["Whether memory, compute or latency limits it","How much the GPU costs to buy","The chip's die size in square millimetres","The number of lines in the source code"],"Profile first, then optimize the measured bottleneck: the roofline idea in practice.","b"],
// 22 Computer arithmetic
["22-01","M","In unsigned Q4.4 fixed point (4 integer bits, 4 fraction bits), the pattern 0010 1100 represents:",["2.75","44","2.12","11"],"0010 is 2, and 1100 is 12/16 = 0.75.","a"],
["22-02","M","Saturating 8-bit unsigned arithmetic computes 200 + 100 as:",["255","44","300","0"],"Instead of wrapping to 300 mod 256 = 44, the result clamps at the maximum. DSP and graphics hardware use this.","a"],
["22-03","H","Radix-4 Booth recoding speeds up a multiplier mainly by:",["Roughly halving the number of partial products","Removing the final carry-propagate adder","Switching the multiplier to floating point","Avoiding signed numbers in the partial products"],"Each overlapping group of 3 multiplier bits selects 0, ±1 or ±2 times the multiplicand, so an n-bit multiplier needs about n/2 partial products.","a"],
["22-04","M","The IEEE 754 default rounding mode, which rounds to the nearest representable value and breaks ties toward an even significand, is:",["Round to nearest, ties to even","Round toward zero (truncation)","Round to nearest, ties away from zero","Round toward +∞ (ceiling)"],"Ties to even avoids a systematic bias in long computations.","a"],
["22-05","H","FP32 subnormal (denormal) numbers exist to:",["Fill the gap near zero (gradual underflow)","Represent positive and negative infinity","Represent NaN results such as 0/0","Speed up division by small numbers"],"They have an exponent field of 0 and no implicit leading 1.","a"],
["22-06","H","To add two floating-point numbers, the hardware first:",["Aligns the significands by shifting the smaller one","Multiplies the two exponents together","Adds the two exponents together","Rounds both inputs to the nearest integer"],"Then it adds the significands, normalizes, and rounds.","a"],
["22-07","E","In 8-bit unsigned arithmetic, 200 + 100 sets which flag?",["The carry flag (C)","The overflow flag (V)","The zero flag (Z)","No flag at all"],"300 does not fit in 0–255, so there is a carry out of the MSB and the 8-bit result wraps to 44. For unsigned numbers, the carry is the overflow signal.","a"],
["22-08","M","Adding −1 and −1 in 8 bits (1111 1111 + 1111 1111) produces:",["A carry out, but no signed overflow (V = 0)","A signed overflow (V = 1) and no carry","Neither a carry nor an overflow","A result of 0 with V = 1"],"The result 1111 1110 = −2 is correct as a signed number. A carry goes into and out of the sign bit, so V = 1 XOR 1 = 0. The carry flag is not the overflow flag.","a"],
["22-09","M","Signed 8-bit 100 + 50 with saturating arithmetic gives:",["127","−106","150","−128"],"150 is above the signed maximum, so saturation clamps it to 127. Wrapping arithmetic would give −106.","a"],
["22-10","M","−7 (8-bit two's complement) arithmetically shifted right by 1 gives:",["−4","−3","−3.5","124"],"1111 1001 shifted right with the sign bit copied in is 1111 1100 = −4. An arithmetic shift rounds toward −∞, unlike C's −7/2 = −3.","a"],
["22-11","E","How many layers of 2:1 multiplexers does a 32-bit barrel shifter need?",["5","32","16","1"],"log₂ 32 = 5 layers, shifting by 16, 8, 4, 2 and 1.","a"],
["22-12","M","Which shift-and-add expression computes 10x?",["(x << 3) + (x << 1)","(x << 3) + x","(x << 2) + (x << 1)","(x << 10)"],"8x + 2x = 10x. (x << 3) + x is 9x, and (x << 2) + (x << 1) is 6x.","a"],
["22-13","H","In a carry-lookahead adder, the carry into bit 3 (c₃) is:",["G2 + P2·G1 + P2·P1·G0 + P2·P1·P0·c0","G2 + P2·G1 + P1·G0 + P0·c0","G2 + P2·G1 + P2·P1·G0 + P0·c0","G2·P2 + G1·P1 + G0·P0 + c0"],"Expand c₃ = G2 + P2·c₂ with c₂ = G1 + P1·G0 + P1·P0·c0.","a"],
["22-14","M","A carry-save adder takes three numbers and produces:",["Two numbers (a sum and a carry vector) after one full-adder delay","One final sum, after the carry ripples through every bit","Three partial sums, one for each of its three inputs","The product of the three numbers, after a short tree"],"No carry moves sideways, so the delay does not depend on the width. Only the final addition of the two vectors needs a carry chain.","a"],
["22-15","H","A carry-save adder adds 9, 5 and 3. Its sum vector and carry vector are:",["15 and 2","17 and 0","14 and 3","12 and 5"],"Sum vector = 1001 ⊕ 0101 ⊕ 0011 = 1111 = 15. Carry vector = majority 0001 shifted left = 2. 15 + 2 = 17. (Every option adds to 17; only one is what the hardware produces.)","a"],
["22-16","E","Multiplying a 16-bit number by an 8-bit number needs a product of how many bits?",["24","16","8","128"],"n × m bits gives up to n + m bits.","a"],
["22-17","H","Radix-4 Booth recoding turns the multiplier 0110 (6) into which digits (d1, d0)?",["(+2, −2)","(+1, +1)","(+2, +2)","(−2, +2)"],"Group 0 = (b1, b0, b−1) = (1, 0, 0) gives −2. Group 1 = (b3, b2, b1) = (0, 1, 1) gives +2. Check: 2 × 4 − 2 = 6.","a"],
["22-18","M","A Wallace or Dadda tree speeds up a multiplier by:",["Reducing the partial products with layers of carry-save adders","Removing the need to form partial products at all","Adding the partial products one by one with ripple adders","Replacing the multiplication with a division"],"Each layer turns three rows into two, so n rows shrink to two in about log₁.₅ n layers before one fast adder finishes.","a"],
["22-19","M","Roughly how does a multiplier's area grow with its operand width n?",["About n² (double the width, four times the area)","About n (double the width, double the area)","About log n","It does not grow"],"There are about n² partial-product bits to form and add. That is why narrow formats like BF16 and FP8 make AI hardware so much smaller.","a"],
["22-20","E","Why is integer division slower than multiplication in hardware?",["Each quotient bit depends on the previous step's remainder","It must convert both numbers to floating point first","Dividers cannot reuse adders, so they need far more gates","It needs a carry-save tree twice as deep as a multiplier"],"Long division is inherently sequential: one quotient bit (or a few) per step.","a"],
["22-21","M","Restoring division of 13 by 3 gives:",["Quotient 4, remainder 1","Quotient 3, remainder 4","Quotient 4, remainder 0","Quotient 5, remainder 2"],"13 = 4 × 3 + 1. The remainder must always be smaller than the divisor.","a"],
["22-22","H","For any 16-bit unsigned x, x / 3 can be computed exactly as:",["(x × 43691) >> 17","(x × 3) >> 1","(x >> 2) + (x >> 4)","x − (x >> 2)"],"43691 ≈ 2¹⁷/3, so the multiply-and-shift computes x/3 rounded down for every 16-bit x. Compilers replace division by a constant this way.","a"],
["22-23","M","In signed Q1.7 fixed point, 0.3 is stored as the integer:",["38","30","3","77"],"0.3 × 2⁷ = 38.4, which rounds to 38. The stored value is 38/128 = 0.296875.","a"],
["22-24","M","A Q2.6 number times a Q3.5 number gives a full product in which format?",["Q5.11","Q6.10","Q5.6","Q2.5"],"Integer bits add (2 + 3) and fraction bits add (6 + 5).","a"],
["22-25","E","The resolution (smallest step) of Q4.4 fixed point is:",["0.0625","0.25","0.5","0.004"],"2⁻⁴ = 1/16 = 0.0625.","a"],
["22-26","M","In FP32 multiplication, the result's exponent field is computed as:",["e1 + e2 − 127","e1 + e2","e1 × e2 − 127","The larger of e1 and e2"],"Each field carries a bias of 127, so adding them counts the bias twice; subtract one. Normalization may then add 1.","a"],
["22-27","M","3.0 × 2.5 in FP32: both exponent fields are 128. The product's exponent field is:",["129","256","128","130"],"128 + 128 − 127 = 129, meaning 2². The significands give 1.5 × 1.25 = 1.875, already below 2, so the result is 1.875 × 2² = 7.5.","a"],
["22-28","M","An FP32 exponent field of all 1s (255) with a nonzero fraction means:",["NaN","Infinity","A subnormal number","The largest normal number"],"Exponent 255 with fraction 0 is ±∞; with a nonzero fraction it is NaN.","a"],
["22-29","E","In IEEE floating point, 0/0 gives:",["NaN","0","+∞","An error that stops the program"],"Undefined results such as 0/0 and ∞ − ∞ give NaN, which then spreads through later operations.","a"],
["22-30","H","In FP32, (10⁸ + 1) − 10⁸ evaluates to:",["0","1","8","10⁸"],"Near 10⁸, FP32 numbers are spaced 8 apart, so 10⁸ + 1 rounds back to 10⁸. Computing (10⁸ − 10⁸) + 1 instead gives 1: order matters.","a"],
["22-31","M","Floating-point addition is associative: (a + b) + c always equals a + (b + c).",false,"Each operation rounds, so the order changes the result. GPU reductions that add in a different order than a CPU loop can differ in the last bits.","a"],
["22-32","M","A fused multiply-add (FMA) computes a × b + c:",["With a single rounding at the end","With two roundings, one per operation","With no rounding at all","Only for integer inputs"],"One rounding is both faster (one instruction) and more accurate. GPU floating-point units and tensor cores are built around it.","a"],
["22-33","M","FP32's machine epsilon (the gap between 1.0 and the next larger number) is about:",["1.2 × 10⁻⁷","1.2 × 10⁻³⁸","1.4 × 10⁻⁴⁵","2.2 × 10⁻¹⁶"],"2⁻²³ ≈ 1.2 × 10⁻⁷: about 7 decimal digits. 1.2 × 10⁻³⁸ is the smallest normal number, 1.4 × 10⁻⁴⁵ the smallest subnormal, and 2.2 × 10⁻¹⁶ is FP64's epsilon.","a"],
["22-34","H","During floating-point addition, the guard, round and sticky bits exist to:",["Round correctly after the alignment shift pushes bits off the end","Detect NaN inputs before the addition starts","Hold the sign of the result during normalization","Speed up the comparison of the two exponents"],"They keep just enough information about the discarded bits to round as if the exact result were known.","a"],
["22-35","M","Tensor cores often multiply in BF16 or FP8 but accumulate in FP32 because:",["A long sum in a narrow format would lose small terms to rounding","FP32 multipliers are smaller than BF16 multipliers","FP32 has fewer exponent bits than BF16","Accumulating in FP32 removes the need for multipliers"],"Narrow multipliers are small and fast; a wide accumulator keeps the small products from vanishing in the sum.","a"],
["22-36","M","INT8 quantization represents a real value as approximately:",["scale × (q − zero point)","q × 2^exponent (like floating point)","q / 127 (a fixed range of ±1)","q + scale (an offset only)"],"The scale (and zero point) is chosen per tensor or per channel so the 256 integer levels cover the values that actually occur.","a"],
// 23 Programming, Linux and scripting
["23-01","M","In C, what does the keyword volatile tell the compiler about a variable such as a memory-mapped register?",["Every access must really happen; the value can change by itself","The variable is kept in a CPU register for speed","The variable can never change after it is set","The variable is shared between source files"],"Without volatile, the compiler may keep a register's value in a CPU register and skip later reads, or merge writes, which breaks hardware access."],
["23-02","M","On a 32-bit int, what is undefined behaviour in C?",["Shifting by 32 or more bits","Shifting an unsigned value right by 1","Masking with 0xFF","Adding two unsigned ints that overflow"],"A shift count of at least the type's width is undefined. Unsigned overflow, by contrast, is well defined: it wraps modulo 2ⁿ."],
["23-03","M","What does this print?  int x = 5; int *p = &x; *p += 2; printf(\"%d\", x);",["7","5","2","An address"],"p points to x, so *p += 2 changes x itself."],
["23-04","M","A function-local variable declared static:",["Keeps its value between calls","Is re-created on every call","Is visible to every file","Is stored on the stack"],"Static locals live for the whole program, are initialized once, and stay private to the function."],
["23-05","H","With #define SQUARE(x) x * x, what is SQUARE(1 + 2)?",["5","9","6","3"],"It expands to 1 + 2 * 1 + 2 = 5. Macro arguments should be parenthesized: #define SQUARE(x) ((x) * (x))."],
["23-06","M","For int a[10], what is sizeof(a) / sizeof(a[0])?",["10","40","4","1"],"40 bytes divided by 4 bytes per element gives the element count. It does not work on a pointer parameter, where sizeof gives the pointer size."],
["23-07","E","In Python, what is 0b1011 ^ 0b0110?",["13 (0b1101)","15 (0b1111)","2 (0b0010)","−12"],"Bitwise XOR: 1011 ⊕ 0110 = 1101 = 13.","b"],
["23-08","E","Python integers:",["Grow to any size, so they never overflow","Are always 32 bits and wrap around","Are always 64 bits and saturate","Are floating point"],"That is handy for wide hardware values, but you must mask explicitly to model fixed widths: x & 0xFFFF.","b"],
["23-09","M","Which Python expression gives the even numbers below 10?",["[i for i in range(10) if i % 2 == 0]","[i if i % 2 for i in range(10)]","range(10).filter(even)","[i % 2 == 0 for i in range(10)]"],"A list comprehension with an if clause filters. The last option produces a list of booleans.","b"],
["23-10","M","In Python, int('0x1F', 16) evaluates to:",["31","An error because of the 0x prefix","1F","16"],"With base 16, int accepts an optional 0x prefix. int('0x1F', 0) also works, guessing the base from the prefix.","b"],
["23-11","M","Which data structure gives average O(1) lookup by key in Python?",["dict","list","tuple","a sorted list"],"A dict is a hash table. Counting errors per module or mapping net names to drivers uses dicts.","b"],
["23-12","E","In a Linux shell, what does grep -r \"FAILED\" logs/ do?",["Searches all files under logs/ for FAILED","Removes lines containing FAILED","Renames files containing FAILED","Counts the files inside logs/"],"-r recurses into directories. Add -l to list only file names, or -c to count matches per file.","b"],
["23-13","E","What does the pipe in  cat sim.log | grep ERROR | wc -l  do?",["Sends each command's output into the next command's input","Runs the three commands at the same time on separate files","Saves the output to a file named wc","Comments out the rest of the line"],"This counts ERROR lines. (grep -c ERROR sim.log does the same in one command.)","b"],
["23-14","M","What does  ./simv > run.log 2>&1  do?",["Sends both normal output and error output to run.log","Sends only errors to run.log","Runs simv twice","Appends to run.log instead of overwriting"],"2>&1 points stderr at wherever stdout goes. Use >> to append instead of overwrite.","b"],
["23-15","M","chmod +x run.sh is needed so that:",["The script can be executed as ./run.sh","The script can be read by others","The script runs in the background","The script is deleted on logout"],"Execute permission lets the kernel run the file; the first line (#!/usr/bin/env bash) picks the interpreter.","b"],
["23-16","M","Which command finds every .v file under the current directory?",["find . -name \"*.v\"","ls -r *.v","grep *.v","locate --here .v"],"find walks the directory tree; quote the pattern so the shell does not expand it first.","b"],
["23-17","M","An EDA tool is not found when you type its name. The most likely fix is:",["Add its directory to PATH","Reboot the computer","Run it with sudo","Rename the tool"],"The shell searches the directories listed in PATH. Chip-design environments often set PATH and license variables through a setup script or modules.","b"],
["23-18","M","Which tool is best for printing the third whitespace-separated column of a report?",["awk '{print $3}' report.txt","sed -n 3p report.txt","cut -c3 report.txt","head -3 report.txt"],"awk splits lines into fields. cut -c3 would print the third character, and head -3 the first three lines.","b"],
["23-19","M","In git, what is a branch?",["A movable pointer to a commit for separate work","A full copy of the repository on disk","A backup copy made by GitHub","A list of files that git ignores"],"Branches are cheap. Work on a branch, then merge it after review.","b"],
["23-20","M","git merge differs from git rebase in that rebase:",["Replays your commits onto the other branch","Deletes the other branch after merging","Creates a merge commit with two parents","Uploads the branch to GitHub"],"Rebase gives a straight-line history. Never rebase commits that others already pulled.","b"],
["23-21","E","Which command shows what changed in your files but is not yet staged?",["git diff","git log","git status --short --staged","git push"],"git diff compares the working tree with the staging area; git diff --staged shows what will be committed.","b"],
["23-22","M","In a Makefile, a recipe line must start with:",["A tab character","Four spaces","A dollar sign","A colon"],"make requires a real tab; spaces give the error 'missing separator'.","b"],
["23-23","M","make decides to rebuild a target when:",["Any prerequisite file is newer than the target","The target is older than one day","Every time make runs","The Makefile is newer than the computer's clock"],"Timestamp comparison lets large builds redo only what changed. Targets that are not files are marked .PHONY.","b"],
["23-24","M","In Tcl, what does  set x [expr {3 * 4}]  do?",["Runs expr and stores 12 in x","Stores the text '[expr {3 * 4}]' in x","Prints 12","Defines a procedure named x"],"Square brackets run a command and substitute its result. Braces around expr's argument keep it from being substituted twice.","b"],
["23-25","M","In Tcl, the difference between \"$a b\" and {$a b} is that:",["Double quotes substitute variables; braces keep the text literally","Braces substitute variables; double quotes do not","They are identical","Braces create a list of numbers"],"Braces defer substitution, which is why proc bodies and expr arguments are written in braces.","b"],
["23-26","M","Most EDA tools (synthesis, place and route, static timing) are scripted in:",["Tcl","JavaScript","SQL","HTML"],"Commands like create_clock and report_timing are Tcl commands. SDC constraint files are Tcl too.","b"],
["23-27","M","In Perl, $count{$m}++ does what?",["Adds 1 to the hash entry for key $m","Appends $m to the end of an array","Prints the value of $m","Compares $count with $m"],"% marks a hash, @ an array and $ a single value. Hash counting is a classic log-processing idiom.","b"],
["23-28","M","The regular expression ^ERROR\\s+\\[(\\w+)\\] captures:",["The module name in brackets after ERROR","Every word in the line","The text after the closing bracket","Lines that do not contain ERROR"],"^ anchors at the start, \\s+ matches spaces, \\[ is a literal bracket and (\\w+) captures word characters.","b"],
["23-29","M","Why do hardware engineers write Python or Perl scripts?",["To automate regressions and parse logs and reports","Because RTL cannot be simulated without them","To replace the synthesis tools entirely","Only to make plots of results"],"A large share of day-to-day chip work is automation: running thousands of tests, triaging failures and tracking timing and power trends.","b"],
["23-30","H","A regression of 5,000 tests runs on a compute farm. The usual way to submit them is:",["A job scheduler such as LSF or Slurm","Opening 5,000 terminal windows","Running them one by one on a laptop","Emailing each test to a colleague"],"Farm schedulers queue jobs across many machines; scripts submit jobs and collect results.","b"],
["23-31","E","Which file lists files that git should not track, such as simulation outputs?",[".gitignore",".gitkeep","README.md","Makefile"],"Waveforms and build directories are large and regenerable, so they stay out of the repository.","b"],
["23-32","M","A Python script parses a 2 GB log. Which approach uses the least memory?",["Iterate over the file line by line","Read the whole file with f.read() first","Load it into a list with f.readlines()","Copy it into a string, then split"],"for line in f: streams the file, holding one line at a time.","b"],
["23-33","H","Two shell commands: A is  grep -c PASS log ; B is  grep PASS log | wc -l . When does the answer differ?",["Never; both count matching lines","A counts words, B counts lines","B counts files instead of lines","A only works on compressed logs"],"grep -c counts matching lines per file; with a single file it equals the piped wc -l count.","b"],
["23-34","M","In C, which declaration reads a 32-bit hardware status register at address 0x40001000 correctly?",["volatile uint32_t *status = (volatile uint32_t *)0x40001000;","uint32_t status = (uint32_t)0x40001000;","int *status = (int *)0x40001000;","const uint8_t *status = (const uint8_t *)0x40001000;"],"Casting the address to a pointer to volatile 32-bit data makes every access a real 32-bit read."],
// 28 Design verification
["28-01", "E", "Why is a verification plan written from the specification rather than from the RTL?", ["Checks copied from the RTL repeat the designer's mistakes, so the bug passes", "The RTL is not available until after tape-out", "Simulators cannot read RTL files directly", "Coverage tools only accept specification documents"], "A testbench must check what the design should do. Reading the RTL to write the checks builds the same misunderstanding into both."],
["28-02", "E", "In a layered testbench, which component turns transactions into pin activity?", ["The driver", "The monitor", "The scoreboard", "The sequence"], "The sequence decides what to send, the driver wiggles the pins, the monitor turns pins back into transactions, and the scoreboard checks them."],
["28-03", "M", "Why does a testbench check the monitor's transactions rather than what the driver intended to send?", ["The monitor reports what really appeared on the pins, catching driver bugs and working unchanged when another block drives the interface", "Monitors run faster than drivers", "Drivers cannot store transactions", "The scoreboard can only connect to one component"], "The monitor is an independent observer. It also keeps working in a passive agent when the block is reused at a higher level."],
["28-04", "E", "A logic variable is 4-state and an int is 2-state. What happens if an X from the design is assigned to an int?", ["It silently becomes 0, which can hide a bug", "The simulation stops with an error", "The int also holds X", "The assignment is skipped"], "2-state types have no X or Z. Use 4-state types for anything connected to the design, so unknown values stay visible."],
["28-05", "M", "fork ... join_any continues when:", ["The first child thread finishes; the others keep running", "All child threads finish", "Immediately, without waiting for any child", "Every child thread has been killed"], "join waits for all, join_any for the first, join_none for none. disable fork then stops the rest, a common timeout pattern."],
["28-06", "M", "packet p1 = new(); packet p2 = p1; p2.addr = 5; What is p1.addr?", ["5, because p1 and p2 are handles to the same object", "Its old value, because p2 is a copy", "0, because p2 = p1 resets the object", "Undefined until p1 is randomized again"], "Assigning a class handle copies the handle, not the object. new p1 would make a separate (shallow) copy."],
["28-07", "M", "A base-class handle points at a derived-class object and calls a method that the derived class overrides. Which version runs?", ["The derived version if the method is declared virtual; otherwise the base version", "Always the base version", "Always the derived version", "Neither: it is a compile error"], "Virtual methods are chosen by the object's type (polymorphism); non-virtual methods by the handle's type."],
["28-08", "M", "Which constraint keeps a 32-bit address word-aligned?", ["constraint c { addr[1:0] == 2'b00; }", "constraint c { addr % 2 == 0; }", "constraint c { addr inside {[0:3]}; }", "constraint c { addr[31:30] == 2'b00; }"], "A 4-byte-aligned address has its two lowest bits zero. addr % 4 == 0 would also work."],
["28-09", "H", "In len dist { 1 := 50, [2:5] :/ 40, 6 := 10 }, what is the weight of the value 3?", ["10", "40", "50", "13.3"], "':/' divides the weight 40 across the four values 2 to 5, giving 10 each. With ':=' each value would get 40."],
["28-10", "M", "What does solve a before b change?", ["The probability distribution of the solutions, not which values are legal", "Which values of b are legal", "The order in which a and b are printed", "It makes a and b independent of each other"], "Without it the solver picks uniformly among legal (a, b) pairs, which can make a rare value of a almost never appear. solve before picks a first, evenly."],
["28-11", "E", "Why should the return value of randomize() always be checked?", ["It returns 0 when the constraints conflict, leaving the old values in place", "It returns the random seed, which must be logged", "It returns the number of constraints used", "It is required syntax; the code does not compile otherwise"], "An unchecked failure means the test silently repeats the previous values and may never reach the scenario it was meant to test."],
["28-12", "E", "Running the same random test with the same seed and the same code:", ["Reproduces exactly the same run", "Gives a different run each time", "Is not allowed by the simulator", "Gives the same stimulus but different results"], "Reproducibility from the seed is what makes random failures debuggable. Regressions log the seed of every failure."],
["28-13", "E", "In UVM, which of these is a uvm_object rather than a uvm_component?", ["A sequence item", "A driver", "A monitor", "An agent"], "Components form the permanent hierarchy and have phases. Sequence items and sequences are objects that are created and discarded during the test."],
["28-14", "M", "In a UVM driver, which call fetches the next transaction from the sequencer?", ["seq_item_port.get_next_item(req)", "ap.write(req)", "uvm_config_db::get(req)", "phase.raise_objection(req)"], "The driver pulls items with get_next_item, drives them, then calls item_done so the sequence's finish_item can return."],
["28-15", "M", "Why are UVM components created with type_id::create() instead of new()?", ["So a test can use the factory to override which class is actually created", "Because new() is not allowed in SystemVerilog classes", "So the component runs faster", "Because create() randomizes the component"], "Factory overrides let one environment serve many tests: swap in an error-injecting driver or a tighter-constrained item without editing the environment."],
["28-16", "M", "A monitor's analysis port write() delivers each transaction to:", ["Every connected subscriber, zero or more, without blocking", "Exactly one connected component, blocking until it is read", "The sequencer, which forwards it to the driver", "Only the test at the top of the hierarchy"], "Analysis ports are broadcasts: the scoreboard and the coverage collector can both subscribe to the same monitor."],
["28-17", "E", "Which UVM phase runs top-down?", ["build_phase", "connect_phase", "report_phase", "extract_phase"], "A parent must exist before it can build its children, so build is top-down. The other function phases run bottom-up."],
["28-18", "E", "Which UVM phase consumes simulation time?", ["run_phase", "build_phase", "connect_phase", "report_phase"], "run_phase is a task; all the others listed are functions that run in zero time."],
["28-19", "H", "A UVM test raises no objection in run_phase. What happens?", ["run_phase ends at time 0 and the test reports a pass having driven nothing", "The simulation hangs until the global timeout", "UVM raises an objection automatically for each sequence", "The test fails with a missing-objection error"], "UVM ends run_phase when the objection count is zero. Raise in the test (or sequence) before starting traffic, and drop after it is done."],
["28-20", "M", "What is uvm_config_db typically used for?", ["Passing the virtual interface and settings from the top down to components deep in the hierarchy", "Storing the expected results for the scoreboard", "Connecting analysis ports to their subscribers", "Recording coverage between runs"], "Values are set by path and name at a higher level and fetched in build_phase. A failed get usually means a path or name typo."],
["28-21", "M", "A passive agent contains:", ["Only a monitor", "Only a driver and sequencer", "A sequencer, driver and monitor", "Only a scoreboard"], "Passive agents watch an interface that another block drives, typically when a block environment is reused at subsystem level."],
["28-22", "M", "What is a virtual sequence for?", ["Coordinating sequences on several agents' sequencers, such as configure, then traffic, then status checks", "Running a sequence without a simulator", "Generating stimulus for a passive agent", "Replacing the driver when no interface exists"], "It runs on no interface of its own and starts sub-sequences on the sequencers it holds handles to."],
["28-23", "M", "A RAL back-door register write:", ["Sets the register's flip-flops directly by hierarchical path in zero time, bypassing the bus", "Goes over the real bus through the adapter", "Writes the register model's mirror only", "Is only allowed after reset"], "Back-door is fast for setup but cannot find bugs in the bus logic. Front-door goes through the adapter as real bus transactions."],
["28-24", "M", "What does a |=> b mean in SVA?", ["If a is true this cycle, b must be true on the next clock", "If a is true, b must be true in the same cycle", "a and b must never be true together", "b must be true before a"], "|=> is non-overlapping implication, the same as a |-> ##1 b. |-> checks b in the same cycle."],
["28-25", "M", "Which assertion says: once req is high, gnt must arrive within 1 to 3 cycles?", ["assert property (@(posedge clk) req |-> ##[1:3] gnt);", "assert property (@(posedge clk) gnt |-> ##[1:3] req);", "assert property (@(posedge clk) req ##3 gnt);", "assert (req && gnt);"], "The antecedent req starts the check, and ##[1:3] allows the consequent anywhere from one to three clocks later."],
["28-26", "H", "An assertion valid |-> ready has never failed in a regression. Why might it still have checked nothing?", ["If valid was never high, every attempt passed vacuously; a cover on valid shows whether it was exercised", "Assertions only check in gate-level simulation", "|-> assertions cannot fail", "The scoreboard disables assertions that pass"], "A false antecedent makes the implication pass vacuously. Pair important assertions with covers of their antecedents."],
["28-27", "E", "b[*3] in a sequence means:", ["b is true for three consecutive cycles", "b is true three times, not necessarily in a row", "b is checked every third cycle", "b changes value three times"], "[*n] is consecutive repetition. [->n] and [=n] are the non-consecutive versions."],
["28-28", "M", "What does $stable(data) check?", ["data has the same value as at the previous clock sample", "data has no X or Z bits", "data is a constant parameter", "data has stayed unchanged since reset"], "Sampled-value functions compare with the previous sample: $rose, $fell, $stable, and $past(data, n) for older values."],
["28-29", "E", "Which kind of coverage is measured automatically from the RTL?", ["Code coverage: lines, branches, conditions, toggles and FSM states", "Functional coverage from covergroups", "Assertion cover properties", "Test-plan coverage"], "Code coverage needs no extra code. Functional coverage is written by the verification engineer from the plan."],
["28-30", "M", "100% code coverage has been reached. What can still be wrong?", ["A feature missing from the RTL, or results that were never checked", "Nothing: every line has been tested", "Only gate-level timing problems", "Only problems in the testbench's constraints"], "Code coverage shows code ran. It cannot find code that should exist but does not, and it says nothing about whether outputs were compared."],
["28-31", "M", "A cross of a 2-bin coverpoint with a 4-bin coverpoint has how many bins?", ["8", "6", "16", "4"], "A cross covers every combination: 2 × 4 = 8. Impossible combinations are removed with ignore_bins."],
["28-32", "E", "What happens when an illegal_bins value is sampled?", ["An error is reported", "The value is ignored", "The coverage percentage rises", "The covergroup stops sampling"], "illegal_bins work as a cheap extra checker; ignore_bins simply exclude values."],
["28-33", "M", "Formal model checking returns a counterexample. What is it?", ["A short input sequence from reset that makes the assertion fail", "A proof that the assertion always holds", "A list of uncovered bins", "A waveform from the last random simulation"], "Counterexamples are often the fastest way to find corner-case bugs, because the tool finds the shortest failing trace."],
["28-34", "H", "Why can an overly strong assume property make a formal proof meaningless?", ["It can rule out the very inputs that trigger the bug, so the assertion passes", "Assumptions slow the proof until it times out", "Assumptions are ignored by formal tools", "Assumptions turn assertions into covers"], "Assumptions define the legal input space. Check with cover properties that interesting states are still reachable under them."],
["28-35", "E", "Logical equivalence checking (LEC) is used to:", ["Prove that the synthesized netlist behaves exactly like the RTL", "Check that two testbenches give the same coverage", "Find timing violations", "Compare two random seeds"], "LEC is run after synthesis and after every netlist change, such as an ECO."],
["28-36", "M", "Which bug class is gate-level simulation more likely than RTL simulation to find?", ["A missing reset that RTL's X-optimism hid", "A wrong expected value in the scoreboard", "A constraint that is too tight", "A typo in a coverage bin name"], "RTL treats if (X) as false, which can make an uninitialized register look fine. Gate-level simulation propagates X more realistically."],
["28-37", "M", "A test fails. What should you do first?", ["Rerun it with the same seed to reproduce it, and read the first error", "Change the constraints so the failure goes away", "File a design bug immediately", "Rerun with a new seed to see if it passes"], "Reproduce, read the first error (later ones are often consequences), triage testbench versus design, trace back, find the root cause, fix, and add the case to the regression."],
["28-38", "E", "A mailbox in SystemVerilog is:", ["A thread-safe FIFO for passing items between threads", "A covergroup for transactions", "A virtual interface handle", "A type of assertion"], "A generator can put transactions in and a driver get them out; get blocks until an item is available."],
["28-39", "M", "Why use a clocking block in a testbench interface?", ["It fixes when the testbench samples and drives relative to the clock edge, avoiding races with the design", "It generates the clock", "It makes the interface synthesizable", "It is required for coverage collection"], "Inputs are sampled just before the edge (#1step) and outputs driven just after, so testbench and design never race."],
["28-40", "H", "Which constraint makes every element of a rand int array arr unique?", ["constraint c { unique {arr}; }", "constraint c { arr.size() == 1; }", "constraint c { foreach (arr[i]) arr[i] != i; }", "constraint c { arr inside {[0:9]}; }"], "unique {arr} forbids any two elements being equal. The same can be written with a nested foreach and arr[i] != arr[j] for i != j."],
// 29 FPGA design
["29-01", "E", "A delay line of flip-flops with no reset is usually implemented on an AMD FPGA as:", ["Shift-register LUTs (SRLs), up to 32 stages per LUT", "Block RAM", "DSP slices", "A carry chain"], "SLICEM LUTs can act as 32-stage shift registers. Adding a reset prevents this and forces flip-flops."],
["29-02", "M", "Why does a memory written with an asynchronous read not map to block RAM?", ["Block RAM reads are synchronous: the address or data must be registered", "Block RAM has no write port", "Block RAM only stores constants", "Asynchronous reads are not synthesizable"], "With a combinational read, the tool builds distributed (LUT) RAM or flip-flops instead, which is far larger for big memories."],
["29-03", "M", "Which coding change most often stops a multiply from running at full speed in a DSP slice?", ["Leaving out the input and output registers around the multiply", "Using signed numbers", "Writing the multiply with the * operator", "Putting the multiply in a separate module"], "DSP slices have internal pipeline registers. Code that registers around the multiply lets the tool absorb them; without them, the multiply sits in a long combinational path."],
["29-04", "M", "Vendors recommend avoiding resets on FPGA datapath registers because:", ["Registers already start at their initial value after configuration, and resets add fanout and block SRL, BRAM and DSP inference", "Resets are not allowed on FPGAs", "Resets double the power of each flip-flop", "Resets make simulation impossible"], "Reset only control state that needs it, prefer synchronous resets, and synchronize the release of any external reset."],
["29-05", "E", "A design needs some logic to update at 1 kHz from a 100 MHz clock. The recommended approach is:", ["Keep the 100 MHz clock and use a one-cycle enable pulse every 100 000 cycles", "Divide the clock with a counter and clock the logic from the counter output", "AND the clock with a 1 kHz signal", "Use the 1 kHz signal as an asynchronous reset"], "Every flip-flop has a clock enable. Clocks built from logic travel on general routing with large skew and can glitch."],
["29-06", "M", "An MMCM has a 100 MHz input, multiplier M = 10, input divider D = 1 and output divider O = 8. What is the output frequency?", ["125 MHz", "80 MHz", "1 GHz", "12.5 MHz"], "f_out = f_in × M / (D × O) = 100 × 10 / 8 = 125 MHz. The internal VCO runs at 1 GHz."],
["29-07", "E", "What does create_clock -period 8 [get_ports clk] tell the timing tool?", ["A 125 MHz clock enters on the clk pin, so paths it clocks must meet 8 ns", "The clock pin is 8 ns from the logic", "Clock skew must stay below 8 ns", "The clock should be divided by 8"], "Every clock from a pin needs create_clock; without it, none of its paths are checked."],
["29-08", "H", "An external chip's data is valid 1.5 to 4.5 ns after the shared clock edge, and the board adds 0.5 ns. What set_input_delay values should be used?", ["-max 5.0 and -min 2.0", "-max 4.5 and -min 1.5", "-max 2.0 and -min 5.0", "-max 4.0 and -min 1.0"], "Arrival at the pin is the device's clock-to-out plus the trace: 1.5 + 0.5 = 2.0 ns minimum and 4.5 + 0.5 = 5.0 ns maximum."],
["29-09", "H", "A path is given set_multicycle_path 2 -setup. What else is normally needed?", ["set_multicycle_path 1 -hold on the same path, to move the hold check back", "set_false_path on the same path", "A second create_clock", "Nothing: the hold check is unaffected"], "Moving the setup check by one cycle also moves the hold check, which makes hold much harder to meet. The -hold 1 constraint restores it."],
["29-10", "M", "set_clock_groups -asynchronous between two clocks:", ["Stops the tool from timing paths between them, so every crossing must be synchronized by design", "Makes the tool time the crossings with extra margin", "Merges the two clocks into one", "Adds synchronizers automatically"], "The constraint only removes the checks. If a crossing is not actually safe, timing passes and the design fails at random."],
["29-11", "M", "In a timing report, the worst path's delay is 80% routing and 20% logic. What is the most likely cause?", ["High fanout, poor placement or congestion", "Too many LUT levels", "A missing clock constraint", "A hold violation"], "Mostly logic delay points at too many levels (pipeline it). Mostly routing points at placement, fanout or congestion."],
["29-12", "E", "WNS is -0.3 ns and TNS is -0.3 ns. What does that tell you?", ["Exactly one endpoint fails, by 0.3 ns", "Thousands of paths fail slightly", "Hold fails everywhere", "Timing is met"], "TNS sums the failing slacks. When it equals WNS, only one endpoint is failing."],
["29-13", "E", "Why is a hold violation more serious than a setup violation?", ["Hold does not depend on the clock period, so slowing the clock does not fix it", "Hold violations only occur at high temperature", "Setup violations are always fixed by the tool", "Hold violations affect only the I/O"], "A design with a hold violation can fail at any frequency. Setup problems can at least be avoided by lowering the clock."],
["29-14", "M", "Which change usually reduces logic levels on an FPGA critical path?", ["Replacing a long if/else-if priority chain with a case on mutually exclusive conditions", "Adding a reset to every register", "Using a gated clock", "Merging two pipeline stages into one"], "Priority chains build deep logic. Parallel selection, pipelining and one-hot encoding make paths shallower."],
["29-15", "M", "Why are flip-flops placed in the I/O blocks for external interfaces?", ["It gives the shortest, most predictable path between the pin and the register", "I/O flip-flops use no power", "It is the only way to use LVDS", "It doubles the data rate"], "IOB registers keep external timing separate from internal logic and make input and output delays repeatable."],
["29-16", "M", "Why do multi-gigabit links use 8b/10b or 64b/66b encoding?", ["To guarantee enough transitions for clock recovery and keep the line DC balanced", "To encrypt the data", "To double the bandwidth", "To remove the need for equalization"], "The receiver recovers the clock from transitions. 8b/10b costs 25%; 64b/66b about 3%."],
["29-17", "E", "On a Zynq-style SoC FPGA, software usually controls an accelerator in the fabric through:", ["AXI4-Lite memory-mapped registers", "AXI4-Stream", "JTAG", "The bitstream"], "AXI4-Lite carries control and status; bulk data moves over AXI4 or AXI4-Stream, often with a DMA."],
["29-18", "M", "In HLS, a pipelined loop reports II = 2 where II = 1 was expected. A likely cause is:", ["Two reads per iteration from an array stored in a single-port memory", "The loop has a fixed trip count", "The function has no return value", "The clock period is too long"], "Port conflicts and loop-carried dependencies raise the initiation interval. ARRAY_PARTITION or a dual-port memory can fix the first."],
["29-19", "E", "The HLS UNROLL directive:", ["Copies the loop body so several iterations run in parallel, using more hardware", "Starts a new iteration every cycle with one copy of the hardware", "Splits an array across several memories", "Turns a loop into a state machine"], "PIPELINE overlaps iterations in one copy of hardware; UNROLL duplicates the hardware."],
["29-20", "M", "A signal you want to watch in an ILA disappears after synthesis. What usually fixes it?", ["Marking it mark_debug (or keep) so synthesis does not optimize it away", "Adding a reset to it", "Increasing the ILA sample depth", "Lowering the clock frequency"], "Synthesis removes or renames logic. Debug attributes preserve the net so it can be probed."],
["29-21", "M", "A design passes simulation but fails on the board, differently each power-up. Which is the most likely cause?", ["An unsynchronized clock-domain crossing or an unconstrained path", "A typo in a comment", "Using a case statement", "Too much free BRAM"], "Random, power-up-dependent behavior is classic for metastability and timing that was never checked. Check CDC and the unconstrained-paths report."],
["29-22", "E", "Most SRAM-based FPGAs need their configuration reloaded:", ["At every power-up, from flash or a processor", "Only once, at the factory", "Every clock cycle", "Never, because SRAM keeps data without power"], "SRAM configuration cells lose their contents at power-off. Flash-based FPGAs keep theirs."],
["29-23", "M", "How many 6-input LUTs, at minimum, does a 4:1 multiplexer of single bits need?", ["1", "2", "3", "4"], "A 4:1 mux has 4 data inputs and 2 select inputs: 6 inputs, which fit one 6-input LUT."],
["29-24", "H", "Why should Gray-coded FIFO pointers crossing clock domains be constrained with set_max_delay -datapath_only rather than only a false path?", ["The bits must arrive within about one destination clock period of each other, or the Gray-code guarantee can break", "False paths are not allowed on FPGAs", "set_max_delay adds synchronizer flip-flops", "It forces the pointers into block RAM"], "Gray code lets only one bit change at a time, but if routing skews the bits by more than a clock period, the receiver can see two changes. A bounded delay keeps the bits together."],
["29-25", "E", "Partial reconfiguration lets you:", ["Reload one region of the FPGA while the rest keeps running", "Change the FPGA's process technology", "Store two bitstreams in one BRAM", "Run the FPGA without a clock"], "One device can switch between accelerators without stopping the rest of the system."],
// 30 Embedded and firmware
["30-01", "E", "Why must a pointer to a hardware status register be declared volatile?", ["So the compiler rereads it on every access instead of reusing a cached value", "So the register cannot be written", "So the access is atomic", "So the variable is stored in flash"], "Without volatile, a loop waiting for a status bit may read the register once and spin forever."],
["30-02", "M", "What does const volatile uint32_t *p describe?", ["A register the program must not write but whose value can change, such as a read-only status register", "A constant that is stored in RAM", "A contradiction that will not compile", "A register that can only be written once"], "const forbids writes by the program; volatile says the hardware may change it."],
["30-03", "E", "Which expression clears bit 3 of reg without changing the other bits?", ["reg &= ~(1u << 3);", "reg |= (1u << 3);", "reg ^= (1u << 3);", "reg = ~(1u << 3);"], "AND with a mask that has a 0 only at bit 3. OR sets, XOR toggles, and plain assignment changes every bit."],
["30-04", "M", "Which expression sets the 3-bit field at bits [6:4] of reg to v?", ["reg = (reg & ~(0x7u << 4)) | ((v & 0x7u) << 4);", "reg |= v << 4;", "reg = (reg & (0x7u << 4)) | v;", "reg &= v << 4;"], "Clear the field first, then OR in the new value shifted into place. ORing alone cannot turn 1s into 0s."],
["30-05", "H", "A status register has write-1-to-clear flag bits. Code runs STATUS |= FLAG_A; to clear flag A. What can go wrong?", ["Every other flag that is currently set is read as 1 and written back as 1, clearing it too", "Nothing: OR only touches flag A", "Flag A is set instead of cleared", "The write is ignored by the hardware"], "Write-1-to-clear registers should be cleared with a plain write of just the bit: STATUS = FLAG_A;."],
["30-06", "M", "Which is the best thing to do inside an interrupt service routine for a received UART byte?", ["Copy the byte into a ring buffer, clear the flag, and return", "Parse the whole message and print it with printf", "Wait in a loop for the next byte", "Take a mutex that the main code also uses"], "ISRs should be short and never block. Heavy work happens in the main loop or a task."],
["30-07", "E", "The vector table holds:", ["The addresses of the interrupt and exception handlers", "The values of every peripheral register", "The stack of the interrupted code", "The program's global variables"], "On an interrupt, the core looks up the handler address in the vector table and jumps to it."],
["30-08", "M", "A 64-bit tick counter is incremented by a timer ISR on a 32-bit MCU. Why can the main code read a wrong value?", ["Reading it takes two 32-bit loads, and the ISR can update it between them", "volatile variables cannot be 64 bits", "The ISR runs too slowly", "The counter overflows every second"], "A non-atomic read can mix halves from before and after an update. Disable interrupts briefly, or read the high half twice and retry if it changed."],
["30-09", "H", "A DMA engine fills a buffer in cacheable memory. Before the CPU reads the buffer it should:", ["Invalidate the buffer's cache lines so the CPU reads memory, not stale cached data", "Clean (flush) the buffer's cache lines", "Disable interrupts", "Do nothing: DMA keeps the cache coherent automatically on every MCU"], "DMA writes memory behind the cache. Invalidate before reading DMA data; clean before DMA reads data the CPU wrote, or use non-cacheable memory."],
["30-10", "E", "UART at 9600 baud, 8 data bits, no parity, 1 stop bit. How many bytes per second?", ["960", "1200", "9600", "1067"], "Each byte takes 10 bits on the wire (start, 8 data, stop): 9600 / 10 = 960 bytes per second."],
["30-11", "E", "Which bus uses two shared open-drain wires with pull-up resistors and 7-bit device addresses?", ["I2C", "SPI", "UART", "CAN"], "I2C has SCL and SDA. SPI uses a clock, two data lines and a chip select per device; UART is point to point."],
["30-12", "M", "In I2C, what does the receiving device do in the ninth clock of every byte?", ["Pulls SDA low to acknowledge (ACK), or leaves it high to NACK", "Sends its address", "Stretches the clock to 2× speed", "Sends a parity bit"], "Every byte is followed by an ACK bit. The controller NACKs the last byte of a read before the STOP."],
["30-13", "M", "What defines the four SPI modes?", ["Clock polarity (idle level) and clock phase (which edge samples data)", "The number of data bits and the parity", "The number of chip selects", "The speed: 1, 2, 4 or 8 MHz"], "CPOL and CPHA. Controller and peripheral must use the same mode or bits are sampled at the wrong edge."],
["30-14", "M", "Two CAN nodes start sending at the same moment, with identifiers 0x120 and 0x0F0. What happens?", ["0x0F0 wins arbitration because dominant 0 bits override recessive 1 bits, and its frame is not corrupted", "Both frames are corrupted and resent", "0x120 wins because it is larger", "The node that started first by nanoseconds wins"], "A node that sends a recessive 1 but reads back a dominant 0 stops and retries. The lowest identifier has the highest priority."],
["30-15", "M", "A timer clock is 72 MHz. With a prescaler of 71 (divide by 72), what reload value gives a 1 kHz update rate?", ["999", "1000", "71", "71 999"], "The timer counts at 1 MHz. It must count 1000 ticks per period: reload = 1000 - 1 = 999."],
["30-16", "E", "A PWM output with reload value 199 and compare value 50 has what duty cycle?", ["25%", "50%", "20%", "75%"], "The period is 200 counts and the output is high for 50: 50/200 = 25%."],
["30-17", "M", "What does the start-up code do with the .data section before main()?", ["Copies the initial values of initialized globals from flash to RAM", "Fills it with zeros", "Moves the code into RAM", "Nothing: .data already lives in RAM at power-up"], ".data's initial values are stored in flash and copied to RAM; .bss is zeroed; then constructors run and main is called."],
["30-18", "E", "In C, a global int counter; with no initializer is placed in:", [".bss, zeroed at start-up", ".data", ".rodata", "the stack"], "Uninitialized (zero-initialized) globals and statics go in .bss."],
["30-19", "M", "Why do many embedded coding standards forbid malloc after start-up?", ["The heap fragments over time and allocation time is unpredictable", "malloc is not available in C", "Heap memory is slower than stack memory", "malloc always returns flash memory"], "Fixed pools or static buffers give predictable memory use and timing."],
["30-20", "E", "A watchdog timer resets the chip when:", ["Software fails to reload it in time", "The supply voltage is too high", "An interrupt is missed", "The stack is empty"], "The main loop kicks the watchdog only when healthy, so a hang ends in a reset instead of a stuck device."],
["30-21", "H", "A low-priority task holds a mutex that a high-priority task needs, and a medium-priority task keeps preempting the low one. What is this, and what is the standard fix?", ["Priority inversion; priority inheritance raises the holder to the waiter's priority", "Deadlock; take locks in the same order", "Starvation; use round-robin scheduling", "A race condition; make the variables volatile"], "The high-priority task is effectively blocked by the medium one. Priority inheritance lets the low-priority holder finish quickly and release the mutex."],
["30-22", "M", "Task A holds mutex 1 and waits for mutex 2; task B holds mutex 2 and waits for mutex 1. How is this usually prevented?", ["Always take the mutexes in the same global order", "Give both tasks the same priority", "Make the mutexes volatile", "Use a bigger stack"], "Deadlock needs a cycle of waits. A fixed lock order removes the cycle; timeouts let a task back off."],
["30-23", "M", "A binary semaphore is typically used to:", ["Let an ISR signal a task that an event happened", "Protect a resource so only the holder can release it, with priority inheritance", "Store a queue of messages", "Count clock cycles"], "Mutexes have ownership (and often priority inheritance) for protecting resources; semaphores are for signaling and counting."],
["30-24", "M", "A sensor sleeps at 10 µA and wakes for 20 ms every second at 5 mA. What is the average current?", ["About 0.11 mA", "About 5 mA", "About 0.01 mA", "About 1 mA"], "5 mA × 0.02 + 0.01 mA × 0.98 ≈ 0.100 + 0.0098 ≈ 0.11 mA. The active time dominates."],
["30-25", "E", "What does 'hard real-time' mean?", ["A missed deadline counts as a failure, so worst-case timing must be guaranteed", "The code runs as fast as possible", "The system uses a hardware timer", "The OS has no scheduler"], "Real-time is about predictability. Soft real-time systems tolerate an occasional late result."],
["30-26", "M", "An ADC samples a signal containing frequencies up to 20 kHz. What is the minimum sampling rate that avoids aliasing?", ["More than 40 kHz", "20 kHz", "10 kHz", "More than 80 kHz"], "Sampling must exceed twice the highest frequency (the Nyquist rate). In practice an anti-aliasing filter and some margin are used, such as 44.1 or 48 kHz for audio."],
// 31 Implementation, low power and DFT
["31-01", "E", "What does a Liberty (.lib) file describe?", ["Each standard cell's function, area, power and delay tables at one PVT corner", "The placed and routed layout", "The timing constraints of the design", "The power domains of the chip"], "Synthesis and STA read the library to know what each cell does and how fast it is."],
["31-02", "M", "Why is logical equivalence checking run after synthesis?", ["To prove the gate-level netlist performs exactly the same function as the RTL", "To check setup and hold timing", "To measure functional coverage", "To verify the layout matches the netlist"], "LEC compares functions without stimulus. LVS is the layout-versus-netlist check."],
["31-03", "M", "In on-chip variation (OCV) analysis for a setup check, the capture clock path is treated as:", ["Early (fast), while the launch clock and data paths are late (slow)", "Late (slow), the same as the data path", "Ideal, with no variation", "Ignored"], "Making the capture clock arrive early and the data arrive late gives the worst case for setup. Hold uses the opposite."],
["31-04", "H", "What does CRPR remove?", ["The pessimism of applying both early and late derates to the clock path shared by launch and capture", "Crosstalk delay on clock nets", "Hold violations on scan paths", "Skew between unrelated clocks"], "One buffer cannot be both slow and fast at once. Clock reconvergence pessimism removal adds that impossible difference back to the slack."],
["31-05", "M", "An aggressor net switches in the opposite direction to the victim at the same time. What happens to the victim's delay?", ["It increases, which matters for setup", "It decreases, which matters for hold", "It is unchanged", "The victim cannot switch at all"], "Opposite-direction switching effectively doubles the coupling capacitance (slow-down). Same-direction switching speeds the victim up, which matters for hold."],
["31-06", "M", "Setup is usually worst at which corner?", ["Slow process, low voltage (and, at advanced nodes, possibly cold because of temperature inversion)", "Fast process, high voltage, cold", "Typical process, nominal voltage", "Fast process, low voltage"], "Slow transistors and low supply give the longest delays. Hold is checked at fast process, high voltage."],
["31-07", "E", "Which is a valid fix for a hold violation?", ["Insert delay cells on the data path", "Upsize the cells on the data path", "Swap to low-Vt cells", "Lower the clock frequency"], "Hold needs the data to arrive later. Upsizing and low-Vt cells speed the path up, the wrong direction; the clock period does not affect hold."],
["31-08", "M", "Useful skew fixes a setup violation by:", ["Delaying the capture register's clock slightly, giving the data more time", "Removing the clock tree", "Increasing clock uncertainty", "Making the launch clock arrive later"], "The extra time must come from the next stage, which must have setup slack to spare, and hold on the capture path gets harder."],
["31-09", "E", "Utilization in floorplanning is:", ["Standard-cell area divided by the placeable core area", "The fraction of time the chip is busy", "The percentage of routing tracks used", "Power divided by area"], "Typical targets of 60-75% leave room for buffers, CTS and routing."],
["31-10", "M", "Why do macros (memories) get halos around them?", ["To keep standard cells away from their edges, leaving room for routing and avoiding congestion at their pins", "To shield them from radiation", "To isolate their power supplies", "To make them easier to test"], "Halos (keep-out margins) and channels between macros keep the area around their pins routable."],
["31-11", "M", "What is the main difference between static and dynamic IR drop?", ["Static uses average current; dynamic captures sudden local current when many cells switch together", "Static happens only at power-up", "Dynamic IR drop only affects the I/O ring", "They are the same analysis at different temperatures"], "Dynamic drop is fixed with decap cells near busy logic and a denser grid."],
["31-12", "E", "Which is a primary goal of clock tree synthesis?", ["Low skew between registers that communicate", "Maximum wire length", "As many clock domains as possible", "Removing all buffers from the clock path"], "CTS also targets low insertion delay, clean transitions and low power."],
["31-13", "M", "Why are clock nets routed with non-default rules (wider wires, double spacing)?", ["To reduce resistance, crosstalk and variation on the most sensitive nets", "To save area", "Because clock nets carry DC current", "So they can be removed after test"], "Clock timing affects every path. Wider wires cut resistance; extra spacing or shielding cuts coupling."],
["31-14", "E", "What does SPEF contain?", ["The extracted resistance and capacitance of every net", "The cell timing tables", "The power intent", "The scan chain order"], "Sign-off STA reads the parasitics from SPEF."],
["31-15", "M", "Why does an integrated clock gating (ICG) cell contain a latch?", ["The latch holds the enable steady while the clock is high, so the gated clock cannot glitch", "To store the register values while the clock is off", "To divide the clock by two", "To shift the clock phase by 90 degrees"], "The latch is transparent while the clock is low, so enable changes cannot chop the high phase of the gated clock."],
["31-16", "M", "When a power domain is switched off, isolation cells:", ["Clamp its outputs to known values so floating signals do not disturb powered-on logic", "Keep its registers' values", "Convert its signals to a different voltage", "Turn its supply off"], "Retention registers save state; level shifters convert voltages; power switches cut the supply."],
["31-17", "E", "Signals crossing between two voltage domains need:", ["Level shifters", "Isolation cells only", "Clock gating cells", "Scan flip-flops"], "A signal from a low-voltage domain may not fully turn off the receiving gate's transistors in a higher-voltage domain."],
["31-18", "M", "What does a UPF file describe?", ["Power domains, power switches, isolation, retention and level shifters", "Timing constraints", "Cell layout geometry", "Test patterns"], "Keeping power intent separate means simulation, synthesis, layout and sign-off all read the same description."],
["31-19", "E", "Lowering both voltage and frequency by 20% reduces dynamic power to about:", ["51% of the original", "80%", "64%", "40%"], "P ∝ CV²f: 0.8² × 0.8 = 0.512."],
["31-20", "E", "In a scan design, what happens in shift mode?", ["The flip-flops form shift registers so patterns can be loaded and results unloaded", "The chip runs its normal function at full speed", "The clock is stopped", "The memories are tested"], "The scan-enable selects the scan input of each flip-flop's mux, chaining the flip-flops together."],
["31-21", "M", "Which fault model needs at-speed testing?", ["Transition (slow-to-rise or slow-to-fall) faults", "Stuck-at faults", "IDDQ faults", "Boundary-scan faults"], "A transition fault only shows when the node must switch within one functional clock period."],
["31-22", "H", "A design has 2 million scan flip-flops in 200 chains. About how many shift cycles does loading one pattern take?", ["10 000", "2 000 000", "200", "400"], "Chain length = 2 000 000 / 200 = 10 000 flip-flops. Scan compression uses many more, shorter internal chains to cut this."],
["31-23", "M", "In launch-on-capture (LOC) at-speed testing:", ["The pattern is shifted slowly, then two fast functional clock pulses launch and capture the transition", "The last shift pulse launches the transition", "The tester supplies a GHz clock", "No clock is used at all"], "LOC (broadside) needs no fast scan-enable. Launch-on-shift uses the last shift as the launch."],
["31-24", "E", "Memory BIST mainly uses:", ["March algorithms that write and read patterns in ascending and descending address order", "Random patterns from an LFSR", "Boundary scan", "Functional software tests"], "March tests catch stuck cells, coupling between cells and address decoder faults."],
["31-25", "M", "How does memory repair raise yield?", ["Failing rows or columns found by BIST are remapped to spares, with the mapping stored in fuses", "Defective memories are switched off", "ECC corrects every manufacturing defect", "The memory is retested at a lower voltage"], "Memories are large and defect-prone, so a few spare rows and columns save many dies."],
["31-26", "M", "Why can scan shift cause failures on the tester even when the design is correct?", ["So many flip-flops toggle at once that power and IR drop exceed what the chip sees in normal use", "Scan chains are not timed", "The tester's clock is too slow", "Scan disables the clock tree"], "Power-aware patterns and slower shift clocks keep test power within limits."],
["31-27", "E", "Which file is sent to the foundry at tape-out?", ["GDSII or OASIS", "SDC", "Liberty", "SPEF"], "The layout shapes go to the foundry to make the masks."],
// 32 Bring-up and validation
["32-01", "E", "When powering a new board for the first time, a good first step is:", ["Ramp each supply slowly with a current limit and watch for a short", "Apply full power to all rails at once", "Load the full firmware before powering up", "Run the high-speed links first"], "A current-limited ramp protects the board and reveals shorts before anything is damaged."],
["32-02", "M", "A test fails only at low supply voltage and passes at nominal voltage. What does that most suggest?", ["A speed (setup-like) path or IR drop, because gates are slower at low voltage", "A hold violation", "A software bug unrelated to the hardware", "A failing oscilloscope probe"], "Lower voltage slows logic. Check whether the failing frequency rises with voltage on a shmoo."],
["32-03", "M", "On a voltage-versus-frequency shmoo, the pass/fail boundary does not move when the voltage changes. That points to:", ["Something other than a normal speed path, such as a functional bug, a hold-type problem or a fixed-delay circuit", "A normal critical path", "Perfect margin", "Too much supply noise"], "A normal speed path passes at higher frequency when the voltage is raised, giving a sloped boundary."],
["32-04", "E", "A scan dump in post-silicon debug:", ["Stops the clocks and shifts out every flip-flop's state through the scan chains", "Records an oscilloscope trace", "Runs the memory BIST", "Measures supply current"], "It gives a full snapshot of the chip's state to compare with simulation."],
["32-05", "M", "Why is changing several settings at once a bad debug practice?", ["You cannot tell which change mattered, and the evidence is lost", "The chip may overheat", "Lab instruments only accept one command at a time", "It violates the test standard"], "Change one variable per experiment and log every setting."],
["32-06", "M", "A parameter has mean 50, standard deviation 2 and an upper spec limit of 58 (no lower limit). What is Cpk?", ["1.33", "4.0", "0.75", "2.67"], "Cpk = (58 - 50) / (3 × 2) = 8/6 ≈ 1.33."],
["32-07", "E", "Why are production test limits set tighter than the specification (guard bands)?", ["To allow for tester accuracy and drift, so no part outside the specification ships", "To make more parts pass", "Because the specification is only a guess", "To shorten test time"], "Tighter guard bands lose some good parts; looser ones risk escapes."],
["32-08", "M", "Using the rule rise time ≈ 0.35 / bandwidth, what is the fastest rise time a 500 MHz oscilloscope can show?", ["About 700 ps", "About 70 ps", "About 2 ns", "About 350 ps"], "0.35 / 500 MHz = 0.7 ns. To see a faster edge accurately, use a scope several times faster than the edge."],
["32-09", "M", "A long ground lead on an oscilloscope probe tends to:", ["Add inductance that makes fast edges ring, showing overshoot that is not really there", "Reduce the probe's bandwidth to DC only", "Short the signal to ground", "Make the scope trigger earlier"], "Use the shortest ground spring possible when measuring fast signals."],
["32-10", "E", "An eye diagram's opening shrinks horizontally mainly because of:", ["Jitter", "DC offset", "Too much bandwidth", "A long cable at low speed"], "Jitter closes the eye in time; loss and noise close it in voltage."],
["32-11", "H", "About how many error-free bits must be sent to show a BER of 1e-12 with 95% confidence?", ["3 × 10^12", "1 × 10^12", "1 × 10^6", "3 × 10^9"], "With zero errors, about 3 / BER bits are needed: 3 × 10^12. At 10 Gb/s that takes about 5 minutes."],
["32-12", "E", "Random jitter is:", ["Unbounded and Gaussian, quoted as an RMS value", "Bounded and caused by data patterns", "Caused only by crosstalk", "Zero in a well-designed link"], "Deterministic jitter is bounded (data-dependent, crosstalk, duty-cycle). Total jitter is quoted at a target BER."],
["32-13", "E", "SCPI commands such as MEAS:VOLT:DC? are used to:", ["Control and read lab instruments from a script", "Program the FPGA bitstream", "Configure JTAG", "Write UVM tests"], "Python libraries like PyVISA send SCPI text over USB, Ethernet or GPIB."],
["32-14", "M", "What is burn-in for?", ["Running parts hot at high voltage before shipping, so weak parts fail in the factory instead of the field", "Measuring Fmax", "Programming fuses", "Removing static charge from the parts"], "It screens early-life failures, the left side of the bathtub curve."],
["32-15", "M", "A silicon bug is fixed by changing only the metal layers. This is usually done because:", ["A metal-only stepping is cheaper and faster than changing every mask", "Metal layers are the only ones that can have bugs", "Transistor layers cannot be changed after the first tape-out", "Metal changes need no verification"], "Spare cells left in the layout let small logic fixes be made by rewiring them in metal."],
["32-16", "E", "Vmin of a chip is:", ["The lowest supply voltage at which it still works at a given frequency", "The voltage at which it is fused", "The minimum voltage any pin can read as 1", "The voltage at power-off"], "Vmin and Fmax vary from part to part and are measured across many parts to set bins and operating points."],
["32-17", "M", "A failure appears on one board but not on others with parts from the same lot. A good next step is:", ["Swap the chip onto a good board and see whether the failure follows the chip or stays with the board", "Change the firmware and retest every board", "Assume a design bug and file it", "Raise the supply voltage on all boards"], "Swapping isolates part from board, one variable at a time."],
["32-18", "E", "HTOL (high-temperature operating life) testing is used to:", ["Accelerate aging so a part's lifetime reliability can be estimated", "Measure leakage at room temperature", "Find logic bugs", "Calibrate the oscilloscope"], "Many hours at high temperature and voltage stand in for years of normal use."],
];

